window.BH_SEARCH = [{"href":"docs/adr-001-rate-limit-in-each-service.html","title":"ADR-001: Rate limit inside each service","kind":"decision","example":true,"sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Architecture decision record ADR-001: Rate limit inside each service Each service enforces its own per-client token bucket in process, so we can ship limits before launch without new infrastructure. We will embed a per-client token bucket in every service, keyed by API key, because launch is three weeks away and this needs no shared infrastructure."},{"id":"context","heading":"Context","text":"The public API launches on 2 December. Two design partners have scripted clients that retry aggressively, and nothing today stops one of them saturating a service. We have no gateway able to run custom logic, and no shared low-latency store in production."},{"id":"options","heading":"Options considered","text":"In-process limiter per service Ships with a library and a config file No new runtime dependency Limits multiply with replicas and services Central limiter service One accurate limit per client New service, new on-call, before launch"},{"id":"consequences","heading":"Consequences","text":"What gets easier Each team tunes limits for its own endpoints What gets harder A client's real limit is per-service limit × replicas × services , so it grows as we scale Limits drift between services"}]},{"href":"docs/adr-002-rate-limit-at-the-edge.html","title":"ADR-002: Rate limit at the edge gateway","kind":"decision","example":true,"sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Architecture decision record ADR-002: Rate limit at the edge gateway Enforce one per-client token bucket in the gateway, with state in Redis, so limits hold however many services and replicas run behind it. We will enforce client rate limits once, in the gateway, using a token bucket updated atomically in Redis, because per-service limits no longer bound what a client can send. Services keep only a coarse safety limit against runaway internal callers."},{"id":"context","heading":"Context","text":"Under the per-service limits of ADR-001, one client sent 9× its contracted rate during the September incident without hitting a single limit: their traffic was spread across 3 services with 3 replicas each. We now have a gateway that runs plugins and a managed Redis cluster with p99 under 2 ms in-region. The token bucket explainer covers the algorithm, and the design document covers the request path, the script and the canary results."},{"id":"options","heading":"Options considered","text":"Keep per-service limits No migration The limit still multiplies as we scale Gateway + Redis token bucket One accurate limit per client Rejections never reach services Redis on the request path: +1–2 ms, needs a fail-open policy CDN provider limiting Absorbs floods before our network Can't see API keys or weight endpoints"},{"id":"consequences","heading":"Consequences","text":"What gets easier Limits are contractual numbers that hold One place to tune, observe and explain a 429 What gets harder Redis becomes part of every request; if it's down we fail open and lean on service safety limits"}]},{"href":"docs/announcing-patchcov.html","title":"Announcing patchcov","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Announcement · Testing Announcing patchcov patchcov answers one question precisely: which lines did this change add that no test ran? It is a command-line tool that reads the coverage report you already produce, and a GitHub Action that turns its answer into a pull-request comment and a gate. Neither needs an account, a token or a server. Command line & Rust library rust-works/patchcov Install it, run patchcov diff , read the docs GitHub Action action-works/patchcov-action Comment and gate on every pull request patchcov scores the lines a change added , lists the untested ones as file:line , and exits non-zero when a gate fails. patchcov-action runs it on every pull request, with a baseline from the merge base. That output is meant for automation as much as for people. An AI agent can loop on it, \"add tests until the gate passes\", without reading a dashboard. The project-wide percentage is the wrong signal for a pull request. It says how well the whole project is tested, not what this change left untested."},{"id":"motivation","heading":"Why another coverage tool","text":"More and more code is written by agents in a loop: write, run the checks, read the result, fix, repeat. That loop is only as good as the signals it can read. Coverage tooling was built to give people a picture, and an agent needs an instruction. Predict: a project has 4,000 executable lines at 92.00%. A pull request adds 6 executable lines and tests 4 of them. How far does the project total move? And how far if none of the six were tested? From 92.00% to 91.96%, a move of −0.04 percentage points. (3,680 + 4) ÷ (4,000 + 6) = 91.96%. That is too small for patchcov's headline to flag: it treats a move under 0.05 pp as neutral ⚪. The share of the new lines that are tested, which patchcov calls patch coverage, is 66.67% , with the two untested lines named. With none tested, to 91.86%: −0.14 pp. 3,680 ÷ 4,006 = 91.86%. A pull request with no tests at all moves the total by about a seventh of a point, while its patch coverage is 0%. Try both in the toy below . Three things kept getting in the way: The total hides the change. A pull request's lines are averaged in with every line the project already has. The new code's gap is real, but the total is too coarse to show it. Dashboards need interpreting. Trend graphs and coloured file trees are made for a person to look at. A script or an agent needs a number, a list of lines, and an exit code. A hosted service is a dependency of your merge button. If the coverage gate lives on someone else's server, an outage, an expired upload token or a rate limit blocks merges. Nothing can be checked before you push, either. patchcov is the smallest tool that fixes all three. It reads report files from disk, joins them to git diff , and prints a result that needs no human to interpret it. The mission, from the README: help projects that use agentic workflows keep their new code properly tested, with minimal friction."},{"id":"patch-coverage","heading":"Patch coverage, hands on","text":"Patch coverage is the share of the added executable lines that some test ran. Click a line's mark to decide whether a test covers it, and watch both numbers. 40 pub fn parse_header(line: &str) -> Result<Header, Error> { 41 let line = line.trim(); 42 + // A shebang is not a header. 43 + ✓ if line.starts_with(\"#!\") { 44 + ✓ return Ok(Header::Shebang); 45 + } 46 + ✓ let Some((key, value)) = line.split_once(':') else { 47 + ✗ return Err(Error::MissingColon); 48 + }; 49 + ✓ if key.is_empty() { 50 + ✗ return Err(Error::EmptyKey); 51 + } 52 Ok(Header::Field(key.into(), value.trim().into())) 53 } Lines 42, 45, 48 and 51 are added but not executable: a comment and closing braces never appear in a coverage report, so they are not counted. 66.67% Patch coverage (4/6 new lines) 91.96% Project total ⚪ −0.04 pp, neutral --fail-under-patch 80 Patch: 66.67% (4/6 new lines covered) Uncovered new lines: src/parser.rs:47, src/parser.rs:50 Error: patch coverage 66.67% is below the --fail-under-patch threshold of 80.00% exit 1 Loop until the gate passes Add one test Reset The total barely moves however many lines you leave untested; patch coverage swings by 16.67 points per line and names each gap. “Loop” plays the agent’s part: it writes a test for the first line patchcov lists, runs the gate again, and stops when it exits 0."},{"id":"how-it-works","heading":"How patchcov works","text":"patchcov is a join between two things you already have: a per-line coverage report, and the lines git says the change added. Notice that nothing leaves the machine: every input is a file or a git object, and every output is stdout or an exit code. Read the report you already have. lcov, llvm-cov JSON, Cobertura, JaCoCo XML and Go coverprofiles are detected from the content, so one command serves Rust, Go, Java, Kotlin, TypeScript, Python, C++ and more. Paths are made repo-relative by a fixed, configurable pipeline, with no guessing: a wrong guess would attribute coverage to the wrong file without a sign. Ask git what the change added. By default that is everything from the merge base with the default branch to HEAD , so lines that reached main while your branch was open are not counted against you. Score each added line. It is covered, uncovered, or not executable. Lines the report doesn't mention, such as comments and braces, are left out of the denominator. Optionally, compare with a baseline. A report from the merge base adds per-file deltas and indirect changes: lines whose coverage flipped without their text changing. These are scoped to the files the diff touched, because two separate test runs always differ a little elsewhere. Print, then exit. The markdown is a ready-made pull-request comment; JSON and YAML follow a documented schema. The exit code is 0 , or 1 when --fail-under-patch or --fail-under-lines fails, and 2 – 8 for a usage, report, marker, config, git or path problem. So a script can tell a failed gate from a broken run."},{"id":"agent-loop","heading":"Built for the agent loop","text":"Because the answer is a list of file:line and an exit code, an agent can act on it without a person in between. Agent Test run patchcov tests + coverage head.lcov diff --fail-under-patch 80 exit 1 · parser.rs:47, :50 write 2 tests tests + coverage diff --fail-under-patch 80 exit 0 The agent never reads a chart. Each round trip is a command, a list of lines, and a yes or no. The agent runs the test suite under any coverage tool that writes a supported report, such as cargo llvm-cov , go test -coverprofile , coverage.py or JaCoCo. patchcov exits 1 and names the gaps. With -o json , they arrive as uncovered_new_lines , and --error-format json puts the cause on stderr as an object too. The agent writes tests for exactly those lines and runs both steps again. The same command works on a laptop, in a sandbox and in CI, so the agent can pass the gate before it pushes."},{"id":"trustworthy","heading":"A signal you can trust","text":"A gate that cries wolf gets ignored, by people and agents alike. patchcov is quiet where coverage is noisy and loud where it is broken. 1 Quiet by construction Deltas are scoped to the files the diff touched. The headline ignores moves under 0.05 pp. Code that flaps between runners, such as a path gated on a CPU feature, can be wrapped in a tolerate marker, so a flap isn't reported as a regression. 2 Fails loudly An empty report, a failed shard, report paths that match no tracked file, or a malformed marker is an error with its own exit code, not a quietly lower number. 3 Silencing is visible Every marker needs a reason= , and every exclusion is listed in the comment. Settings live in .patchcov/config.yaml , in version control, so a reviewer can see what the gate does. // patchcov: coverage tolerate reason=\"AVX2 path; runners differ in CPU\" fn popcount_avx2(words: &[u64]) -> u64 { /* … */ } // patchcov: coverage end Go deeper: why tolerated lines still count in the patch A tolerate region masks deltas : each tolerated line is scored with its status in the baseline, so a flap reads as no change. The displayed percentages stay the real, measured values. An added line has no baseline counterpart, so it keeps its real status and stays in the patch-coverage denominator: new code should still be tested, even if it flaps later. To remove lines from every number, use ignore instead."},{"id":"action","heading":"patchcov-action: the same answer on every pull request","text":"The command answers the question; the action wires it into GitHub. It installs a cached patchcov binary, finds the right baseline, posts one sticky comment, and applies the gates last. Notice that the baseline is pinned to the pull request’s fork point, not to the tip of main , so the deltas belong to this pull request alone. Every push to main publishes a baseline: that commit's per-line report, as an artifact named coverage-baseline . On a pull request, produce the head report. In fat mode (the default) the action runs cargo-llvm-cov for a Rust workspace. In thin mode you bring a report from any language, and the action only diffs, comments and gates. Fetch the baseline for the merge base. If that commit has none, it tries up to 10 first-parent ancestors, nearest first, and says so in the comment. As a last resort in fat mode, it rebuilds coverage at the merge base in a git worktree. A miss is a warning: patch coverage never needs a baseline. Post one comment and keep it current. It has the patch coverage, the per-file deltas and the uncovered file:line list, and is updated in place on every push. Gate last. fail-under-patch and fail-under-lines run after the comment posts, so a failing pull request still gets its explanation. The action also handles the topologies real projects grow into: sharded test runs merged into one result, merge queues, and files a CI runner can't execute. jobs: coverage: runs-on: ubuntu-latest permissions: contents: read actions: read # download the merge base's baseline pull-requests: write # post the coverage comment steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v7 with: fetch-depth: 0 # full history, so the merge base resolves - uses: action-works/patchcov-action@v2 with: fail-under-patch: 80 - run: pytest --cov --cov-report=lcov:coverage.lcov - uses: action-works/patchcov-action@v2 with: run-coverage: false report: coverage.lcov fail-under-patch: 80 coverage: needs: shard # a failed shard fails this job steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v7 with: fetch-depth: 0 - uses: actions/download-artifact@v8 with: pattern: coverage-shard-* merge-multiple: true path: shards - uses: action-works/patchcov-action@v2 with: run-coverage: false shard-reports: shards/shard-*.lcov fail-under-patch: 80 cargo install patchcov --locked # or: cargo binstall patchcov cargo llvm-cov --lcov --output-path head.lcov patchcov diff --report head.lcov --fail-under-patch 80"},{"id":"when-to-choose","heading":"When to choose patchcov","text":"patchcov is a signal, not a dashboard. Pick it for the question it answers, and keep the other tools for the questions they answer. patchcov A command and an action that score the change, in your own CI. Answers \"what did this change leave untested, and where?\" Machine-readable output and distinct exit codes, ready for agents No service, account or upload token: the gate works whenever your CI does Runs the same locally, in a sandbox and in CI Built for noisy coverage and sharded runs No history, trend graphs or cross-repository views Line coverage; branch scoring is opt-in and limited to lcov and Cobertura A hosted coverage service You upload reports; the service keeps them and comments on pull requests. Coverage history and trends over months Dashboards and views across many repositories A third party between your tests and your merge button Reports leave your CI; tokens to manage The result is learned after pushing, not before A code-quality platform Coverage as one input to a wider quality gate. Static analysis, security scanning and coverage in one place Another server to run or to depend on Much more to set up than a coverage gate needs Choose by the question you need answered. If you need… Choose To know whether a pull request added untested code, and where patchcov A gate an agent or script can act on, in a loop patchcov A coverage gate that can't be blocked by someone else's outage patchcov One combined result from a sharded test run patchcov Coverage history, trend graphs, or views across repositories A hosted service, alongside patchcov Per-branch percentages, or JaCoCo and llvm-cov branch data Your coverage tool's own report Static analysis and security scanning as well A code-quality platform You don't have to pick one patchcov-action can upload to Codecov as well ( codecov: true ), so the history keeps flowing to a dashboard while the gate stays in your own CI."},{"id":"limits","heading":"What it doesn't tell you","text":"Covered is not tested patchcov measures that a line ran , not that a test asserted anything about it. Treat it as a regression signal, and pair it with review, or with mutation testing, for confidence in the tests themselves. Measure the report at the revision you diff. A report from older code has line numbers that no longer match the diff, and the result is silently wrong. Check out full history in CI ( fetch-depth: 0 ), or the merge base won't resolve. The library API is not stable at 0.x. The command line is the better-supported interface."},{"id":"get-started","heading":"Get started","text":"Install. cargo install patchcov --locked , cargo binstall patchcov , or a prebuilt binary for Linux, macOS or Windows from the GitHub releases. Run it on your branch. Produce a per-line report from your test run, then patchcov diff --report head.lcov . Add the gate. Add action-works/patchcov-action@v2 to your pull-request workflow with fail-under-patch , and let the comment and the exit code do the rest. Command line & Rust library rust-works/patchcov Install it, run patchcov diff , read the docs GitHub Action action-works/patchcov-action Comment and gate on every pull request"},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"rust-works/patchcov : the command, its usage guide, the reference for every flag and the JSON schema. Explanation : why patchcov's total differs from llvm-cov's summary, and how scoping and tolerate masking work. action-works/patchcov-action : fat and thin modes, sharded runs, merge queues and every input. docs.rs/patchcov : the analysis as a Rust library."}]},{"href":"docs/announcing-succinctly-0-8.html","title":"succinctly 0.8: a jq you can trust, three times faster","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Announcement · jq succinctly 0.8: a jq you can trust, three times faster Plenty of tools are faster than jq. The hard part is being faster while giving the same answers , down to jq's own quirks, and proving both in public. succinctly 0.8.0 is a drop-in replacement for jq 1.7.1: about three times faster across 223 measured workloads on three chips, with every place it answers differently written down. Release v0.8.0 Archives for macOS arm64, Linux x64 and arm64, Windows x64 Rust crate succinctly 0.8.0 cargo install succinctly --features cli Evidence docs/compare/jq.md The committed comparison reports, one per chip Swap sjq in where you run jq . It is jq 1.7.1, bug for bug, and runs 2.84x to 3.18x faster as a geomean over 223 workloads, measured on an AMD Ryzen 9 7950X, an AWS Graviton4 and an Apple M4 Pro. On large documents it also needs less than half of jq's memory, so the JSON that made jq slow or fall over in a pipeline is where it helps most. The common mistake is to judge a jq replacement by its speed. Speed is easy to claim; what you need is the same output and the same exit codes as the jq your scripts were written against, and a list of every place they differ. 2.84–3.18x Geomean speedup over jq 1.7.1, 223 rows, three chips 446 / 447 jq 1.7.1's own jq.test cases that agree 0.41–0.48 Peak memory as a share of jq's, 100 MB inputs 65 / 0 Known differences listed / found unlisted If you only want to try it, the next section takes 30 seconds. The four after it back one claim each: Fast , Faithful , Lean and Honest . Then comes the part a release post usually skips: where jq is still the better tool ."},{"id":"try-it","heading":"Try it in 30 seconds","text":"Install the binary, create the short aliases, and run sjq wherever you would run jq . Install. cargo install succinctly --features cli , or unpack one of the release archives . The macOS archive needs macOS 12 or newer, the x86-64 archives a CPU with POPCNT (2008 or newer), and the Linux archives the glibc of Ubuntu 24.04 or newer. cargo install builds for your own machine and has none of these floors. Add the aliases. succinctly install-aliases puts sjq , syq , sjq-locate and syq-locate next to the binary. Compare. Run a filter you already use through both tools and diff the output. $ succinctly --version succinctly 0.8.0 $ cat users.json {\"users\":[{\"name\":\"Ada\",\"langs\":[\"ml\",\"rust\"]},{\"name\":\"Grace\",\"langs\":[\"cobol\"]}]} $ P='[.users[] | {name, langs: (.langs | length)}]' $ jq -c \"$P\" users.json [{\"name\":\"Ada\",\"langs\":2},{\"name\":\"Grace\",\"langs\":1}] $ sjq -c \"$P\" users.json [{\"name\":\"Ada\",\"langs\":2},{\"name\":\"Grace\",\"langs\":1}] $ diff <(jq \"$P\" users.json) <(sjq \"$P\" users.json) && echo identical identical The binary also answers to the name jq . A symlink called jq earlier on your PATH ( ln -s \"$(command -v succinctly)\" ~/bin/jq ) runs your existing scripts on it unchanged, and deleting the link puts them back on jq."},{"id":"why","heading":"Why a faster jq matters","text":"jq sits in the middle of shell scripts, CI jobs and log pipelines, and nobody notices it until a document gets large. Then it is the slow step, or the step that runs out of memory. Here is one ordinary command, jq . , pretty-printing a 1 GB file of user records on an AMD Ryzen 9 7950X: . over a 1 GB array of user records, median of 3 interleaved runs, AMD Ryzen 9 7950X. Tool Time Peak memory jq 1.7.1 25.9 s 9.7 GiB succinctly jq 3.1 s 1.27 GiB jq builds the whole document as a tree of values before it runs the filter, and that tree takes almost ten bytes of memory for every byte of input. A faster jq is easy to want. The trouble is that a script written for jq depends on far more than the filter language: how numbers are printed, which error comes out and with what exit code, what happens on the edge cases nobody tested. A tool that is fast but answers a little differently moves the cost from your CPU to your debugging time. So 0.8.0 was built around one question: can a jq be several times faster and still give the same answers, every time, provably? At 0.7.0, in April, succinctly was a succinct-data-structures library with a jq-flavoured command line. About 3,000 merged pull requests later, it is a jq 1.7.1 replacement whose differences are all listed and whose speed figures anyone can regenerate."},{"id":"fast","heading":"Fast: about three times jq, and more as inputs grow","text":"Across all 223 workloads the geomean speedup is 3.18x on an AMD Ryzen 9 7950X, 3.10x on an AWS Graviton4 (ARM Neoverse-V2) and 2.84x on an Apple M4 Pro. The lead grows with the size of the input. Each workload, which the reports call a row , is one jq program over one input: field access, select , map , group_by , regex, @csv , NDJSON streams, early exits, plain pretty-printing, over generated documents of several shapes at 1, 10 and 100 MB, plus small real files and process startup. The chart groups them by input size. Input size AMD Ryzen 9 7950X AWS Graviton4 Apple M4 Pro jq 1.7.1 (1x) Startup 1.19 1.01 0.69 1 1 MB 2.37 2.34 1.84 1 10 MB 3.86 3.70 3.77 1 100 MB 5.01 4.85 5.51 1 Notice that the three chips climb together, from about 2x at 1 MB to about 5x at 100 MB, and part company mainly at startup, where the M4 Pro dips below jq's line. Startup is 2 rows, 1 MB 106, 10 MB 73 and 100 MB 42. Each point is a geomean over its rows, so no single row's speedup should be read off it. Why does the lead grow? A fixed cost, starting the process, is the same for both tools at any size, and at 1 MB it is still a good part of the run. What grows with the input is the cost of reading it, and that is where succinctly does less work. Instead of a tree of values it builds a compact structural index of the text, and it turns into values only the parts the program reads. How succinctly reads JSON without building a tree walks through that index. Ducky asks Three times faster. Isn't that just a Rust tool using all my cores? Predict: how much of the 3x comes from running on more than one core? Most of it, some of it, or none? Commit to a guess before you open this. None of it. succinctly jq runs one thread. The reports measure CPU time over wall-clock time for . on a 10 MB input, where a ratio near 1.00 means one thread: it is 0.50 on the 7950X and the M4 Pro and 1.00 on the Graviton4, against jq's 0.94 to 0.97. Neither tool uses more than one core. \"Most of it\" is the natural guess, because many modern command-line tools win that way. \"Some of it\" is the guess that a little parallel parsing helps on big files. The real difference is the amount of work per byte, which is also why the gap widens with size rather than staying a constant multiple. Geomean speedup over jq 1.7.1 at commit 955a984a3 (below 1.00x jq is faster); the per-size figures are under the chart's “Show data”. Every row's output was checked against jq's before its timing counted. Chip All 223 rows jq faster on Piped stdin (24 rows) 1 GB tier (6 rows) AMD Ryzen 9 7950X 3.18x 0 of 223 4.32x 3.92x AWS Graviton4 (ARM Neoverse-V2) 3.10x 7 of 223 4.55x 3.65x Apple M4 Pro 2.84x 26 of 223 4.39x 3.96x The stdin and 1 GB columns are separate sets of rows, kept out of the 223: the same programs fed through a pipe, and streaming and early-exit programs over files of about 1 GB. The fastest single rows reach 44x to 55x ( ascii_upcase over every string of a 100 MB document, on the 7950X) and 58x ( jq -c . over a 100 MB document of nested objects, on the M4 Pro). Those are the tail of the distribution, not the typical case; the geomean is the number to plan with. Every speed figure here is from the comparison harness's own build ( cli,simd,regex,serde plus bench-runner ). The release archives are built with cli alone, and no figure was measured on that build."},{"id":"faithful","heading":"Faithful: jq's answers, including its quirks","text":"The jq mode follows jq 1.7.1 bug for bug. It differs only for a stated reason, and every difference is recorded. \"Compatible\" usually means \"the common filters work\". For a tool that replaces jq inside scripts, the bar has to be the output bytes and the exit status, on the odd inputs as well as the common ones. Three small cases, each captured from jq 1.7.1, jq 1.8.2 and the 0.8.0 binary: Output of each program in three tools ( -c , so one line each). The first two rows follow jq 1.7.1; the third follows jq 1.8.2's fix of a 1.7.1 bug. Program and input jq 1.7.1 jq 1.8.2 succinctly jq ., map(. + 0) on [100000000000000000001, 1.000, 1E2] [100000000000000000001,1.000,1E+2] [1e+20,1,100] the same the same [limit(-1; 1,2,3)] with -n [1,2,3] limit doesn't support negative count , exit 5 [1,2,3] [last(empty)] with -n [null] [] [] The first row is jq's number handling: a literal is printed with the exact digits it was written with, 1E2 becomes 1E+2 , and arithmetic falls back to a double. The second is a case where jq 1.8 changed the behaviour and succinctly keeps 1.7.1's, because 1.7.1 is the version it replaces. Ducky asks Why copy jq's bugs at all? Wouldn't fixing them be better? Better for a new script, perhaps, but a script that already runs was written against the jq that has them, and a \"fix\" is a change in what that script prints. So the default is to match, and the exceptions are narrow: output jq could not read back, a result that would corrupt data or drop a write, a crash, or a bug that jq 1.8 has fixed itself. The third row is that last case: jq 1.7.1's last answers null for a generator that yields nothing, jq 1.8 fixed it, and succinctly follows the fix. Bug for bug: what jq compatibility means sets out the rule that decides each case. What the comparison checks, identically on all three chips: jq's own test suites: 446 of the 447 cases in jq 1.7.1's jq.test , all 224 in man.test , 39 of 40 in onig.test and 7 of 7 in base64.test . The two misses are the two places where succinctly follows a jq 1.8 fix: a NaN with a payload is an invalid number (CVE-2024-53427, #7522 ), and a regex group that takes no part in an empty match is unmatched ( #7520 ). Generated programs: 6,000 random programs and documents from a typed grammar, over three fixed seeds. jq itself fails to compile or times out on 104 of them. Of the other 5,896, all but 2 give jq's exit status, jq's output and jq's class of error, and the 2 are again jq 1.8 fixes. Builtins and flags: 201 of 207 builtins agree on eight probe inputs each; the six that differ depend on the host ( get_jq_origin , the libc's weekday in strptime ), are a crash in jq ( strftime on a non-string format), or follow 1.8. Of 490 command-line probes, 459 to 464 agree; the rest are by design, such as --version naming succinctly, or follow 1.8. Each of those differences is an entry in a ledger, which holds 65 in all, and CI fails if the comparison finds a difference the ledger does not list, or an entry that no longer differs. At 0.8.0 both counts are 0. The full list is docs/compliance/jq/limitations.md ."},{"id":"lean","heading":"Lean: less than half of jq's memory on large inputs","text":"From 10 MB up, succinctly jq peaks at less memory than jq on every chip, and at 100 MB less than half. Below that it uses more, and the table says so. Geomean of succinctly jq 's peak RSS over jq 1.7.1's on the same rows (below 1.00 succinctly uses less), commit 955a984a3 . Chip 1 MB 10 MB 100 MB Piped stdin 1 GB tier All 223 rows AMD Ryzen 9 7950X 1.06 0.55 0.41 0.43 0.49 0.72 AWS Graviton4 (ARM Neoverse-V2) 1.66 0.67 0.48 0.54 0.64 0.98 Apple M4 Pro 1.58 0.68 0.46 0.54 0.64 0.96 The small-input column is a fixed cost: a run that reads no input peaks at about 4 to 7 MiB in succinctly against jq's 2.5 to 2.9 MiB, and at 1 MB that fixed part is still most of succinctly's peak. As the input grows, what counts is what each tool keeps per byte of input. jq keeps a tree of values. succinctly keeps the text itself plus an index of about a fifth to two-fifths of the input's size ( what the index costs ). The best rows, pretty-printing or compacting a 100 MB document, peak at 0.12 to 0.14 of jq's memory. Predict: for . over the 1 GB document in the table above, jq peaks at 9.7 GiB. Roughly how much does succinctly need: about 5 GiB, about 1 GiB, or a few MiB? About 1 GiB: 1.27 GiB, on all three chips. That is about 1.25 times the 1.02 GiB document (the text itself, plus its index and what printing needs), where jq's 9.7 GiB is about 9.5 times it. \"About 5 GiB\" is what you get if you assume any jq must build some tree of the document, just a tighter one. \"A few MiB\" is the streaming intuition: true for a stream of many small values, where 0.8.0 reads a large NDJSON file a piece at a time, but one 1 GB array is one value, and . has to print all of it. The floor for a single document is having the text at hand, and the index is what keeps the rest small."},{"id":"honest","heading":"Honest: numbers you can regenerate","text":"A speed claim is only as good as the method behind it. Every figure on this page comes from reports committed to the repository, and the tool that wrote them ships with the source. Output first, timing second. A row's time counts only if both tools printed the same bytes. All 223 rows passed that gate on all three chips. Interleaved repetitions. jq and succinctly alternate within each of 21 repetitions per row, so a machine that warms up or slows down during a run affects both alike. Running all of one tool and then all of the other once made an improved binary measure up to twice as slow. Geomean, not the best row. The headline is the geometric mean over every row, which a few very fast rows cannot dominate. The reports also flag each row whose spread is above 5%, and list every row jq wins. Each chip measured on its own. No figure is carried from one architecture to another. An earlier optimisation measured 6.1x on an M4 Pro and 16.4x on a Zen 4, which is why the reports cover three chips. Reproducible end to end. scripts/compare-jq-build.sh builds the pinned jq releases from source, succinctly dev compare jq --full regenerates a report, and scripts/compare-ec2/ reruns the Graviton4 report on a fresh AWS instance. Reproducing the comparison has the steps. The method earned its keep during the release itself. The release-commit run on the 7950X showed three rows 5% to 8% slower relative to jq than a run of the previous commit that same morning, and the only code change between the two never reached those rows. Re-timed interleaved, beside an A/A' control of two copies of the old binary, the two copies agreed within 0.1% and the new binary was within 0.1% to 1.0% of the old by median; instruction counts moved by fewer than 100 out of 13.8 to 80.3 million. The shift was between two separate runs of two and a half hours each, not in the binary. Benchmarking against jq honestly tells that story and the rest of the method. The same discipline also measured the release getting faster. A run a week earlier, before the final round of fixes, read 2.74x on the 7950X, 2.71x on the Graviton4 and 2.56x on the M4 Pro, with jq faster on 9, 28 and 30 rows."},{"id":"where-jq-wins","heading":"Where jq still wins","text":"\"About three times faster\" is a geomean, and a geomean has rows below it. These are the cases where jq is the better choice today, or where succinctly behaves differently on purpose. Startup on Apple silicon. With no input to speak of, jq starts faster on the M4 Pro (0.69x). That accounts for all 26 rows jq wins there: startup-bound programs such as args_many and ndjson_inputs_first (0.68x, which reads one value and stops), and small real files such as a 2.7 KB npm package.json . On Linux startup is a tie or better (1.01x on the Graviton4, 1.19x on the 7950X), and the Graviton4's 7 losing rows are all between 0.96x and 0.99x. If your workload is thousands of tiny documents on a Mac, keep jq. Memory on small inputs. At 1 MB succinctly peaks at 1.06x to 1.66x of jq's memory, as the memory table shows. Binary size. Stripped, the measured binary is 17 to 21 MiB against jq's 0.8 to 0.9 MiB, roughly twenty times larger. Malformed input it never reads. jq parses the whole document before it runs, so any syntax error fails the run. succinctly reads lazily, so a filter that never reaches the bad token gets its answer. Ask for jq's strictness, and more, with --validate : $ echo '{\"a\":1,\"b\":tru}' | jq -c .a jq: parse error: Invalid literal at line 1, column 15 $ echo '{\"a\":1,\"b\":tru}' | sjq -c .a 1 $ echo '{\"a\":1,\"b\":tru}' | sjq --validate -c .a jq: validation error: invalid keyword 'tru' (expected null, true, or false) --> <stdin>:1:12 | 1 | {\"a\":1,\"b\":tru} | ^ A pipe that stays open. succinctly reads standard input to its end before the program runs, except under --stream . tail -f app.log | jq -c .msg prints as lines arrive; with sjq it prints nothing until the pipe closes. For live streams, use jq or --stream . jq 1.8's new features. succinctly targets jq 1.7.1, so it agrees with 488 of the 550 cases in jq 1.8.2's jq.test . If you rely on 1.8, stay on 1.8. A few recursive programs. Very deep recursion is refused at the native stack's limit where jq keeps going, and a handful of recursive shapes cost more time or memory than in jq. The answers are jq's wherever they finish; each case is an open issue on the limitations page . The repository's guide to choosing between jq and succinctly jq ties each of these to the report rows behind it, and a test fails when a regenerated report stops supporting one."},{"id":"also-new","heading":"Also in 0.8.0","text":"jq's stream flags: --stream , --stream-errors and -b are supported, alongside --seq . Streams in flat memory: a large NDJSON or --seq file, or a big pipe of them, is read a piece at a time, so a plain filter's peak memory stays at a few MiB however long the stream is. Locale-aware dates: strftime and strflocaltime use the names and layouts of LC_TIME , as jq does. succinctly yq , as a preview: it aims to follow yq v4.53.3, including many of yq's input and output formats (XML, TOML, CSV, TSV, properties, INI, KYAML). Its known differences are on the yq limitations page . The comparison harness: succinctly dev compare jq , which produced every number above. For library users: rust-version is 1.89.0, now checked in CI; the crate ships src/ only and is 6.9 MB; BitVec and BalancedParens gain select0 . The 0.8.0 section of the changelog runs to over 2,200 entries, most of them fixes that brought one more corner of jq's behaviour into line."},{"id":"get-started","heading":"Get started","text":"Install with cargo install succinctly --features cli , or from the v0.8.0 release . Run succinctly install-aliases , then use sjq in place of jq , or link it in as jq . Check it on your own data: diff its output against jq's on the filters your scripts use. If they differ and the difference is not on the limitations page , that is a bug: open an issue ."},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"How succinctly reads JSON without building a tree : the structural index, and how a query finds its way through the text without a tree. Benchmarking against jq honestly : output gating, interleaving, A/A' controls, and the 7% slowdown that turned out to be nothing. Bug for bug: what jq compatibility means : when succinctly may answer differently from jq, and how each difference is checked. The comparison reports , one per chip, with every row's timing and memory."}]},{"href":"docs/benchmarking-against-jq.html","title":"Benchmarking against jq honestly","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer · Benchmarking Benchmarking against jq honestly Lots of tools claim to beat jq. This is how succinctly's claim is measured: what gets timed, what has to match before a timing counts, how the tools take turns, how 223 ratios become one number, and how to tell a real slowdown from noise. A faster-than-jq number means something only if the method rules out the usual ways benchmarks mislead. succinctly's comparison times no row whose output differs from jq 1.7.1's, interleaves the tools inside every repetition, and sums up 223 rows per chip with a geometric mean. The reports behind 3.18x (AMD Ryzen 9 7950X), 3.10x (AWS Graviton4) and 2.84x (Apple M4 Pro) are committed with their provenance, and one script reruns the Graviton4 one on a fresh AWS instance. You can check them rather than trust them. The common mistake: trusting a timing because its minimum and median agree. Drift during or between runs moves both together, so their agreement is necessary, never sufficient. This page is the method behind the succinctly 0.8.0 announcement , which has the results. If you already run interleaved A/B comparisons with a control, skip to the release-day case study and see whether you'd have called it the same way."},{"id":"the-claim","heading":"What a faster-than-jq number has to survive","text":"On an AMD Ryzen 9 7950X, succinctly jq 0.8.0 is 3.18x faster than jq 1.7.1: that is the geometric mean over 223 workloads. Every speed figure on this page is of the comparison harness's own build: a release build with the features cli,simd,regex,serde plus bench-runner , stripped. The release archives are built with cli alone, and no committed figure is of that build. You have probably seen numbers like that before, and learned to ask what they hide. Benchmark claims tend to go wrong in one of four ways. Each of the next sections is the check for one of them: The fast tool did a different job. It printed something else, or stopped early. The check is an output gate . The machine changed between the tools' turns. The check is interleaving . The workloads were picked to win, or a few huge wins carry the average. The check is many rows and a geometric mean , with the rows jq wins listed. Noise was read as a result. The checks are an A/A′ control and instruction counts . The page ends with a slowdown that showed up on release day, and the four checks at work on it."},{"id":"rows","heading":"What gets timed","text":"The comparison is a subcommand of the succinctly binary, succinctly dev compare jq . Files in the repository drive it, not code: a manifest pins the reference builds and the inputs, and a table lists the queries. With --full , it times 84 queries over 40 inputs: 223 query and input pairs, which the reports call rows. 2 rows with no input ( startup and args_many ) measure start-up. 106 rows at 1 MB, 73 at 10 MB and 42 at 100 MB run over generated documents of several shapes: users, nested, wide objects, strings, numbers, NDJSON and JSON text sequences. The 1 MB tier also holds seven real files from a benchmark corpus, all under 1 MB. Reported apart: 24 rows that pipe the input on standard input, and, with --huge , six rows at 1 GB. Neither group moves the headline numbers. Everything that could drift is pinned. jq 1.7.1, the reference, and jq 1.8.2, timed beside it, are built from source whose SHA-256 is in the manifest. The harness never uses the jq on your PATH . Every generated input is checked against its SHA-256 too. The run records the commit, CPU, OS, toolchain and build features in env.json . It marks itself INVALID if the tree is dirty, the machine is on battery, the build is not a release build, or its features differ from the manifest's. Why three chips? Because memory-bound effects don't carry over from one CPU to another. One fix in this codebase ( #106 ) measured 6.1x on an Apple M4 Pro and 16.4x on a Ryzen 9 7950X. So each chip gets its own report, and no figure here is carried from one chip to another."},{"id":"output-gate","heading":"First, the outputs must match","text":"A timing compares two tools doing the same job, so the first thing the harness checks is that they did the same job. The usual quick benchmark is hyperfine over two commands, which times whatever each command does. Suppose one of them printed a number in another format, skipped a record, or failed early with an error. Then you would be timing two different jobs, and a tool that does less is easy to make fast. So before any timing, the harness runs each tool once on the row and keeps two things: its exit status, and the SHA-256 of everything it wrote to standard output. Then it compares succinctly's pair with jq 1.7.1's: // Output-identity gate: hash each tool's stdout, compare with its exit status. let mut digest = vec![]; for t in tools { let e = exec_digest(t, &args, feed.unwrap_or_default(), inputs_dir, deadline)?; // … (a deadline check) digest.push((e.exit, e.stdout_sha256)); } let gate_of = |i: usize| { if huge && (ends[0].killed() || ends[i].killed()) { Gate::Incomplete } else if digest[i] == digest[0] { Gate::Match } else { Gate::Differs } }; A row that differs stops there, before its first timed run. It gets no timing, and the report lists it as a correctness failure. jq 1.8.2 gets the same check, and a row where it disagrees only loses its 1.8.2 column. In the committed runs, all 223 rows matched jq 1.7.1 on all three chips , so every row has a timing. Ducky asks So matching output means succinctly checks the input as carefully as jq does? No. The gate compares answers on these inputs, and the inputs are valid JSON, apart from the jsonseq-bad rows, which carry one truncated record on purpose. succinctly reads JSON by building a light index instead of a full tree, and it validates less than a full parser while doing it. How succinctly reads JSON covers that trade-off, and --validate for when you need strict checking. The gate makes a narrower promise: on every row it times, the two tools printed the same bytes and exited the same way."},{"id":"interleaving","heading":"Then, the tools take turns","text":"With the outputs matched, the order you run things in can still decide the result. The naive plan is to run jq 21 times, then succinctly 21 times, and compare the medians. This repository has a record of what that does. Running all of one binary and then all of the other made an improved binary measure 0.47-0.9x, up to 2x slower, on every workload. The second half started on a machine the first half had warmed up. Ducky asks Wouldn't more repetitions average that out? No. Averaging cancels noise that goes both ways, and this drift goes one way: the later tool is always on the warmer machine. More runs only measure the bias more precisely. In that record, the minimum and the median agreed with each other, and both were wrong. The fix is to take turns. In the naive order , each tool's position decides its conditions. In the interleaved order , each repetition runs every tool once and the starting tool rotates, so each one goes first, second and last equally often: Notice that in the top row a tool's place in the queue decides whether it runs cool or warm. In the bottom row no tool has a fixed place: over a 21-repetition row, each of the three goes first 7 times. The loop is a few lines of the harness: 'reps: while rep < total { for k in 0..tools.len() { let i = (rep + k) % tools.len(); let (ms, end) = time_once_ended(&tools[i], &args, inputs_dir, feed)?; Before the loop, each tool gets one untimed warm-up run. A --full run gives start-up and 1 MB rows 21 repetitions, 10 MB rows 9 and 100 MB rows 3. A row whose slowest tool takes longer than 15 ms gets more repetitions, up to 21, but no more than 30 seconds of extra timed runs. A row that reaches the full 21 is rated by each tool's fastest run instead of its median, because the median of a long row moves with the run and its minimum does not. Rows whose run-to-run variation is over 5% are flagged as noisy in the report. Peak memory comes from separate, untimed runs, so the program measuring it never adds to a timing."},{"id":"geomean","heading":"Many rows, one number","text":"Now there are 223 ratios, one per row, and they need a summary. Which average you pick is itself a claim. Predict: succinctly is 2x faster than jq on one row and 2x slower on another. What is its average speedup over the two? A guess is enough; decide before you open this. 1.00x: no speedup. A 2x win and a 2x loss cancel, and the geometric mean says so: √(2 × 0.5) = 1.00. The ordinary mean of the ratios says (2 + 0.5) ÷ 2 = 1.25x, so if you guessed 1.25x, that is the misconception the geometric mean exists to fix. Turn the ratios round and work out jq's speedup over succinctly instead: the ordinary mean is again (0.5 + 2) ÷ 2 = 1.25x. Each tool would be \"25% faster\" than the other. The ordinary mean of ratios rewards whichever tool you put on top of the fraction. The geometric mean treats a factor of 2 the same in both directions. On the 7950X's 223 rows the gap is large. The ordinary mean of the ratios is 4.58x, and the geometric mean, the figure every report quotes, is 3.18x. The difference is mostly a tail of a few huge wins: strings_upcase is 44-55x faster on the 7950X at every size. The reports quote the smaller number, and these tail rows are never the headline. Geometric-mean speedup over jq 1.7.1 (jq's time over succinctly's; below 1.00x, jq is faster), commit 955a984a3 . The standard-input and 1 GB columns are separate rows, not part of the 223. CPU Start-up 1 MB 10 MB 100 MB All 223 rows jq faster on stdin (24) 1 GB (6) AMD Ryzen 9 7950X 1.19x 2.37x 3.86x 5.01x 3.18x 0 of 223 4.32x 3.92x AWS Graviton4 (ARM Neoverse-V2) 1.01x 2.34x 3.70x 4.85x 3.10x 7 of 223 4.55x 3.65x Apple M4 Pro 0.69x 1.84x 3.77x 5.51x 2.84x 26 of 223 4.39x 3.96x Read each line from left to right: on every chip the ratio grows with input size, from start-up to 100 MB. A constant-factor trick can't produce that curve. Doing less work per byte can, and that is the explainer's subject. Then read the \"jq faster on\" column. On the M4 Pro, jq wins 26 rows, and start-up time decides every one of them. Twenty-one are small corpus files, two are the start-up rows, and three are ndjson_inputs_first , which reads only the first record of its input, even a 100 MB one. The slowest are args_many and ndjson_inputs_first at 0.68x. On the Graviton4, jq wins 7 rows, all between 0.96x and 0.99x. On the 7950X it wins none. jq also starts faster on Apple silicon: 0.69x on the M4 Pro. Memory gets the same treatment, as a geometric mean of peak-memory ratios. At 100 MB succinctly holds less than half of jq's peak memory on all three chips: 0.41 of it on the 7950X, 0.48 on the Graviton4 and 0.46 on the M4 Pro. At 1 MB it holds more than jq: 1.06, 1.66 and 1.58 times as much."},{"id":"noise-floor","heading":"How big is nothing? The A/A′ control","text":"Every number so far is a ratio of two noisy measurements. Before you read meaning into a change in one, you need to know what no change looks like. The tempting check is to compare today's run with yesterday's. But two runs hours apart differ in many things you did not change: the room temperature, what else woke up on the machine, how the OS laid out memory. A difference between two runs is a question, not an answer. The control is an A/A′ comparison : time a binary against a byte-identical copy of itself, interleaved, exactly as you will time the real change. Whatever it reports is noise by construction, and that is the bar a real difference has to clear. The harness's --control flag does this for the jq comparison: it runs jq in succinctly's place, so the report shows jq against itself. For comparing two succinctly builds, the repository's scripts/ab-cli.py --control does the same with the older binary. Ducky asks If A and A′ are the same file, why would they ever differ? In principle, they shouldn't. Whatever they do differ by is the resolution of your measurement, on this machine, today. A change smaller than that is not a finding, however many decimal places it has."},{"id":"placement","heading":"Same instructions, different speed","text":"An A/A′ control bounds the noise of the machine. It can't bound the noise of building a different binary, because A and A′ share one code layout by construction. Change any code, even code the benchmark never runs, and the linker may place the hot functions a few bytes away from where they were. On x86_64 that can change how the CPU's front end copes with a tight loop's branches, while it runs exactly the same instructions. In #3100 , a change to math builtins, code the query never runs, made keys_unsorted on a 10 MB document 12% slower by minimum and 18% by median on the 7950X. The instruction counts were identical. To test it, the base was rebuilt with an inert padding function, which executes nothing and only shifts the code after it. Shifted by 112 bytes, the same query read 14.7% slower by minimum. Shifted by 128 bytes, it was back within 0.4%. The effect repeats every 64 bytes. The repository's rule since then: on x86_64, a wall-clock difference between two builds whose per-function instruction counts agree is code placement, until a padding test says otherwise. That makes the instruction count the second signal. Cachegrind counts the instructions a program executes, and neither machine load nor code placement moves that count. The harness adds it with --cachegrind on Linux, for rows up to 1 MB, since cachegrind runs about 50 times slower. A count can't see what the CPU does with the instructions, so it can't tell you how fast a build is. What it can tell you is whether the work changed. Time that moved while the count stayed flat came from the machine or the layout, not the code."},{"id":"case-study","heading":"Release day: three rows 5-8% slower","text":"Here is a measurement that needed all of this, from the day the 0.8.0 numbers were taken. On the 7950X, the release commit's run took about two and a half hours. It was compared with a run of the previous commit from that morning. Three rows had become 5-8% slower relative to jq, by median and by minimum alike, while jq's own times on those rows barely moved: Release run against the morning run of the previous commit, AMD Ryzen 9 7950X, 21 repetitions per row. \"Relative to jq\" is succinctly's change after dividing out jq 1.7.1's. The programs are . , -c . and [.users[]]|length . Row Relative to jq, median Relative to jq, minimum jq 1.7.1's own median identity , nested-1mb +6.8% +7.9% +0.1% identity_compact , strings-10mb +5.4% +5.8% +0.6% users_length , users-1mb +5.0% +7.8% +0.8% Across all 223 rows, jq 1.7.1's medians had moved by a geometric mean of +0.4% between the two runs, so the machine as a whole was not slower. The only code change between the two succinctly binaries was a revert of some tail-call code in the evaluator ( #7945 ), and none of these three programs reaches it. For scale, on the release run identity over nested-1mb took a median 2.19 ms against jq's 13.45 ms. The question was never whether succinctly is faster on this row. It was whether the release binary had just lost 7% of its lead. Ducky asks Three rows, all slower, minimum and median both past 5%, and jq flat. Isn't that a regression? That is the question to answer before tagging the release. The check was the one from the A/A′ section : three binaries, timed interleaved on just these three rows. A was the previous commit, rebuilt. A′ was a byte-identical copy of A. B was byte-identical (same SHA-256) to the binary the release run had timed. Before any timing, each one's output on each row was checked against jq's. Predict: timed side by side like this, how much slower is B than A on these three rows? About 7%, a few percent, or under 1%? Decide before you open this. Under 1%: +0.1%, +0.1% and +1.0% by median, while A′ read within 0.1% of A. Input Two long runs (relative to jq) Interleaved: A′, an identical copy Interleaved: B, the release binary nested-1mb 6.8 0.0 0.1 strings-10mb 5.4 0.0 0.1 users-1mb 5.0 0.0 1.0 The slowdown the two long runs showed ( identity , identity_compact and users_length , by input) is not there when the binaries take turns. A′ and B are each compared with the pooled runs of A and A′, so A′'s bar is the noise floor. The interleaved run had three rounds. In each round every binary ran 601 times on each 1 MB row and 301 times on the 10 MB row, taking turns, with an idle check before and after the round (no busy cores, load average 0.18-0.44). All of it took about a minute and a quarter. Each binary against the pooled runs of A and A′, all three rounds (1,803 runs per binary on the 1 MB rows, 903 on the 10 MB row). Row A′ median B median B per round B minimum identity , nested-1mb +0.0% +0.1% +0.1, +0.1, +0.3% +1.3% identity_compact , strings-10mb −0.0% +0.1% +0.4, +0.2, +0.2% +0.3% users_length , users-1mb +0.0% +1.0% +1.2, +1.1, +0.7% +1.4% The minimums are noisier than the medians here: between the identical copies, one round's minimum moved by up to 1.5%, so B's minimums are inside that range too. The instruction counts settle the rest. B executed 83, 99 and 77 fewer instructions than A, out of 13.8 million, 80.3 million and 17.8 million. The few that differ are in the same five libc and dynamic-loader functions on all three rows, by almost the same amounts whatever the input, so they belong to start-up, not to reading JSON. Instructions executed (cachegrind I refs ), one run each. Row A B B − A identity , nested-1mb 13,826,716 13,826,633 −83 identity_compact , strings-10mb 80,279,237 80,279,138 −99 users_length , users-1mb 17,785,243 17,785,166 −77 Verdict: the slowdown belonged to the gap between two separate two-and-a-half-hour runs, not to the binary. We never found out what moved between those runs. The method doesn"},{"id":"reproduce","heading":"Check it yourself","text":"The reports are committed. A claim you can rerun is worth more than one you have to trust. Each chip's report is a pair of files in docs/compare/ . The Markdown is for reading. The JSON has every row's samples, minimum, median, spread and peak memory, plus the run's env.json provenance: the commit, CPU, OS, toolchain, build features, and whether the timed binary was the stripped harness build. The commit measured was 955a984a3 , and the 0.8.0 binary has not changed since. --verify reruns the deterministic parts against a committed report and spot-checks five of its timing rows, and exits 1 on any difference. git clone https://github.com/rust-works/succinctly.git && cd succinctly git checkout --detach 955a984a3 scripts/compare-jq-build.sh 1.7.1 # sha256-verified source builds into target/compare/ scripts/compare-jq-build.sh 1.8.2 scripts/sync-bench-corpus.sh # the corpus files too large to commit cargo build --release --locked --features cli,simd,regex,serde,bench-runner ./target/release/succinctly dev compare jq --full --huge # or: rerun the deterministic parts and spot-check a committed report ./target/release/succinctly dev compare jq --verify docs/compare/jq-linux-x86_64.json The 7950X run took about two and a half hours. On a Graviton4, --full takes about three hours and --huge adds about one. The 1 GB tier needs about 16 GiB of memory, because jq holds a 1 GB document at 9-11 GB. Check that the machine is idle before you start: the harness records the load, but it can't make the machine quiet. mkdir -p ~/.config/succinctly cp scripts/compare-ec2/compare-ec2.env.example ~/.config/succinctly/compare-ec2.env $EDITOR ~/.config/succinctly/compare-ec2.env # your region and bucket name scripts/compare-ec2/compare-ec2.sh setup # bucket, role, a security group with no inbound rules scripts/compare-ec2/compare-ec2.sh bake # an image with Rust and both pinned jq builds scripts/compare-ec2/compare-ec2.sh run --commit 955a984a3 scripts/compare-ec2/ reproduces the Graviton4 report on a fresh instance (by default a c8g.2xlarge ). The instance clones the commit, checks that HEAD is exactly it, builds, runs --full --huge , uploads the results and provenance to S3, and terminates itself. It has a hard time cap and an idle watchdog in case anything hangs. The repository's guide put a run at a few dollars in November 2026. A reproduction fixes the commit, the jq sources, the inputs and the build's features, and checks each one. It does not fix the Rust toolchain, the OS image or the physical machine behind an instance type. It records those instead. So expect your numbers to agree with the committed ones to within the run's own noise, not to the last digit, and only on the same kind of chip."},{"id":"checklist","heading":"What to ask of the next faster-than-jq claim","text":"Before you open the list below, try to name the four checks from memory, each with the way of going wrong it guards against. Compare with your list Did both tools do the same job? Output and exit status compared before any timing, on every row. Did they run under the same conditions? Interleaved within each repetition, never all of one and then all of the other. Is the average honest? Many rows, a geometric mean, a curve over input sizes rather than one ratio, and the rows the other tool wins listed by name. Is the difference bigger than nothing? An A/A′ control for the noise floor, and instruction counts to tell a change in the work from a change in the machine or the layout. Then one more question, for any number at all: which chip? A figure without one can't be checked, and the next chip can tell a different story. The trigger for the A/A′ habit is any comparison of two runs that weren't interleaved. A short experiment shows your own floor. Copy a binary ( cp \"$(command -v jq)\" ./jq-copy ) and time the original against the copy, taking turns, on a 1 MB input. Whatever difference it reports is the smallest one your setup can see."},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"succinctly 0.8: a jq you can trust, three times faster : the release these measurements are for. How succinctly reads JSON without parsing it : why the speedup grows with input size. Bug for bug: what jq compatibility means : the other half of the comparison, the part that checks answers rather than times. A/B benchmarking method : every rule above, with the mistake that taught it, plus the padding-test tables from #3100. comparison/jq/README.md : every dimension of dev compare jq and the files that drive it. Reproducing the jq comparison : what makes a run valid, which binary it measures, and the EC2 pipeline in full. perf.rs : the gate, the interleaving loop and the repetition rules."}]},{"href":"docs/bug-for-bug-jq-compatibility.html","title":"Bug for bug: what jq compatibility means","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer Bug for bug: what jq compatibility means Why succinctly jq reproduces jq 1.7.1's quirks, when it is allowed to differ, and how every difference is checked and written down. succinctly jq gives the answer jq 1.7.1 gives, quirks included. It departs only on a short, closed list of grounds, every departure is written down, and the comparison run fails on any difference it finds that is not. A drop-in replacement is only drop-in if the scripts you already have keep printing what they printed. Writing every difference down means you can read what will change before you switch, instead of finding out in production. The common mistake is to hear \"compatible\" as \"correct by some standard\", or as \"the same as the newest jq\". It means the observable behaviour of one pinned binary, its accidents included. This page is one of three that go with the succinctly 0.8 announcement . Every output on it was captured from three binaries on macOS (arm64): the system's jq 1.7.1, Homebrew's jq 1.8.2 and the released succinctly 0.8.0, whose sjq alias is succinctly jq ."},{"id":"the-question","heading":"What should a jq replacement print?","text":"Start with a file that is not quite JSON: an array with a stray NUL byte glued to a number. What should a jq replacement print for it? printf '[1\\0x,2]\\n' | jq -c . All three print the same array, and the x is gone. Result jq 1.7.1 jq 1.8.2 succinctly jq 0.8.0 stdout [1,2] [1,2] [1,2] exit status 0 0 0 Note the newline at the end; the next section shows why it matters. A careful parser would reject this text: 1 , a NUL and an x are not a number. jq reads it as [1,2] because it hands each number token to its number parser as a C string, and a C string ends at the first NUL, so the x is never seen. That is an accident of jq's implementation, and the jq manual says nothing about it. But it is what jq does, so a pipeline that has been feeding jq such files has been getting [1,2] , and a \"fixed\" replacement would break that pipeline the day it was installed. Ducky asks Why would anyone want a copy of jq's bugs? Nobody wants the bug for its own sake. What they want is that swapping jq for sjq changes nothing they did not ask for. A replacement that tidies up jq's surprises makes changes nobody requested, and the people it hurts are exactly the ones who depended on what jq really printed. So the specification is not the manual or the JSON standard. It is the observable output of one binary, jq 1.7.1, pinned by version. That is what bug for bug means here, and succinctly's ADR-0018 makes it the rule. The rule also says how a fact about jq is admitted: only when it was captured from the pinned binary with the command shown, never recalled and never inferred from succinctly's own output. succinctly is written in Rust, which has no C strings, so this answer did not come for free: reading the NUL as jq reads it took its own fix ( #7437 )."},{"id":"first-rule","heading":"A first rule: match jq 1.7.1 everywhere","text":"The simplest version of the rule is \"whatever 1.7.1 prints, print that\". It is about right, and it breaks on the same byte one step later. Move the NUL to sit between two whole values, and leave off the final newline: printf '[1]\\0[2]' | jq -c . jq 1.7.1 prints the first value, drops the second and reports success. Result jq 1.7.1 jq 1.8.2 succinctly jq 0.8.0 stdout [1] [1] [1] stderr (nothing) jq: parse error: Invalid numeric literal at line 1, column 5 jq: parse error: Invalid numeric literal at line 1, column 5 exit status 0 5 5 The cause is the same C-string habit, one layer up. jq 1.7.1 reads its input in pieces of up to 4,095 bytes, and when a piece holds no newline it measures it with strlen , which stops at the NUL. Everything after the NUL in that piece is thrown away without a word. In a short file with no trailing newline, the whole file is one such piece, so [2] is never read, and the exit status says all went well. Matching that would mean a replacement that silently loses records and reports success. That is the crack in the first rule: some of jq's behaviour is not a quirk that someone's script relies on, it is damage to their data. Ducky asks Hang on. The first example dropped the x too. Why is that one fine and this one not? Look at what goes missing. In the first example, every value in the text comes out: the bytes jq skips are the inside of one malformed token, and the array still has both its numbers. In this one, a complete, valid value disappears, and the exit status hides it. The first is a reading of bad input that you might depend on; the second loses good input. So the rule needs exceptions, but a list that grows whenever someone finds jq's behaviour odd is no rule at all. ADR-0018's answer is a closed list . succinctly may depart from the pinned jq only when: jq emits output it cannot itself read back; matching would corrupt a document or silently discard data; matching would take the process down (succinctly is a library as well as a command, so its crash is its caller's crash); or no portable dependency can express jq's behaviour, which has to be shown with a written table of the candidates that were considered. \"The reference's behaviour is surprising\", \"our architecture makes it awkward\" (without that table) and \"the other mode does it differently\" are named in the ADR as reasons that do not qualify. And every departure must be recorded on the jq limitations page : in the ADR's words, a divergence that is not written down is a bug that has not been found yet. The dropped [2] falls under the second, since matching would mean losing input while reporting success, and the limitations page records it ( #5654 ). Notice the middle column of the table: jq 1.8.2 agrees with succinctly here. Upstream fixed the truncation as a security bug (CVE-2026-33948). That is a second reference point, and it raises the next question. Go deeper: where the NUL rows still differ from both jq releases Not every NUL row is settled. With the NUL inside an array and no newline, the three tools give three answers, captured with printf '[1\\0,2]' | jq -c . : Result jq 1.7.1 jq 1.8.2 succinctly jq 0.8.0 stdout (nothing) [1,2] (nothing) stderr jq: parse error: Unfinished JSON term at EOF at line 1, column 2 (nothing) jq: error (at <stdin>:0): Invalid JSON text: expected ',' or ']', found '\\0' exit status 5 0 5 The limitations page says so in as many words: jq 1.8.2 reads this as [1,2] , and succinctly jq does not follow that change yet. A recorded residual is still a difference, but it is one you can look up rather than trip over."},{"id":"which-jq","heading":"Which jq? Follow 1.8.2 where it fixed a bug","text":"jq 1.8.2 exists, and it disagrees with 1.7.1 in dozens of places. A replacement pinned to 1.7.1 has to decide what to do about each one. The tempting answer is \"follow the newest jq\". But 1.8 did not only fix bugs; it also changed the language and added limits, and following those would change answers that 1.7.1 users get today and that were not wrong. So the rule succinctly applies is narrower: where upstream fixed a 1.7.1 answer as a bug, give 1.8.2's answer; otherwise stay with 1.7.1. Each such case is still recorded, as a difference from the pin with the category newer-than-primary . Here is one a jq user is likely to meet. last(f) of an f that produces nothing: jq -nc '[last(empty)]' jq 1.7.1 invents a null ; jq 1.8.2 and succinctly produce nothing. Result jq 1.7.1 jq 1.8.2 succinctly jq 0.8.0 stdout [null] [] [] exit status 0 0 0 jq 1.7.1 defines last(g) as reduce g as $item (null; $item) , and a reduce always produces one value, so when g is empty you get the seed, null . That made last disagree with first , which yields nothing. jq 1.8 changed it in a commit titled \"fix: make last(empty) yield no output values\" (jqlang/jq#3179), listed in its release notes under changes to existing functions. That is upstream calling the 1.7.1 answer a bug, so succinctly follows 1.8.2 ( #7518 ). Ducky asks If 1.8.2 is right about this, why not just target 1.8.2 and be done? Because 1.8.2 is a different language in places, and plenty of jq installed today is not 1.8: the jq bundled with macOS, for one, is 1.7.1. Targeting 1.8.2 would change the meaning of programs written against the jq people actually run. The narrow rule takes 1.8's corrections and leaves its redesigns alone."},{"id":"fix-or-feature","heading":"A fix, a feature or a guard","text":"The narrow rule turns on one question: did upstream call the change a bug fix? Try it on a change that is not. jq 1.8 added Tcl-style comment continuation: a comment that ends in a backslash (an odd number of them) carries on to the next line (jqlang/jq#2989). Its release notes list it under language changes , not under fixes. Here is a three-line program where it matters: 1 # a comment ending in a backslash \\ + 2 jq -n -f prog.jq In 1.8.2 the comment swallows the + 2 . Result jq 1.7.1 jq 1.8.2 stdout 3 1 Predict: what does succinctly jq -n -f prog.jq print, 3 or 1 ? Decide before you open this. Result jq 1.7.1 jq 1.8.2 succinctly jq 0.8.0 stdout 3 1 3 exit status 0 0 0 3 , as jq 1.7.1 prints. Upstream presents the continuation as a new language feature, not as a repair of something 1.7.1 got wrong, so the pin stands. If you said 1 , you were applying \"follow the newest jq\", which is the rule this section set aside: a 1.7.1 user's program, with a comment that happens to end in a backslash, would silently lose its + 2 . If you were unsure, that is the right instinct: the decision does not depend on which answer looks better, only on what upstream says the change was. jq 1.7.1 does have a narrow special case for a backslash comment: skip_shebang drops the first three lines of a -f file that opens with the shell/jq polyglot. succinctly implements that rule too ( #7481 ). There is a third kind of change in 1.8.2: hard limits. jq 1.8.2 refuses, for example, to compare two values nested more than 10,000 levels deep, a security fix (CVE-2026-47770) that protects its C implementation's memory safety: jq -n 'def deep: reduce range(10001) as $_ ([]; [.]); deep == deep' jq 1.8.2's cap refuses an input that 1.7.1 and succinctly answer correctly. Result jq 1.7.1 jq 1.8.2 succinctly jq 0.8.0 stdout true (nothing) true stderr (nothing) jq: error (at <unknown>): Equality check too deep (nothing) exit status 0 5 0 Here 1.7.1's answer is not a bug: true is correct. The cap guards a C program's memory safety, and succinctly, which answers correctly and has its own guard against running out of native stack, would gain no safety by adopting it, only lose capability. So it is not adopted, and the limitations page records all five of 1.8's new limits of this kind as deliberate differences from 1.8.2."},{"id":"decision-order","heading":"The whole rule, in order","text":"Put the three sections together and you get a short procedure. It is applied in this order, to every behaviour where the candidates differ. Does matching jq 1.7.1 meet one of the four conditions? Output jq cannot read back, corrupted or discarded data, a crashed process, or no portable way to express it. Then refuse jq's behaviour and record the divergence. (The dropped [2] .) Did upstream fix the 1.7.1 answer as a bug in 1.8.2? Then give 1.8.2's answer and record it as newer than the pin. ( last(empty) .) Otherwise, match jq 1.7.1 exactly, including its error messages and its own inconsistencies. (The swallowed x , and the comment that does not continue.) Only when two options are equally faithful, choose the faster one or the one that uses less memory. This step can never create a divergence, because it only ever chooses between answers that match. Steps 1, 3 and 4 are ADR-0018's decision order (its #2416 amendment). Step 2 is how the limitations page applies it to jq's own later releases: an upstream bug fix is adopted, while a feature, a refactor or a rewording is not."},{"id":"how-its-checked","heading":"How it is checked","text":"A rule like this is only worth as much as the checking behind it. succinctly's comparison harness, succinctly dev compare jq , measures it against a source build of jq 1.7.1. The four counts below are the same in its committed reports from an AMD Ryzen 9 7950X, an AWS Graviton4 (ARM Neoverse-V2) and an Apple M4 Pro. 446 / 447 jq 1.7.1's own jq.test cases 224 / 224 man.test , the manual's examples 5,894 / 6,000 fuzzed programs that agree 65 ledger entries; 0 unlisted, 0 stale jq's own test suites. jq 1.7.1 ships test files, and the harness runs them through both tools. Besides the two above: onig.test (regular expressions) 39 of 40, base64.test 7 of 7, manonig.test 17 of 17 and optional.test 3 of 3. Both misses are cases where succinctly follows a 1.8 bug fix: jq.test 's fromjson | isnan on \"nan1234\" , which 1.8 rejects (CVE-2024-53427), and an optional group in an empty regex match, which 1.8 reports as unmatched. Against jq 1.8.2's suites it is informational only: 488 of 1.8.2's 550 jq.test cases agree. The rest test what 1.8 changed on purpose: how far as and unary minus reach, new builtins such as add(f) and trimstr , and the depth limits above. Probes of the whole surface. Of the 218 builtins jq 1.7.1 lists, 207 are probed and 201 agree; there are also 1,097 syntax probes (1,090 agree) and 490 command-line probes, from --indent to locales. Separately, 308 of 308 error-message probes are byte-identical to jq's, because a message is part of what a program sees: try f catch (if test(\"Cannot index\") then …) is a real jq idiom. Grammar fuzzing. Three fixed seeds generate 2,000 random programs and documents each, and every result is compared on exit status, standard output and the class of any fault. The ledger. Every difference the harness finds must be listed in comparison/jq/divergences/ , one file per entry, with a category and a reason. The run fails on a difference that is not listed, and on a listed one that no longer differs, so the list cannot drift from the truth in either direction. Ducky asks 5,894 out of 6,000 is 98%. So the fuzzer finds succinctly wrong on 2% of programs? No. Of the 106 programs that do not count as agreeing, 104 were skipped: jq refused to compile them, or one tool timed out, and a generator's mistake is not evidence. The other 2 are real differences, and both are rows you have already seen: one is last of an empty generator, the other a foreach whose initial value is a generator, which jq 1.8 also fixed as a bug. Both are in the ledger as newer than the pin, and the run would fail if a finding were not."},{"id":"ledger","heading":"What the 65 ledger entries are","text":"The ledger holds every difference the harness's probes reach; the limitations page also records some no probe reaches, such as the 1.8 limits above. Its categories show where the differences come from: more than half are 1.8 bug fixes adopted on purpose, and none is waiting to be classified. The ledger at v0.8.0, by category. The examples are entries in it. Category Entries What it means Example newer-than-primary 36 Follows a 1.8.2 bug fix [last(empty)] is [] ; malformed --indent values are refused, as in 1.8 by-design 11 A deliberate difference with a recorded reason --version names succinctly; --debug-dump-disasm is refused, since there is no bytecode host-dependent 10 The answer depends on the platform's C library, so the tools differ by host strptime 's weekday for a format that sets no date reference-crash 6 jq 1.7.1 crashes where succinctly answers or reports an error [] | .[1e12] = 1 crashes jq 1.7.1; succinctly exits 5 with an error reference-slow 2 jq 1.7.1 does not finish within the probe's 10 seconds gsub(\"a\";\"b\") over 100,000 characters Each entry's file names its probe, and most link the issue that decided it, so \"why does sjq differ here?\" always has an answer you can read."},{"id":"gotchas","heading":"Edge cases worth knowing","text":"Compatible does not mean identical on every host jq calls the platform's C library for maths and dates, and so does succinctly. Apple's and glibc's libm disagree in the last bit on many ordinary tan inputs, so a float digit from a maths builtin is a fact about a platform, not only about a jq version. succinctly matches the jq on the same platform, not one golden answer for all of them. Deep recursion is refused, not run. A user-defined function that recurses 10,000 deep runs in jq (in about 3 seconds); succinctly's native-stack guard refuses it with exceeded maximum recursion depth rather than risk the process. It is in the ledger as by-design . Less validation is a separate trade-off. succinctly reads JSON through a structural index rather than a full parse, so a filter checks only the parts of the text it reads. How succinctly reads JSON explains why, and --validate checks the whole text first when you need that. succinctly yq is a preview. Everything on this page is about the jq mode. The yq mode aims at yq v4.53.3 under the same rule, with its own limitations page."},{"id":"your-turn","heading":"Your turn","text":"Without scrolling back: what are the questions, in order, that decide whether succinctly jq gives jq 1.7.1's answer? Say them to yourself, then compare with the procedure above . Now apply them to two cases this page has not shown. The facts about upstream are given; the answer is yours. \"1\\u00002\" | tonumber (a NUL inside the string): jq 1.7.1 prints 1 , reading up to the NUL. jq 1.8.2 refuses the string, and its release notes list \"Fix tonumber and toboolean to reject strings with embedded null bytes\". \"abc\" | split(\"b\"; empty) (a flags argument that yields nothing): jq 1.7.1 prints [\"abc\"] ; jq 1.8.2 prints nothing, after a commit titled \"Refactor splits/2 using foreach syntax\". Predict: for each of the two, does succinctly jq give 1.7.1's answer or 1.8.2's? Program jq 1.7.1 jq 1.8.2 succinctly jq 0.8.0 \"1\\u00002\" | tonumber 1 string (\"1\\u00002\") cannot be parsed as a number , exit 5 string (\"1\\u00002\") cannot be parsed as a number , exit 5 \"abc\" | split(\"b\"; empty) [\"abc\"] (nothing) [\"abc\"] tonumber follows 1.8.2; split stays with 1.7.1. Neither matching 1.7.1 here corrupts data or crashes, so step 1 does not apply to either, and step 2 decides. Upstream calls the tonumber change a fix, so it is adopted ( #7521 ). It calls the split change a refactor, so the pinned answer stands, even though 1.8.2's looks tidier ( #7560 ). If you picked 1.8.2 for both because both look like improvements, that is the \"follow the newest\" model again: the rule asks what upstream said the change was, not which answer you prefer. If you picked 1.7.1 for tonumber because the NUL looked like data loss, look again at the dropped [2] : there a whole value vanished from the input, here the string is the input and jq 1.7.1 reads part of it, which is why this one is decided by step 2 rather than by step 1. The next time a jq replacement, or any drop-in tool, says it is compatible, these are the questions to ask it: compatible with which version, by which rule, and where is the list of every place it is not?"},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"succinctly 0.8: a jq you can trust, three times faster : the release this page belongs to. Benchmarking against jq honestly : how the same harness times every row, and why a row's output is checked against jq before its timing counts. How succinctly reads JSON without parsing it : the index behind the speed, and what it does not validate. ADR-0018 : the fidelity rule, its four conditions and its decision order, with the cases that shaped them. The jq limitations page : every recorded difference, with captured commands from both jq releases. Its \"Deliberate divergences\" section has the 1.8 caps. The ledger's README and the committed comparison reports : the categories, and the per-chip counts quoted above."}]},{"href":"docs/component-gallery.html","title":"Component gallery","kind":"reference","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Reference Component gallery Every building block in the theme, rendered live. View source to copy the markup — each section is self-contained. Pages are plain HTML linking theme/bh.css and theme/bh.js — no build step. Chrome (top bar, contents, progress, anchors, theme toggle) is added automatically. Interactive parts are web components that fall back to readable static content."},{"id":"typography","heading":"Typography","text":"A .lead paragraph opens a section with a little more presence. Body copy is set in IBM Plex Sans at a comfortable measure, with headings in Fraunces. Inline elements: inline_code() , ⌘ K , highlighted text , ADR , and a link . Sidenotes keep asides out of the main line of thought. Hover, focus or tap the number to read it: it opens in the same popup as a glossary term."},{"id":"headings","heading":"A third-level heading","text":"Second-level headings get an automatic §n eyebrow and appear in the contents; third-level headings are indented beneath them."},{"id":"callouts","heading":"Callouts","text":"Note Neutral information the reader should not miss. Tip A shortcut or recommended practice. Gotcha Something that surprises people. Danger Data loss, security, or outage risk."},{"id":"badges","heading":"Badges","text":"Draft Proposed Accepted In review Rejected Superseded High Medium Low v2.4.0"},{"id":"cards-metrics","heading":"Cards & metrics","text":"1 Single source Tokens in one file drive every colour, size and duration. 2 Both themes Light and dark from light-dark() — no duplicated rules. 3 Degrades well No JavaScript? Steps become a list, tabs become sections. 99.95% Availability (30d) 142 ms p99 latency +18% 3.2k Requests / s +6% 0 Open incidents"},{"id":"charts","heading":"Charts","text":"Write the numbers as a table inside <bh-chart> to get a themed line or column chart. Hover, or focus the chart and use the arrow keys, to read every series at a point. The table stays one click away under “Show data”. Day Before After Mon 412 402 Tue 398 260 Wed 430 190 Thu 405 176 Fri 441 181 Sat 390 172 Sun 420 168 Tail latency fell by more than half once the edge limiter shed abusive bursts. emphasis puts the story in colour and the context in grey. Region Per service At the edge us-east 4.1 1.2 eu-west 3.6 1.1 ap-south 6.8 1.9 sa-east 2.2 0.8 Column charts group series per category. Only the extreme value is labelled; the axis, tooltip and table carry the rest."},{"id":"comparisons","heading":"Comparisons & decisions","text":"PostgreSQL Relational, mature, already operated by the team. Transactions across tables Existing runbooks Vertical scaling ceiling DynamoDB Managed key-value with predictable latency. No capacity planning Access patterns fixed up-front Vendor lock-in We will use PostgreSQL with read replicas, because transactional integrity outweighs elastic scaling at our current size. Revisit when write volume exceeds 5k/s. When the choice depends on how much each criterion matters, show the scoring with <bh-matrix> . Readers can move the weights and watch whether the leader changes. Scores 1–5, higher is better. Weights 0–5 say how much each criterion matters. Criterion Weight PostgreSQL DynamoDB CockroachDB Multi-table transactions 4 5 2 5 Team familiarity 3 5 2 2 Elastic scaling 2 3 5 4 Cost at current scale 3 4 3 2 Vendor independence 2 5 1 4"},{"id":"sequences","heading":"Steps & timelines","text":"Drain the node. Stop routing new traffic and wait for in-flight requests. Upgrade. Apply the new image and restart the service. Verify & restore. Health checks pass, then re-add to the pool. 14:02 Deploy begins Config change rolled to 5% of hosts. 14:06 Error rate spikes 5xx climbs to 12% on canary hosts. 14:09 Alert fires On-call paged by SLO burn alert. 14:15 Rolled back Error rate returns to baseline."},{"id":"data","heading":"Key-values & tables","text":"Service billing-api Owner Payments platform Runtime Rust 1.90, Tokio, Axum Latency by region, last 7 days. Region p50 p99 Meets SLO us-east-1 21 ms 96 ms Yes eu-west-1 24 ms 131 ms Yes ap-southeast-2 38 ms 412 ms No"},{"id":"disclosure","heading":"Disclosure & predict-then-reveal","text":"Go deeper: why not just retry? Retries multiply load during an outage. Without jittered backoff and a retry budget, a brief blip becomes a sustained overload. If each of 3 layers retries 3 times, how many calls can one request cause? 4³ = 64. Each layer makes up to 4 attempts (1 + 3 retries), and they multiply."},{"id":"code","heading":"Code","text":"Language classes get syntax highlighting (Prism, loaded only when needed). Every block gets a copy button; data-title labels it. pub fn try_acquire(&mut self, now: Instant) -> bool { self.refill(now); // ① if self.tokens >= 1.0 { self.tokens -= 1.0; // ② true } else { false // ③ } }"},{"id":"annotated-code","heading":"Annotated code","text":"let tokens = min(capacity, tokens + elapsed * rate); 1 if tokens >= cost { tokens -= cost; allow() } 2 else { reject(retry_after(cost - tokens)) } 3 Refill lazily from elapsed time — no background timer needed. Spend tokens only when the request is admitted. Tell the client exactly when to come back."},{"id":"diffs","heading":"Diffs","text":"timeout_ms: 2000 - retries: 5 + retries: 2 + retry_budget: 0.1 backoff: exponential"},{"id":"code-walkthrough","heading":"Code walkthrough","text":"For explaining an implementation, <bh-codewalk> pins the code beside the prose. As you scroll, each step highlights its lines and shades the rest. use std::time::{Duration, Instant}; pub struct TokenBucket { capacity: f64, // b: the largest burst rate: f64, // r: tokens per second tokens: f64, last: Instant, // monotonic clock } impl TokenBucket { pub fn try_acquire(&mut self, cost: f64) -> Result<(), Duration> { let now = Instant::now(); let elapsed = now.duration_since(self.last).as_secs_f64(); self.tokens = (self.tokens + elapsed * self.rate).min(self.capacity); self.last = now; if self.tokens >= cost { self.tokens -= cost; Ok(()) } else { Err(Duration::from_secs_f64((cost - self.tokens) / self.rate)) } } } The whole state is four fields. Capacity and rate are configuration; tokens and last are all the limiter remembers per client. Refill lazily. No timer runs in the background: on each call, add the tokens earned since last and clamp to capacity. Admit by spending tokens. Weighted requests just pass a larger cost . Reject with a time. The error carries exactly how long until enough tokens exist, which becomes the Retry-After header."},{"id":"diagrams","heading":"Diagrams","text":"Inline SVG drawn with theme classes — .node.c1…c6 , .edge , .zone , .label . Colours follow the theme; arrowheads are automatic. Pair a diagram with <bh-stepper for=\"…\"> to walk through it. Each step is linkable ( #gallery-arch-step-3 ), and the link button in the stepper bar copies a link to the current step. A request path. Use the stepper below; edges animate while lit. External Network edge Our service Storage Infrastructure Prose can point into a diagram. Hover over the gateway , or over the service and its two stores , to light those parts. Click one to bring the diagram into view. Mark up the phrase with data-ref=\"figure-id:part-id …\" . The client calls the gateway over HTTPS; TLS terminates here. The gateway authenticates and forwards to the service . The service checks the cache first. On a miss it reads the database and back-fills the cache. Side effects are published to the queue asynchronously."},{"id":"current-vs-proposed","heading":"Current vs proposed","text":"Wrap two versions of a diagram in <bh-versions> to switch between them, or press Compare and drag the divider. Mark parts with data-change=\"added|changed|removed\" to show what the proposal changes. The highlights start off, so a page first reads as it will look: a page with marked parts gets a button in the top bar, the switch for the whole page, and pressing it shows them. Each figure then has a Show changes button in its bar to turn its own highlights and legend off and on; that choice holds while you switch versions or compare, and while the page's switch is turned off and on again. Neither choice is part of a link. Where there is no button, the highlights show: without JavaScript, in print and in the slides. Every service enforces its own limit, so a client's real limit grows with the number of services. One limiter at the gateway sees all of a client's traffic, so the limit holds however many services there are."},{"id":"diff-marks","heading":"Diff marks","text":"What npm run diff and the Review panel's \"changes since\" put on a page. A mark names the kind of change before the block it is about, the block gets a bar, and the words that differ are struck through underlined . Authors never write these by hand. Changed text The limiter refills its bucket on a timer lazily, on each request , so an idle client costs nothing. Added A rejected request never reaches the service. Removed A heading that is gone The old version of a block is an inert copy: no ids, and links are plain text. Moved from “Typography” A block that turns up elsewhere is a move, not a removal and an addition."},{"id":"sequence-diagrams","heading":"Sequence diagrams","text":"Describe actors and messages, and <bh-sequence> lays them out with the theme's vocabulary. Replies are dashed, notes sit over one or more lifelines, and every part keeps its data-id , so a stepper can walk through the exchange. Client Limiter Service GET /orders refill tokens take 1 token GET /orders 200 OK later: bucket empty GET /orders 429 · Retry-After: 0.5 The limiter answers rejected requests itself; the service never sees them. The client calls the limiter. The limiter refills lazily, then takes a token. Admitted: the request reaches the service, which replies. When the bucket is empty, the limiter replies 429 without bothering the service."},{"id":"mermaid","heading":"Mermaid (fallback)","text":"For quick sequence or state diagrams, a <pre class=\"mermaid\"> renders with theme colours (loaded from a CDN only when present). Prefer hand-placed SVG for anything central to the argument. sequenceDiagram participant C as Client participant L as Limiter participant S as Service C->>L: request alt token available L->>S: forward S-->>C: 200 OK else bucket empty L-->>C: 429 Retry-After end"},{"id":"tabs","heading":"Tabs","text":"let resp = client.get(url).send().await?; resp, err := http.Get(url) resp = httpx.get(url)"},{"id":"playground","heading":"Playground","text":"Named inputs feed data-expr outputs and data-bind attributes — live models with no custom script. Replicas Per-replica capacity (rps) Offered load (rps) Utilisation % · headroom rps"},{"id":"glossary-terms","heading":"Glossary terms","text":"Words defined in the glossary are underlined automatically the first time they appear on a page. Hover over one to see its definition. For example, a gateway might enforce a rate limit with a token bucket and answer rejected calls with Retry-After, keeping p99 latency inside the SLO. The popup stays put while it's open. Move the pointer into it to select and copy text or follow its links. Click a term, or press Enter on it, to pin the popup; Esc or a click elsewhere closes it. To mark a word explicitly, use data-term=\"id\" , as in this GCRA reference . Add class=\"no-glossary\" to keep a passage unmarked."},{"id":"layout","heading":"Layout","text":"Children of .bh-article sit in the reading column. Add .wide to use the gutters, or .full to go edge to edge. .wide — figures, tables, comparisons .full — hero visuals, big diagrams"}]},{"href":"docs/deflate-format-reference.html","title":"DEFLATE format reference","kind":"reference","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Reference · Compression DEFLATE format reference Every range, field and bit-order rule in a DEFLATE stream, in one place, for reading a stream by hand or writing a decoder. The two explainers in this series say why the format is built this way; this page says exactly what it is. A series in three parts How DEFLATE compresses : the two savings, how they join, and a block read bit by bit. How a DEFLATE block sends its own code : codes fitted to the data, and how a block describes them cheaply. DEFLATE format reference , this page: every range, field and bit-order rule, for reading a stream or writing a decoder."},{"id":"two-alphabets","heading":"Every range, and the decoder's loop","text":"How LZ77's output maps onto DEFLATE's two alphabets, with the exact ranges. Part 1's join explains why lengths and distances become a range symbol plus plain extra bits. How LZ77's output maps onto DEFLATE's two alphabets. Where a symbol covers a range, plain extra bits follow it to pick the exact value; they are not Huffman-coded. LZ77 gives Symbol Alphabet Extra bits In the example a literal byte, 0–255 0–255, the byte's own value literal/length 0 a (byte 97) → 97 the end of the block 256 literal/length 0 256 , once at the end a length, 3–10 257–264, one each literal/length 0 length 4 → 258 a length, 11–257 265–284, each covering 2–32 lengths literal/length 1–5 length 23 → 270 ( 23–26 ) + 2 bits a length of 258 285 literal/length 0 a distance, 1–4 d0–d3, one each distance 0 a distance, 5–32,768 d4–d29, each covering 2–8,192 distances distance 1–13 distance 7 → d5 ( 7–8 ) + 1 bit; 12 → d6 ( 9–12 ) + 2 bits The decoder's inner loop runs the join backwards. Its two tables are the two alphabets: while True: sym = read_code(lit_len_table) # literal, length or end if sym < 256: out.append(sym) # a literal byte elif sym == 256: break # end of block else: i = sym - 257 length = LENGTH_BASE[i] + read_bits(LENGTH_EXTRA[i]) d = read_code(distance_table) # always follows a length distance = DIST_BASE[d] + read_bits(DIST_EXTRA[d]) for _ in range(length): # byte by byte: may overlap out.append(out[-distance])"},{"id":"not-symbols","heading":"Everything in the stream that isn't a symbol","text":"Parts 1 and 2 introduce each part of a stream where it is needed. To read a stream yourself, or to write a decoder, you need them in one place, with one rule for how each is written. A DEFLATE stream is mostly Huffman codes, but not entirely. Bits are written in two ways, and a decoder must know which kind comes next: A Huffman code is read one bit at a time, most significant bit first, because the decoder walks down the code tree as each bit arrives. Everything else is a plain number of a fixed, known width, read lowest bit first. Each byte of the stream fills from its lowest bit up, so a plain number's bits arrive in the order they are packed. In other words, DEFLATE is little-endian throughout, at the level of bits and of bytes (a stored block's 16-bit LEN comes low byte first), except that Huffman codes are stored bit-reversed. Write a byte the usual way, most significant bit on the left, and its plain fields read as ordinary binary, stacked from the right. Here is the first byte of part 2's dynamic block : 0 1 1 1 0 | 1 0 | 1 HLIT | BTYPE | BFINAL = 14 | = 2 | = 1 A Huffman code reads correctly in arrival order instead, so in this byte view it would look reversed. Decoders such as zlib reverse each code once, when they build their lookup tables, so that one little-endian bit reader can handle everything. Every plain field in a DEFLATE stream. None of these is Huffman-coded: the decoder knows each one's width from what came before it. Field Bits Where Holds BFINAL 1 start of every block 1 if this is the last block BTYPE 2 after BFINAL the block type ( values ) length extra bits 0–5 after a length symbol which length within the symbol's range: 270 means lengths 23–26, and its 2 extra bits, 0–3, add the offset ( try one ) distance extra bits 0–13 after a distance symbol which distance within the symbol's range: d6 means distances 9–12, and its 2 extra bits add the offset, so 11 = 3 gives 12 HLIT 5 dynamic block, after BTYPE the number of literal/length code lengths, minus 257: 257–288 is 32 values HDIST 5 after HLIT the number of distance code lengths, minus 1: 1–32 is 32 values HCLEN 4 after HDIST the number of code-length-code lengths, minus 4: 4–19 is 16 values code-length-code lengths 3 each after HCLEN , 4–19 of them the lengths of the code that compresses the code lengths, in the fixed order; that code's lengths are at most 7 run extra bits 2, 3 or 7 after code-length symbol 16, 17 or 18 the run's count, minus 3, 3 or 11 alignment 0–7 stored block, after BTYPE nothing: skipped to reach the next byte boundary LEN , NLEN 16 each stored block, after the alignment the number of bytes, then its ones' complement as a check stored bytes 8 each stored block, after NLEN the data itself, byte for byte final padding 0–7 after the last block nothing: fills the last byte Everything else in the stream is a Huffman code: the literal/length and distance symbols, and, inside a dynamic block's header, the code-length symbols. The worked example's 103 bits show both kinds side by side in the bit walk . Nothing in the stream says how long a field is. The decoder always knows what comes next and how big it is, in one of three ways: A width fixed by the RFC , chosen to fit the field's range: BFINAL , BTYPE , HLIT , HDIST , HCLEN , the 3-bit code-length-code lengths, and a stored block's LEN and NLEN . After BTYPE = 2, the next 5 bits are HLIT because the format says so. A width set by the symbol just decoded : the extra bits. Symbol 270 is always followed by 2 extra bits, d6 by 2, run symbol 18 by 7. No width at all : Huffman codes end themselves. The decoder reads one bit at a time until the bits so far match a code, and since no code is the start of another, it can never stop in the wrong place. That is why the stream needs no separators or length prefixes, and also why it must be read strictly in order: where HDIST starts depends on having read HLIT , and one corrupted bit misaligns everything after it."},{"id":"edge-cases","heading":"Edge cases","text":"Overlapping copies Allowed and common: distance 1 with length 258 repeats the previous byte 258 times. A decoder must copy byte by byte, not with a block copy, or it reads bytes that haven't been written yet. The longest match 258 bytes, with its own symbol (285) and no extra bits, so with the fixed codes a long run costs 13 bits per 258 bytes. 258 = 255 + 3: a length fits in a byte once the minimum of 3 is subtracted. End of block Symbol 256 ends every Huffman block, even the last. It needs a code, so it counts as a symbol when the tree is built. Empty input zlib writes 2 bytes, 03 00 : a final fixed block holding only the end-of-block code. Incompressible input Stored blocks cap the overhead at 5 bytes per 65,535. A large file of random bytes grows by under 0.01%; the 1 KiB in the chart grows by 0.5%."},{"id":"terms","heading":"Terms used on this page","text":"These are defined here, for this series. DEFLATE's broader terms are in the site glossary : DEFLATE , LZ77 , Huffman code , canonical Huffman code , back-reference , sliding window , end-of-block and extra bits . Alphabet The fixed set of symbols a Huffman code is built over. DEFLATE uses three: literal/length, distance and code-length. How the two ideas join BFINAL The first bit of every block: 1 if it is the last block of the stream. Blocks, and the fixed code BTYPE The 2 bits after BFINAL that give the block type: 00 stored, 01 fixed Huffman codes, 10 dynamic Huffman codes. 11 is invalid. Like every plain number, they arrive lowest bit first. The values Code length The number of bits in a symbol's Huffman code. 0 means the symbol has no code. A dynamic block sends only the lengths; the codes follow from them by the canonical rule. Send lengths, not trees Code-length code The small Huffman code a dynamic block uses to write down its code lengths. Its alphabet has 19 symbols: 0–15 are lengths, 16 repeats the previous length, and 17 and 18 are runs of zeros. CL is short for code length. Huffman-code the run symbols Distance alphabet The 30 symbols, d0–d29, that stand for back-reference distances from 1 to 32,768. Most stand for a range, picked out by extra bits. It has a Huffman code of its own. How the two ideas join Dynamic Huffman code Codes built for one block from that block's own symbol counts, and sent as code lengths at its start ( BTYPE 10). They don't change while the block is read: dynamic means per block, not adaptive. A dynamic block, bit by bit Fixed Huffman code Codes set once by the RFC, for any block with BTYPE 01, so no table is sent: 8 or 9 bits per literal, 7 or 8 per length symbol, 5 per distance symbol. Why 7–9 bits HCLEN 4 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many code-length-code lengths follow, minus 4. Those lengths come in a fixed order, so the count can cut off the usually-unused ones at the end. Why that order HDIST 5 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many distance code lengths follow, minus 1. In the bit walk HLIT 5 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many literal/length code lengths follow, minus 257. The lengths come as a bare list in symbol order, so the count says where the list ends. In the bit walk Literal A byte that LZ77 sends as itself, because no match of 3 or more starts there. It becomes the literal/length symbol with the same value, 0–255. LZ77 Literal/length alphabet The 286 symbols 0–285: 0–255 for literal bytes, 256 for the end of the block, and 257–285 for ranges of lengths. One Huffman code covers them all, so a single code says whether a literal or a copy comes next. How the two ideas join Overlapping copy A back-reference whose length is greater than its distance, so it reads bytes it is itself writing. It works because the decoder copies one byte at a time; distance 1 simply repeats a byte. The bit walk shows one Plain number A field of fixed, known width that is not Huffman-coded, read lowest bit first: the block header, the extra bits, a dynamic block's counts and code-length-code lengths, and a stored block's LEN and NLEN . Every plain field Prefix code A code in which no codeword is the start of another, so codes can be read back to back without separators. Every Huffman code is a prefix code. Huffman Stored block A block of up to 65,535 raw bytes ( BTYPE 00), after a byte-aligned length and its complement. Encoders use it when compressing would make the data bigger. Choosing a block type Symbol One element of an alphabet: what Huffman coding counts and gives a code. DEFLATE maps LZ77's tokens onto symbols, so Huffman coding never sees the raw bytes. How the two ideas join Token One item of LZ77's output: a literal, or a back-reference (length, distance). Each becomes one or two symbols. LZ77"},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"RFC 1951, DEFLATE : the format itself, short and readable. Section 3.2 has the alphabets and the canonical code. RFC 1952, gzip and RFC 1950, zlib : the two wrappers, a few header bytes and a checksum around the same stream. Huffman (1952), \"A Method for the Construction of Minimum-Redundancy Codes\" : the original paper. Ziv and Lempel (1977), \"A Universal Algorithm for Sequential Data Compression\" : where LZ77 gets its name. zlib's algorithm.txt : how zlib's encoder chooses matches, which this page leaves out."}]},{"href":"docs/edge-rate-limiting-design.html","title":"Moving rate limiting to the edge","kind":"design","example":true,"sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Design document Moving rate limiting to the edge Replace per-service limiters with one token bucket per client in the gateway, backed by Redis, so a client's limit holds however far we scale. Problem: per-service limits multiply with services and replicas. One client sent 9× its contract without being limited. Proposal: enforce limits once, in the gateway, with an atomic Redis script. Services keep only a coarse safety limit. Ask: review the fail-open policy and the rollout by 17 October."},{"id":"context","heading":"Context & problem","text":"Since ADR-001 , each service has run its own token bucket. That shipped quickly, but a limit enforced in nine places isn't a limit. During the September incident, one client's traffic spread across three services with three replicas each, and every replica admitted it. 9× Peak traffic vs contract, never limited 3 × 3 Services × replicas the limit was split across 412 ms p99 for everyone else during the incident"},{"id":"goals","heading":"Goals & non-goals","text":"Goals One limit per client, accurate to within 5% Rejected requests never reach a service Under 2 ms added at p99 Non-goals DDoS protection: that stays with the CDN Per-endpoint quotas (a later phase)"},{"id":"design","heading":"Proposed design","text":"Compare the two versions below. Today the limiters sit inside each service. In the proposal, the gateway checks one bucket per client in Redis before routing to any service . Each service limits on its own, so a client's real limit is the per-service limit × replicas × services. One bucket per client, checked once at the gateway: the limit holds however many services and replicas there are."},{"id":"request-path","heading":"The request path","text":"The gateway makes one Redis round trip per request. The script refills and takes tokens atomically, so concurrent requests can never spend the same token twice. Client Gateway Redis Orders GET /orders EVALSHA bucket refill + take, atomically allowed · 7.4 left GET /orders 200 OK a burst empties the bucket GET /orders EVALSHA bucket denied · retry 0.4 s 429 · Retry-After: 1 The service only ever sees admitted requests; Redis answers in well under a millisecond in-region. A request reaches the gateway, keyed by its API key. The gateway runs the bucket script in Redis. Refill and take happen in one atomic step. Admitted requests are routed on as before. Once a burst empties the bucket, the gateway answers 429 itself, with a Retry-After the client can trust."},{"id":"limiter-script","heading":"The limiter script","text":"-- KEYS[1] = bucket key (one per API key) -- ARGV = capacity, rate (tokens/s), now (ms, from Redis TIME), cost local b, r, now, cost = tonumber(ARGV[1]), tonumber(ARGV[2]), tonumber(ARGV[3]), tonumber(ARGV[4]) local s = redis.call('HMGET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', 'ts') local tokens = tonumber(s[1]) or b local ts = tonumber(s[2]) or now tokens = math.min(b, tokens + math.max(0, now - ts) / 1000 * r) local ok = tokens >= cost if ok then tokens = tokens - cost end redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', tokens, 'ts', now) redis.call('PEXPIRE', KEYS[1], math.ceil(b / r * 1000) + 1000) return { ok and 1 or 0, tostring(tokens) } Inputs. The gateway passes capacity and rate from the client's plan, plus the current time from Redis itself, so gateway clocks never matter. Read the bucket. A client seen for the first time starts with a full bucket. Refill, then take. This is the same arithmetic as the in-process version. Running it as a script makes the read-modify-write atomic. Write back and expire. An idle key expires once it would be full anyway, so memory tracks active clients only. Answer. Admitted or not, plus the tokens left, which the gateway turns into Retry-After ."},{"id":"alternatives","heading":"Alternatives considered","text":"The gateway option wins under the weights we agreed in the kickoff. Move the weights to test how sensitive the choice is: favouring time to ship above accuracy brings back per-service limits. Scores 1–5, higher is better. Weights 0–5 say how much each criterion matters. Criterion Weight Per service Gateway + Redis CDN rules Accuracy of the limit 5 1 5 3 Latency added 3 5 3 4 Operational cost 3 4 3 4 Sees API keys and plans 4 5 5 1 Time to ship 2 5 3 4"},{"id":"results","heading":"Canary results","text":"We ran the gateway limiter for the noisiest 5% of clients for a week. Everyone else's tail latency stopped tracking those clients' bursts. Day Per-service limits Edge limiter Mon 380 371 Tue 412 214 Wed 395 188 Thu 441 181 Fri 404 179 The canary started on Tuesday. p99 for the other 95% of clients fell by more than half and stayed flat through the noisy clients' bursts."},{"id":"risks","heading":"Risks & mitigations","text":"Risk Likelihood Impact Mitigation Redis unavailable Low High Fail open after 5 ms; services' coarse safety limits still apply Hot key for one huge client Medium Medium Shard that client's bucket by region; reconcile every second Clock skew between gateways Low Low None needed: the script reads time from Redis"},{"id":"rollout","heading":"Rollout plan","text":"Week 1 Canary: noisiest 5% of clients Done. The results are above. Week 2 All clients, shadow mode Decide but don't reject; compare against per-service decisions. Week 3 Enforce at the edge Exit criterion: under 0.1% disagreement in shadow mode. Week 5 Retire per-service limiters Keep a coarse safety limit at 10× the plan rate."},{"id":"decision","heading":"Decision","text":"We will enforce client rate limits at the gateway with the Redis bucket script, failing open, and retire the per-service limiters. Recorded as ADR-002 , which supersedes ADR-001."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html","title":"Glossary","kind":"reference","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Reference Glossary Shared definitions for terms used across these documents. Defined words are underlined wherever they appear. Hover over one, or tap it, to read its definition without leaving the page. ADR An architecture decision record: a short, permanent note of one significant decision, covering its context, the options considered and the consequences. ADRs are superseded by new records rather than edited. Back-reference In LZ77 compression, an instruction to copy L bytes starting D bytes back in the output already produced, instead of storing them again. DEFLATE allows distances up to 32,768 bytes. Step by step Backoff Waiting longer after each failed attempt before retrying, usually doubling the delay each time. Without jitter , clients that failed together retry together. Barrier A GPU instruction that makes every thread in a threadgroup wait until all of them arrive, and makes their earlier memory writes visible to each other. There is no safe barrier across threadgroups. Burst A short spike of requests above the average rate. In a token bucket, the largest burst allowed is the bucket's capacity b . Cache miss A lookup that finds nothing in the cache, so the request falls through to the slower source of truth and usually back-fills the cache. Canonical Huffman code A Huffman code rebuilt from code lengths alone: symbols are sorted by length, then by value, and each gets the previous code plus one, shifted left when the length grows. A DEFLATE block sends only the lengths. Step by step Compute kernel A function that runs on the GPU once per thread of a dispatch . Each thread reads its own index to decide which part of the work it does. CRC-32 A 32-bit checksum computed by polynomial division over the bits of the data. ZIP and gzip store one per file so a decoder can detect corruption. Checksums of adjacent pieces can be combined, which allows computing it in parallel. Cyclic redundancy check (Wikipedia) DEFLATE The compression format inside ZIP, gzip and PNG: LZ77 back-references plus Huffman coding , organised in blocks. Its bit stream has no index, so decoding is serial. Step by step · RFC 1951 Dispatch One launch of a compute kernel over a grid of threads, divided into threadgroups . Dispatches in one command buffer run in order, which is the safe way to order work between threadgroups. Divergence When lanes of one SIMD group take different sides of a branch. The hardware runs each side in turn with the other lanes switched off, so the branch costs the sum of both sides. End-of-block Symbol 256 of DEFLATE 's literal/length alphabet. It closes every Huffman-coded block, so it needs a code of its own even though it appears once. Step by step Extra bits In DEFLATE , the plain bits that follow a length or distance symbol to pick an exact value, because each symbol stands for a range: symbol 270 means a length from 23 to 26, and the 2 extra bits after it say which. They are written as a plain number, not Huffman-coded, and the symbol alone decides how many there are. Step by step GCRA The generic cell rate algorithm. It is equivalent to a token bucket, but stores one timestamp per key: the theoretical arrival time of the next conforming request. Generic cell rate algorithm (Wikipedia) Huffman code A variable-length binary code that gives frequent symbols short bit patterns and rare ones long patterns. No code is a prefix of another, so a decoder can read them back to back without separators. Step by step · Huffman coding (Wikipedia) Jitter Randomness added to retry or polling delays, so many clients that start together don't keep acting in lock-step and overload a recovering service. Lane One thread's slot within a SIMD group . Each lane has its own registers, but all lanes execute the same instruction at the same time. lcov A plain-text coverage format that lists, per source file, how many times each line ran. Most coverage tools can write it, which makes it the common currency between them and tools like patchcov. Lea"}]},{"href":"docs/how-deflate-compresses.html","title":"How DEFLATE compresses","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer · Compression How DEFLATE compresses The format inside gzip, zlib, PNG and ZIP is two simple ideas stacked on top of each other: first remove the repetition, then remove the skew. This page builds both up from a single 35-byte string, and ends by reading a real block bit by bit. Compression here is two separate savings: remove repetition, then remove skew. DEFLATE does the first with LZ77 and the second with Huffman coding. The only awkward part is joining the two. It matters because nearly everything uses it: gzip and zlib, PNG images, ZIP files, and Content-Encoding: gzip on the web. The common misconception is that DEFLATE says how to compress. It only says how to decompress: for the same 35 bytes, zlib writes 15 and the encoder on this page writes 13, and both are valid. A series in three parts How DEFLATE compresses , this page: the two savings, how they join, and a block read bit by bit. How a DEFLATE block sends its own code : codes fitted to the data, and how a block describes them cheaply. DEFLATE format reference : every range, field and bit-order rule, for reading a stream or writing a decoder."},{"id":"question","heading":"A question to start","text":"How do you make abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra smaller without losing a single letter? Stored as plain bytes it takes 35 bytes, or 280 bits. Two things about it are wasteful. The same 11 letters appear three times, and the letter a makes up almost half of the text while c and d appear once per word. A good compressor should exploit both. Guess before you read on: compressed as a gzip file by zlib at its best setting (level 9), how many bytes do these 35 take? 33 bytes. That looks like almost no saving, but 18 of those bytes are the gzip wrapper: a 10-byte header, then a CRC-32 and the original length (8 bytes) at the end. The DEFLATE stream inside is 15 bytes , 43% of the input. By the end of this page you will have read, bit by bit, a stream that does it in 13, and seen why the two differ."},{"id":"two-ideas","heading":"Two ideas, stacked","text":"DEFLATE runs two stages, and each removes a different kind of waste. LZ77 turns the bytes into a shorter list of tokens : a literal byte, or an instruction to copy earlier bytes. Huffman coding then writes each token with as few bits as its frequency allows. literal byte back-reference (length, distance) Notice that the output box is drawn to scale: 103 bits against 280. Repetition made the token list shorter (35 bytes, 10 tokens). Skew could make each token cheaper too, but in this block it saves nothing, as the tally at the end shows. The literal and back-reference colours keep their meaning in every figure below. The two stages are independent, and each is useful alone. A file of random English words has little exact repetition but plenty of skew. A file of one byte repeated is all repetition. DEFLATE needs both because real data has both. So let's take them in that order: LZ77 on its own, then Huffman coding on its own, then the one part that isn't obvious, how DEFLATE joins them. Then we read a real block bit by bit, and come back to the opening question with every bit accounted for. If you already know LZ77 and Huffman coding, skip to the join : it is the part you are least likely to have seen. If you'd rather experiment as you go, the playground runs all of it on your own text."},{"id":"lz77","heading":"LZ77: say it once, then point back","text":"Read the input left to right. Keep everything already read in a sliding window (DEFLATE's holds the last 32,768 bytes). At each position, look for the longest run of upcoming bytes that already appears in the window. If it is at least 3 bytes long, emit a back-reference : copy length bytes, starting distance bytes back. Otherwise emit the byte itself as a literal. Why 3? A short copy costs about 12 to 25 bits once it is coded, as the bit walk shows (its 4-byte copy takes 13). Two literals cost 16, so a 2-byte copy would rarely pay; three cost 24. Watch the second copy : it starts 12 bytes back but is 23 bytes long, so it reads bytes that the copy itself is still writing . Start with nothing. The cursor is at the first byte and the window behind it is empty, so there is nothing to point back to. Seven literals. None of a b r a c a d starts a run of 3 bytes seen before, so each one drops out of the input as a literal token. The window grows behind the cursor. A match. The next four bytes, abra , already appear 7 bytes back. Any run of 3 or more is worth replacing, and this one is 4 long. Emit (4, 7). The four bytes collapse into one token: copy 4 bytes from 7 back . Four letters cost one token. The space is new , so it goes out as a literal. A long match that overlaps. Everything that is left, 23 bytes, repeats what started 12 bytes back. Only 12 bytes lie between the source and the cursor, so the copy runs into its own output: the striped bytes, 12 to 22, are both copied from and written by this one copy. Emit (23, 12). The decoder copies one byte at a time, so when it writes byte 24 it reads byte 12, which this same copy wrote 12 steps earlier. 35 bytes are now 10 tokens. Overlap is not a corner case. It is how LZ77 encodes runs: aaaaaaaa is the literal a followed by (7, 1) , \"copy 7 bytes from 1 back\"."},{"id":"huffman","heading":"Huffman: short codes for common symbols","text":"LZ77 has removed the repetition, but it leaves every literal at a full 8 bits: the common a costs as much as the rare d . Eight bits per letter is fair only if every letter is equally likely. In abracadabra the counts are a×5, b×2, r×2, c×1 and d×1, so a should get the shortest code. A first try: hand out the shortest codes in order of count. a gets 0 , b 1 , r 10 , c 11 , d 100 . That looks cheap, but it can't be read back. The codes go out back to back with nothing between them, so 10 could be r , or b then a . The trouble is that b 's code is the start of r 's. So a usable code needs one property: no code may be the start of another . Then a decoder can stop the moment the bits so far match a code. Drawing the codes as a tree gives that property for free. Each 0 goes left and each 1 goes right, and the letters sit only at the leaves. A code then ends at a leaf, and nothing passes through a leaf to make a longer code. What is left is to choose the tree. Huffman's algorithm finds the best one for known counts, and it is greedy: repeatedly join the two rarest things. Here the symbols are the letters themselves, to keep the tree small. DEFLATE runs the same algorithm on the output of LZ77 instead, as the join below shows: on counts of literals, lengths and distances, not of raw bytes. Notice that a sits one step from the root and gets a 1-bit code, while the four rare letters share the bottom row with 3-bit codes. No code is the start of another, because every letter is a leaf. Count each letter. At 8 bits a letter, the 11 letters of abracadabra cost 88 bits. Line them up, rarest first. Each letter is now a leaf, weighted by its count. Join the two rarest: c and d, 1 each, become a node of weight 2. Again. Three things now weigh 2: b, r and the new node. Join any two. Here b and r make 4. Again: 2 and 4 make 6. Only a (5) and this node are left. The last join makes the root, 11, which is every letter. The common a joined last, so it ends up nearest the root. Read the codes. Label every left branch 0 and every right branch 1. A letter's code is the path from the root to it: a is 0 , c 100 , d 101 , b 110 , r 111 . Count the bits. A code's length is the letter's depth in the tree: a has 1 bit, the others 3. Weighted by the counts, the total falls from 88 bits to 23. As a table. The tree has done its job. What remains is each letter with its code and its length, back in the table it started from: a 0 , 1 bit; b , r , c and d 3 bits each. Go deeper: at the second join, three nodes tied at weight 2. Join a different pair and you get a different tree. Does the total change? No: it is still 23 bits. Join c+d with b instead, and the code lengths become a 1, r 2, b 3, c 4 and d 4. The tree is different, but 5×1 + 2×2 + 2×3 + 1×4 + 1×4 = 23 as well. Huffman's algorithm gives an optimal code, not the optimal code. Remember this one: that ambiguity is what DEFLATE removes when a block sends its own code, in part 2 ."},{"id":"joining","heading":"How the two ideas join","text":"The two tools don't fit together yet. Huffman coding works on a known alphabet of symbols, like the five letters of abracadabra , and counts how often each one appears. LZ77's output is not like that: it mixes literal bytes with back-references, and a back-reference is a pair of numbers, a length up to 258 and a distance up to 32,768. Before Huffman coding can start, every token has to become symbols from a fixed alphabet. That translation is the join. The two stages meet once per block, always in this order: LZ77 turns the block's bytes into tokens: literals and (length, distance) copies. Map every token to symbols from two alphabets, described below. This is the join. Code the symbols: one Huffman code for each alphabet. Where those codes come from is a later section . Write the block: a short header, then each token's symbols as their codes, then the end-of-block code. So Huffman coding never codes LZ77's tokens as they are. A literal passes through as its own symbol, but a copy has to become symbols first. The join is therefore a choice of alphabet for everything LZ77 can produce. The obvious alphabet gives every value its own symbol: one per byte, one for the end of the block, one per length from 3 to 258 and one per distance from 1 to 32,768, 33,281 symbols in all. Huffman coding would then count exact values and give the common ones short codes. Let's see what that costs. Look at what LZ77 actually produces. In the 381-byte paragraph from the playground , this page's encoder finds 32 copies at 29 different distances, and 26 of those distances turn up exactly once. Lengths do repeat (length 3 appears 13 times, length 5 nine times), but exact distances barely do. That breaks the obvious alphabet, and you can work out how with nothing but a budget. Some blocks use a code fixed by the format, which can't know which symbols are common ( below ). A code of L bits uses up 2 − L of all bit patterns, since no other code may start with it, and the shares can't add up to more than 1. Give the 256 bytes 9 bits each and they use half of it. The other 33,025 symbols, the lengths and distances, share the other half, so each gets about 16 bits. A copy is a length and a distance, about 32 bits, so copying 4 bytes would save almost nothing over sending them as 4 literals at 9 bits each, 36. A code fitted to the block's own counts doesn't escape either. Its codes are fine: the paragraph's distances still get 4- or 5-bit codes. But a block with its own code has to tell the decoder what that code is, and with this alphabet the description needs room for every distance a copy might have used. How a block describes its code is the subject of part 2 . Here we only need its size, measured with the same method for both alphabets. For the paragraph, with this page's encoder, describing the obvious code takes 567 bits, against 358 for DEFLATE's, and the block comes out 6% larger. On 16 KB of English documentation, where copies reach thousands of bytes back at 1,877 different distances, the description takes 13,075 bits against 718, and the block comes out 14% larger. So the waste is in naming exact distances. What does repeat is roughly how far back a copy reaches. In that 16 KB there are about 3 copies per distance value within 100 bytes back, and about 0.1 per value at 4,000 to 6,000 bytes back, yet neighbouring distances are about equally likely. Whatever replaces the obvious alphabet has to put how far back in the Huffman code, where its skew can be used, and send exactly which distance as plain bits, where a code would find nothing to save. DEFLATE does exactly that, with two changes: A length or distance is a range, then extra bits. Distance 12 has no symbol of its own. It is one symbol meaning \"9 to 12 bytes back\", followed by 2 plain bits that pick 12. The ranges double in width as they reach further back, so the symbol says roughly how far and its extra bits say exactly where. The extra bits are written as they are, not Huffman-coded, bec"},{"id":"join-example","heading":"The join, token by token","text":"Here is the mapping applied to the ten tokens from the window figure , and then the codes that turn the symbols into bits. Notice that the middle row is all Huffman coding ever sees. A back-reference becomes two symbols from two alphabets (square: literal/length; rounded: distance), each followed by plain extra bits, and the end-of-block symbol costs bits too. Start from LZ77's output: eight literals and two back-references. A literal is its own symbol. The byte a becomes the symbol for a in the literal/length alphabet ; its number there is the byte's value, 97, in small print. All eight literals map this way. A back-reference becomes two symbols. Length 4 is short and common, so it has a symbol of its own in the same alphabet, number 258. A symbol's number is only its place in the alphabet: 258 means \"length 4\", not length 258. Distance 7 falls in the range \"7–8 back\" of the second, distance alphabet , so 1 extra bit follows to pick 7. (23, 12) becomes \"lengths 23–26\" with 2 extra bits, then \"9–12 back\" with 2 more. The block must end. Symbol 256, end-of-block, comes from the literal/length alphabet, so it sits among the literals and lengths and gets a code like them. Huffman codes turn symbols into bits. Each symbol is replaced by its code from its own alphabet. For now, take the codes as given: this block uses a set of codes fixed by the format, 8 bits per literal, 7 for the end and the short lengths, 5 per distance, and the next section says where they come from, along with the 3 bits at the start that tell the decoder which codes to use. Extra bits go out as they are, uncoded. That makes 103 bits, the number in the first figure."},{"id":"block-types","heading":"Blocks, and the fixed code","text":"The join took the fixed codes for granted, so here is where they come from. A decoder can read the bits only if it knows which codes the encoder used, and there is more than one sensible choice. So DEFLATE splits a stream into blocks, and each block says at its start how it is coded. A stream is a series of blocks. Each block starts with 3 header bits: a last-block bit, 1 if this is the last block, then 2 bits for the block type, which say where the codes come from. The format calls them BFINAL and BTYPE , and the bit walks use those names. Two bits allow four types, and only three are used: The values of BTYPE . Header fields are read lowest bit first, so the bits arrive in the opposite order to how the value is written; the bit walk shows why that matters. BTYPE Arrives as Block 00 0 , then 0 stored: raw bytes, no compression 01 1 , then 0 fixed Huffman codes, set by the RFC 10 0 , then 1 dynamic Huffman codes, sent at the start of the block 11 1 , then 1 reserved: invalid, and a decoder must reject the stream (zlib reports \"invalid block type\") The rest of this page uses the fixed codes, type 01: a table of codes set once by the RFC, so a block sends no table at all. Every literal from 0 to 143, which covers all of ASCII, gets 8 bits, and 144–255 get 9. End-of-block and the length symbols get 7 or 8, and every distance symbol gets 5. The join figure above used these codes, and the next section reads that block bit by bit. Dynamic codes, which a block fits to its own data, are the subject of part 2 . Go deeper: why are the fixed codes 7–9 bits long? It is the budget from the join again: a code of L bits uses up 2 − L of all possible bit patterns, and the shares can't add up to more than 1. The fixed code covers 288 symbols, two more than the 286 of the literal/length alphabet: 286 and 287 are never sent, but they fill out the code. Without knowing which symbols are common, spread evenly that is about log 2 288 ≈ 8.2 bits each. The RFC spends the budget exactly: Symbols Count Bits Share of the budget bytes 0–143 (all of ASCII) 144 8 144 / 256 = 0.5625 bytes 144–255 112 9 112 / 512 = 0.21875 256–279: end of block and short lengths 24 7 24 / 128 = 0.1875 280–287: long lengths (286 and 287 unused) 8 8 8 / 256 = 0.03125 total 288 1 The 7-bit codes for end-of-block and common lengths are paid for by making the upper half of the byte values, rare in text, cost 9 bits. Only a code fitted to the data can make a common symbol short, by making rare ones longer: that is what a dynamic block's code does."},{"id":"one-block","heading":"One real block, bit by bit","text":"Let's read the stream this page's encoder writes for the opening string, the 13 bytes 4b 4c 2a 4a 4c 4e 4c 01 51 0a 38 d8 00 , the way a decoder reads them. These are the bits after Huffman coding: each literal, length and distance appears as its code from the fixed code, not as a raw byte or number. It holds exactly the tokens of the window figure , so every bit below is one you have already seen produced in the join . One convention first, because it trips everyone up. A decoder takes the bits of each byte lowest first. So the block type 01, fixed, arrives as 1 then 0 , and a decoder that read those two bits the other way round would see 10 and try to read a dynamic block. Plain numbers, like the block type and the extra bits, come out right read lowest bit first, because that is how they were packed. Huffman codes are the exception: they are packed most significant bit first, because the decoder walks down the code from the root one bit at a time and needs the first step of the path first. The format reference has the whole rule. Here is the first byte, 4b , done by hand. In binary it is 01001011 . Read from its lowest bit, the right-hand end, it gives 1 1 0 1 0 0 1 0 : BFINAL = 1, then the block type's two bits, 1 then 0 , which is 01, then 10010 , the first five bits of a 's code. The next byte, 4c , supplies the last three, 001 . A code may run across a byte boundary; only the order of the bits matters. The walk below shows every bit already in reading order. 1 BFINAL = 1: last block 10 BTYPE = 01: fixed codes 10010001 'a', a literal (symbol 97) 10010010 'b', a literal (symbol 98) 10100010 'r', a literal (symbol 114) 10010001 'a', a literal (symbol 97) 10010011 'c', a literal (symbol 99) 10010001 'a', a literal (symbol 97) 10010100 'd', a literal (symbol 100) 0000010 length 4 (symbol 258) 00101 7–8 back (d5) 0 + 1 extra bit = 0: distance 7 01010000 ' ', a literal (symbol 32) 0001110 lengths 23–26 (symbol 270) 00 + 2 extra bits = 0: length 23 00110 9–12 back (d6) 11 + 2 extra bits = 3: distance 12 0000000 end of block (symbol 256) The block header. The last-block bit, BFINAL , is 1, so this is the last block. The two block-type bits, BTYPE , arrive as 1 0 , lowest first, so the type is binary 01: the fixed codes (see the block types ). For 35 bytes, a code fitted to the text would cost more to describe than it saves. Seven literals, 8 bits each. In the fixed code, bytes 0–143 are 00110000 plus the byte, so a (97) is 10010001 . (The fixed codes are handed out in order, shortest first. The 24 seven-bit codes take 0000000 to 0010111 , so the 8-bit ones start at 00110000 ; part 2 explains the rule.) That is no shorter than the raw byte, 01100001 : the fixed code isn't fitted to this text, so it gives every byte from 0 to 143 the same 8 bits ( why ). This block's whole saving comes from LZ77's two copies. A back-reference, (4, 7). Length 4 has a symbol of its own, 258, with no extra bits. Symbols 256–279 have the 7-bit codes, so each one's code is simply the symbol minus 256: 258 is 0000010 . The distance falls in the range 7–8 back, and its one extra bit, 0, picks 7. 13 bits stand for 4 bytes. Another literal: the space (32). The long, overlapping copy, (23, 12). The symbol for lengths 23–26, then 2 extra bits, 00 , that add 0: length 23. Then 9–12 back, plus 11 , 3: distance 12. 16 bits stand for 23 bytes. Only 12 bytes lie between the source and the destination, but the copy is 23 long. Watch the output , counting bytes from 0: the decoder copies one byte at a time, so when it reaches byte 24 it reads byte 12, which this same copy wrote 12 steps earlier. Every byte it reads already exists by the time it needs it. One thing this block can't show you: its extra bits, 0 , 00 and 11 , read the same either way round, so you can't see that they arrive lowest bit first. Part 2 has one that doesn't. End of block. Symbol 256 is seven zeros in the fixed code. That makes 103 bits, the total in the first figure; one more bit of padding"},{"id":"fixed-limits","heading":"Where the fixed code falls short","text":"The fixed code is fair to every byte, and that is its weakness: it can't know which bytes are common. For the 35-byte opening string that didn't matter, and the fixed block was the right choice. But take a longer text, a 381-byte paragraph of English about DEFLATE (one of the playground's examples, further down ). zlib compresses it with codes fitted to its own counts, built with the same algorithm as the abracadabra tree : Some literals in zlib's block for the paragraph: 233 literals, using 38 different bytes. Byte Times Fixed code Fitted code space 32 8 bits 3 bits e 20 8 bits 4 bits i 16 8 bits 4 bits s , t 15 each 8 bits 4 bits o 14 8 bits 4 bits k , u , v , x 1 each 8 bits 8 bits The common bytes drop to 3 or 4 bits, and the rare ones lose nothing. Across all 233 literals the fixed code spends 1,864 bits and the fitted code 1,108, about 4.8 bits a literal instead of 8. With the lengths and distances counted too, the data takes 2,375 bits with the fixed code and 1,521 with the fitted one. The fitted code isn't free: the decoder has to be told what it is, and for this block that costs 352 bits. Even so, with the 3 header bits the block comes to 1,876 bits instead of 2,378, about a fifth smaller. That is the case for dynamic codes, and part 2 is about them: how a block describes its code in a few hundred bits rather than thousands, by sending nothing but code lengths."},{"id":"answer","heading":"Back to the question","text":"Before the tally: the block is 177 bits smaller than the 280 plain bits. How many of those 177 come from removing repetition, and how many from removing skew? A rough split is enough. All of them come from repetition, and a little more. The two copies replace 216 plain bits with 29, a saving of 187. The literals save nothing: the fixed code gives every ASCII byte 8 bits, exactly what it cost before, so the skew of abracadabra is never used. The header and end-of-block cost 10 bits that plain bytes don't, and 187 − 10 = 177. If you gave skew a fair share, that was the reasonable model: Huffman coding does remove skew, but only with a code fitted to the text, and for 35 bytes describing one costs more than it saves. So how do you make abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra smaller without losing a letter? You remove its two kinds of waste, in order, and you can now account for every one of the 103 bits: Where the 13 bytes go, against the 280 bits of the plain text. Part of the block Bits Plain bits it replaces header: last-block bit and block type 3 0 7 literals, abracad 56 56 (4, 7), a copy of abra 13 32 the space, a literal 8 8 (23, 12), a copy of 23 bytes 16 184 end of block 7 0 total 103 280 All of the saving is repetition: two copies stand for 27 of the 35 bytes, in 29 bits. Skew doesn't help at all here: the fixed code gives every ASCII byte the same 8 bits it had before. Codes fitted to the string would save 59 bits on the data but cost 159 bits to describe ( part 2 writes that block out), which is why the block uses the fixed codes. And zlib's 15 bytes are the same story with a slightly worse parse: 16 bits worse . The same two questions explain any compression result you meet: is there repetition, and is there skew? English text has both, which is why the paragraph compresses well. A JPEG, or anything already compressed or encrypted, has neither left, which is why gzipping it gains nothing. PNG is the clever case: before DEFLATE runs, it filters each row of pixels, usually storing each byte as its difference from a neighbouring pixel. A smooth image then becomes mostly small numbers, and that is skew Huffman coding can use. How good is DEFLATE, then? RFC 1951, which defines it, dates from 1996. Newer formats such as Zstandard and Brotli use the same two ideas with longer windows and more sophisticated coding, and usually compress better. DEFLATE stays everywhere because every decoder already reads it."},{"id":"try-it","heading":"Try it","text":"You have seen both savings on one 35-byte string. Here they are on any text you like. Type anything, or pick an example. The tokens are what LZ77 found. The numbers show each saving on its own and both together: a code fitted to the bytes removes only skew, LZ77 with the fixed code removes only repetition, and the last does both. A fitted code's count includes the bits to describe it. Every count is the size of a real DEFLATE stream that this page's encoder writes, not an estimate. Text to compress 35 bytes abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra ×3 A paragraph One letter ×300 No repeats 280 bits as plain bytes 223 bits with a fitted code alone, no LZ77 103 bits with LZ77 and the fixed code 203 bits with LZ77 and a fitted code, 159 of them to describe it 13 bytes out, as a fixed block Write it as the smallest stored fixed dynamic 13 bytes ( 103 bits) as a fixed block: 4b 4c 2a 4a 4c 4e 4c 01 51 0a 38 d8 00 LZ77 replaced 27 of 35 bytes with 2 back-references, leaving 8 literals. Codes built for this text would take 159 bits to describe, more than they save, so the fixed codes win. For comparison, your browser's own DEFLATE ( CompressionStream ) wrote … bytes. literal byte back-reference (length, distance) Click a back-reference to see the bytes it copies. a b r a c a d (4,7) ␣ (23,12) The token stream as a table # Token Stands for At byte 1 a a 0 2 b b 1 3 r r 2 4 a a 3 5 c c 4 6 a a 5 7 d d 6 8 (4,7) abra 7 9 ␣ ␣ 11 10 (23,12) abracadabra abracadabra 12 Click any back-reference to see which earlier bytes it copies, and use Write it as to see the same tokens as each kind of block: the smallest is what an encoder would send, but every one decodes. The paragraph shows both savings clearly: LZ77 finds the repeated words, and the skew of English letters pays for describing a code fitted to it. One letter ×300 is almost all repetition. \"No repeats\" has nothing for LZ77 and too little text to pay for describing a code, so it comes out slightly larger than it went in. A question to try: pick the paragraph, then delete text from its end. Below what length does the smallest block switch from dynamic to fixed? Guess first. Optional: the answer From 69 bytes on, this page's encoder writes a dynamic block; at 68 and below, a fixed one. Describing a fitted code costs about 230 bits even for a short text, so it pays only once there are enough symbols to save that much. zlib, which chooses its tokens differently, switches at 74 ( part 2 )."},{"id":"serial","heading":"Why decoding is serial","text":"Everything so far has been about size. The price of that density is speed. The output has no separators and no index. A code's length is known only once it has been decoded, so the decoder cannot find where symbol n + 1 starts without decoding symbol n . Back-references add a second chain: a copy reads bytes that earlier tokens wrote. That is why a gzip file is decompressed by one core, front to back, however many cores the machine has. Getting around it, for example to decode on a GPU, means finding where blocks start without decoding what comes before, and resolving copies whose sources aren't decoded yet. A later series here takes that on; nothing on this page depends on it. Next in the series: How a DEFLATE block sends its own code , where a block fits a Huffman code to its own text and has to tell the decoder what that code is."},{"id":"terms","heading":"Terms used on this page","text":"These are defined here, for this page. DEFLATE's broader terms are in the site glossary : DEFLATE , LZ77 , Huffman code , canonical Huffman code , back-reference , sliding window , end-of-block and extra bits . Alphabet The fixed set of symbols a Huffman code is built over. DEFLATE uses three: literal/length and distance for the data, and the code-length alphabet, which part 2 uses to send a block's own code. How the two ideas join BFINAL The first bit of every block: 1 if it is the last block of the stream. Blocks, and the fixed code BTYPE The 2 bits after BFINAL that give the block type: 00 stored, 01 fixed Huffman codes, 10 dynamic Huffman codes. 11 is invalid. Like every plain number, they arrive lowest bit first. The values Distance alphabet The 30 symbols, d0–d29, that stand for back-reference distances from 1 to 32,768. Most stand for a range, picked out by extra bits. It has a Huffman code of its own. How the two ideas join Dynamic Huffman code Codes built for one block from that block's own symbol counts, and sent as code lengths at its start ( BTYPE 10). They don't change while the block is read: dynamic means per block, not adaptive. A dynamic block, bit by bit Fixed Huffman code Codes set once by the RFC, for any block with BTYPE 01, so the block never has to describe them: 8 or 9 bits per literal, 7 or 8 per length symbol, 5 per distance symbol. Why 7–9 bits Literal A byte that LZ77 sends as itself, because no match of 3 or more starts there. It becomes the literal/length symbol with the same value, 0–255. LZ77 Literal/length alphabet The 286 symbols 0–285: 0–255 for literal bytes, 256 for the end of the block, and 257–285 for ranges of lengths. One Huffman code covers them all, so a single code says whether a literal or a copy comes next. How the two ideas join Overlapping copy A back-reference whose length is greater than its distance, so it reads bytes it is itself writing. It works because the decoder copies one byte at a time; distance 1 simply repeats a byte. The bit walk shows one Prefix code A code in which no codeword is the start of another, so codes can be read back to back without separators. Every Huffman code is a prefix code. Huffman Stored block A block of up to 65,535 raw bytes ( BTYPE 00), after a byte-aligned length and its complement. Encoders use it when compressing would make the data bigger. Choosing a block type Symbol One element of an alphabet: what Huffman coding counts and gives a code. DEFLATE maps LZ77's tokens onto symbols: a literal is its own symbol, a back-reference becomes two plus extra bits. How the two ideas join Token One item of LZ77's output: a literal, or a back-reference (length, distance). Each becomes one or two symbols. LZ77"},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"RFC 1951, DEFLATE : the format itself, short and readable. Section 3.2 has the alphabets and the canonical code. RFC 1952, gzip and RFC 1950, zlib : the two wrappers, a few header bytes and a checksum around the same stream. Huffman (1952), \"A Method for the Construction of Minimum-Redundancy Codes\" : the original paper. Ziv and Lempel (1977), \"A Universal Algorithm for Sequential Data Compression\" : where LZ77 gets its name. zlib's algorithm.txt : how zlib's encoder chooses matches, which this page leaves out."}]},{"href":"docs/how-deflate-sends-its-code.html","title":"How a DEFLATE block sends its own code","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer · Compression How a DEFLATE block sends its own code A Huffman code fitted to the text saves bits only if the decoder can be told what it is, and telling it costs bits too. This page tries a table, a tree and a list of code lengths, and shows why DEFLATE sends only the lengths, even though for a tiny block the tree would be cheaper. To ship a code along with its data, send the least that determines it, in an order the receiver can predict. A DEFLATE block sends only each symbol's code length, in symbol order, and the decoder rebuilds exactly the same codes by a rule both sides know. It matters because the description is pure overhead: for a 381-byte paragraph, zlib's fitted code saves 854 bits on the data and describing it costs 352. The common misconception is that the Huffman tree travels with the data. It doesn't: no tree and no symbol names are sent. A series in three parts How DEFLATE compresses : the two savings, how they join, and a block read bit by bit. How a DEFLATE block sends its own code , this page: codes fitted to the data, and how a block describes them cheaply. DEFLATE format reference : every range, field and bit-order rule, for reading a stream or writing a decoder."},{"id":"recap","heading":"Where part 1 left off","text":"Part 1 built DEFLATE's two savings and read a block written with the fixed codes, which the format sets once, so a block never has to describe them. The one idea to bring from it: a code fitted to a block's own counts saves bits on the data, but the decoder has never seen those counts, so the block has to tell it the code. For a 381-byte paragraph of English, zlib's fitted code saved 854 bits on the data, and describing it cost 352 of them. Describe it carelessly and the code costs more than it saves. This page is about that description. First we fit a code to part 1's 35-byte example, abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra , so there is something concrete to send. Then we look for the cheapest way to send it: as a table, as a tree, and then the idea that makes DEFLATE's dynamic blocks affordable, sending nothing but code lengths. The rest of the page squeezes that list until, for the paragraph, it beats the tree, and then reads a whole dynamic block bit by bit."},{"id":"example-code","heading":"The example's own code","text":"Fitting a code pays for the paragraph. To follow one all the way through, here is the code fitted to the 35-byte example. Huffman coding never sees the raw letters: it counts the symbols that LZ77's output maps to ( the join ). After LZ77 the example is 10 tokens. Its 8 literals, its two length symbols and the end-of-block symbol make 11 symbols in the literal/length alphabet, 9 of them different: a appears 3 times, as a literal, and every other symbol once. The two distances belong to the distance alphabet, which gets a code of its own below. Notice that this is not the letters' tree from earlier. LZ77 has already replaced most of the a s with copies, so a is 3 of 11 symbols here, not 5 of 11, and gets 2 bits instead of 1. Count the symbols. The 8 literals, the two length symbols 258 and 270, and end-of-block. With the fixed code these 11 symbols cost 85 bits. Join the rarest, in pairs. Eight symbols tie at 1. Joining two at a time, lowest symbol number first, makes four nodes of 2: ␣ with b , c with d , r with end, 258 with 270. Again. The four nodes of 2 pair up into two nodes of 4. Now a is one of the two rarest. With 3 it is lighter than either 4, so it joins one of them to make 7. The last join makes the root, 11: every symbol. Read the codes. Label each left branch 0 and each right branch 1. A symbol's code is the path from the root to it: a is 10 , r is 000 , ␣ is 1100 . No code is the start of another, because every symbol is a leaf. Count the bits. A code's length is the symbol's depth in the tree: a has 2 bits; r , end, 258 and 270 have 3; ␣, b , c and d have 4. The 11 symbols now cost 34 bits instead of 85. As a table. The tree has done its job. What remains is each symbol with its code and its length, in the order of the tree's leaves. The next section starts from exactly this table. The distance code is simpler: d5 and d6 are used once each, so each gets 1 bit, against 5 in the fixed code. Add the 5 extra bits, which no code touches, and the example's data costs 34 + 2 + 5 = 41 bits, against 85 + 10 + 5 = 100 with the fixed code: 59 bits saved. But the decoder has never seen the example's counts, so it can't build the tree itself: the block has to tell it the code, and every bit spent on that comes out of those 59."},{"id":"canonical","heading":"Send lengths, not trees","text":"How should a block tell the decoder its code? The most literal way is a table. For each used symbol, write its number in 9 bits, enough for all 286 literal/length symbols, then its code's length in 4 bits so the decoder knows where the code ends, then the code itself. For the example that is 9 × (9 + 4) bits plus the codes themselves, 30 bits in all, 147 bits, and d5 and d6 add 2 × (5 + 4 + 1) = 20: 167 bits, plus something to say where the table ends. That is four times the 41 bits of data it describes, and nearly three times the 59 bits the code saves. Sending the tree is cheaper, because the codes are implied by its shape and need not be written out. Walk it from the root, left branch before right. At a branch write 0; at a leaf write 1, then the leaf's symbol number. The decoder rebuilds the same tree from those bits: a 0 means two subtrees follow, a 1 means a symbol follows. Against the example's tree , the walk starts 0 0 0 1 r : the root, the node of 4 on its left, the node of 2 on that one's left, then the leaf r , followed by its sibling, 1 end. Notice how little of the tree is tree. The 0s and 1s that give its shape are the thin slivers; the symbol names are most of the bar. A name is the symbol's number, 0–285, in 9 bits: a is symbol 97, 001100001 . It is neither a 's code nor the byte it decodes to. Each block is drawn as wide as the bits it costs. That is 111 bits for the example. Look at where they go: 20 describe the shape , and 91 name the symbols . Neither is what the decoder actually needs. The names. The tree needs them because its leaves come in tree order, so each leaf must say which symbol it is. A list in symbol order needs none, because the decoder counts as it reads: the first length is symbol 0's, the second symbol 1's, so the 98th is symbol 97's, which is a 's. Nothing in the stream says 97; the position does. Below is the whole trip, from that slot to the byte a . The shape. As the tie-break note in part 1 showed (when nodes tie, joining a different pair gives a different tree with the same total), many trees are equally good. Swap the two halves under the node of 7: a becomes 11 , and ␣, b , c and d become 1000 to 1011 . Every one of those codes changes, but no length does, so the data still costs 34 bits. The tree spends bits picking one code out of many that are all as good. So DEFLATE drops both, and this is the step the rest of the page builds on. It fixes one rule for turning code lengths into codes, called the canonical Huffman code , so no shape is sent. And it sends the lengths as a list in symbol order, so no symbol is named. Notice that the codes come from the order alone. Once the symbols are sorted by length and then by symbol number, each code is the previous one plus one, and gains a 0 on the right for each bit it grows longer. Start from the tree's codes, in the order of its leaves: r 000 , end 001 , 258 010 , 270 011 , a 10 , then ␣, b , c and d 1100 to 1111 . Throw the codes away. Keep only each symbol's length. This is all a DEFLATE block sends. Sort by length, and by symbol number within a length: a (97); then r (114), end (256), 258 and 270; then ␣ (32), b , c and d . The first code is all zeros, as long as the shortest length: a gets 00 . Count up, then shift. Add one to the last code ( 01 ), then append a 0 for the extra bit of length: r gets 010 . Within a length, count up: end 011 , 258 100 , 270 101 . Again for 4 bits. 101 + 1 is 110 , and one more bit makes ␣ 1100 ; then b 1101 , c 1110 , d 1111 . The decoder runs the same rules on the same lengths, so it gets the same codes without ever seeing a tree. These are not the tree's codes: a was 10 and is now 00 , and r was 000 and is now 010 . But every symbol keeps its length, so the 11 symbols still cost 34 bits. Counting up keeps the code prefix-free: only a 's code starts 00 , and only the 4-bit codes start 11 . The distance code works the same way: d5 and d6, 1 bit each, get 0 and 1 . These are the codes the example's dyna"},{"id":"dynamic-header","heading":"Sending a dynamic code","text":"A dynamic block must tell the decoder its codes before its data. Thanks to canonical codes , it only needs to send each symbol's code length: 286 literal/length symbols and 30 distance symbols, 316 lengths in all, each from 0 to 15. The question is how to send them cheaply, and it matters: every bit spent on the code comes out of the saving. For the paragraph, the fitted code saved 854 bits on the data, and sending it cost 352 of them. Here are four tries, each fixing the biggest waste in the one before, measured on two blocks: the worked example written as a dynamic block (the one walked through below ), and zlib's real block for the 381-byte paragraph."},{"id":"step-naive","heading":"1. Every length, 4 bits each","text":"Write all 316 lengths as plain 4-bit numbers, in symbol order. That is 1,264 bits, 158 bytes, in every dynamic block whatever it holds. The example's data is only 41 bits, so describing its code would cost 30 times as much as the data itself. Even the paragraph's data, 1,521 bits, would nearly double. It is also more than ten times the tree's 111 bits for the example: the tree pays about 11 bits for each symbol a block uses, but the list pays 4 bits for every symbol it could use. Steps 2 to 4 cut what the unused ones cost."},{"id":"step-counts","heading":"2. Say how many","text":"Any one block uses few of the symbols. So send two counts first, how many literal/length lengths follow and how many distance lengths (plain 5-bit numbers the format calls HLIT and HDIST , each stored as the count minus a fixed offset), and stop each list after its highest used symbol; the rest count as 0. Guess before you read on: the example uses only 11 of its 316 symbols. How much should cutting each list after its last used symbol save? Hardly anything. It barely helps: 1,122 bits for the example and 1,158 for the paragraph, against 1,264. Those are the lengths that remain, at 4 bits each, plus 10 bits for the two counts. The unused symbols are mostly in the middle of the alphabet, the byte values a text never contains, not at the end, so there is little tail to cut. The counts stay in the final scheme anyway: with no separators, they are how the decoder knows where each list ends. To shrink the list, step 3 has to deal with the zeros in the middle."},{"id":"dynamic-runs","heading":"Runs, and a code for the runs","text":""},{"id":"step-runs","heading":"3. Replace runs with one symbol","text":"Write the lengths with a small alphabet of 19 symbols instead: 0–15 for a length, 16 to repeat the previous length, and 17 and 18 for runs of zeros, each followed by plain extra bits that give the count. For now, write each of these symbols as a plain 5-bit number. A whole stretch of unused byte values becomes one symbol: the example's 262 unused literal/length symbols take just 7. The header drops to 146 bits for the example ( step 3 in the figure below ) and 443 for the paragraph. That is the big win, but the paragraph's header is still 443 bits. The code-length alphabet. Fixed by the format: the same in every dynamic block. Each range follows from its extra bits: 2 bits give 4 counts, 3 give 8, 7 give 128. Symbol Means Extra bits Count 0–15 a code length of 0 to 15 0 16 repeat the previous length 2 3–6 times 17 a run of zeros 3 3–10 18 a run of zeros 7 11–138 Runs start at 3 because a run of 1 or 2 is cheaper written out. Zeros get two run symbols, a short one and a long one, because they are by far the most common length. A run's extra bits are a plain number, so they arrive lowest bit first, and they count up from the shortest run the symbol allows. The example's first run is the 32 unused byte values 0–31: an 18, then the 7 extra bits 1010100 . Read backwards that is 0010101 , 21, and an 18's runs start at 11, so 11 + 21 = 32."},{"id":"step-coded","heading":"4. Huffman-code the code-length symbols","text":"Step 3 still spends 5 bits on every one of these code-length symbols, yet they are far from equally common. The paragraph's lengths take 78 of them: symbols 8 and 5 appear 14 times each and 0 appears 12 times, while 2 appears once and 16 not at all. That is the same skew Huffman coding removes from the data, one level down, so DEFLATE gives these symbols a Huffman code too: the code-length code , a Huffman code whose job is to send the other two codes. Its own 19 lengths go first, as plain 3-bit numbers. A dynamic block is therefore a stack of three layers, each written with the one below it: The three layers of a dynamic block, from the top. A decoder reads them from the bottom up. Layer Written with Size the data the literal/length and distance codes the whole block those two codes' lengths the code-length code up to 316 lengths the code-length code's lengths plain 3-bit numbers at most 19 × 3 = 57 bits It stops at three layers because the bottom one is so small: 57 bits at most, too little for yet another code to repay its own description. This is DEFLATE's actual scheme, and it costs 159 bits for the example ( step 4 in the figure below ) and 352 for the paragraph. For the paragraph, coding the code-length symbols saved another 91 bits, 443 down to 352. Ducky asks But the example went from 146 bits to 159. The last step made it bigger? Yes, by 13 bits: describing the small code took more than it saved on just 19 symbols. DEFLATE has no way to stop at step 3, which is one more reason tiny blocks are written with the fixed codes. Of the example's 159 bits, how many only say which symbols are unused: about a tenth, about a third, or more than half? Pick one. More than half: 95 of the 159. The 262 unused literal/length slots take 56 bits as runs of zeros, and marking d0–d4 unused takes 6. The code-length code's own 54-bit list spends 33 bits on its 11 zeros, 3 bits each, because that list has no code to compress it. A run makes each zero cheap, about a fifth of a bit here, but not free, and the list has a slot for every symbol a block could use. \"A tenth\" is the natural guess after step 3, because runs make zeros sound nearly free. Per zero they are; across hundreds of slots, plus a list with no code of its own, it adds up. These costs barely grow with the block, so on real text their share is small. And against the tree? For the example, the tree still wins, 111 bits to 159: a block with only 11 different symbols has too few names to be worth dropping. For the paragraph, which uses 46 literal/length symbols and 11 distances, the tree would cost 581 bits, 469 of them names, against 352 for the lengths. Real blocks run to thousands of symbols and use far more of the alphabet, so the gap only grows. Here are the four steps side by side, on the example's own list of 316 lengths, each drawn to scale against its 41 bits of data. Step through them and watch where the bits go. Notice what the four steps do to the same list: cutting the end of the long bar of zeros barely shortens it, and only collapsing the zeros into run symbols does. The bar is drawn to scale against a mark at the example's 41 bits of data: even at its best, the code costs more than the data it describes. Step 1: every slot, 4 bits. All 316 of the example's code lengths, in symbol order. Only the 11 marked slots are not zero. 1,264 bits. Step 2: say how many. HLIT and HDIST cut each list after its last used symbol, 270 and d6, so 38 slots go: 278 lengths at 4 bits, plus 10 bits for the two counts, make 1,122 bits. The zeros in the middle are still there. Step 3: runs. Each stretch of zeros collapses into one run symbol: 18 for 11–138 zeros, 17 for 3–10. Each used length becomes one symbol too. The grey wedges show which stretch of the old list each symbol replaced. Read each column down. Symbol sent is what goes in the stream; stands for is what it means (×32 is a run of 32 zeros, a is a 's length); extra bits give a run's count, read backwards ; bits is what it co"},{"id":"dynamic-block","heading":"The same tokens as a dynamic block","text":"Part 1 read the example as a fixed block, 103 bits. To see every part of a dynamic Huffman block, here are the same tokens written as one by the same encoder: it is the \"dynamic\" choice under Write it as . Dynamic means the codes are built for this one block, from its own counts, and sent at its start. They don't change while the block is read. The result first, so you can skip the walk: the dynamic block is 203 bits, against the fixed block's 103. Describing the codes takes 159 bits, and the codes save only 59 on the data ( the table at the end has every part). Ducky asks So zlib would never write this block. Why read one that loses? Because it is small enough to read every bit of, and a block that wins has the same parts in the same order, only longer: the paragraph's header has the same three counts, a code-length code and runs of zeros, in 352 bits instead of 159. The walk below reads all 26 bytes, 75 c6 31 15 00 00 08 42 c1 2a 56 fb 6a 02 fa 0f 3c 02 30 dd b1 e2 f8 30 e5 06 , bit by bit. It is there for when you want to read or write a dynamic block yourself; nothing later on this page depends on it. This is step 4 in full. It has the codes for the data, and the code-length code that sends their lengths. Keep the levels apart, because the walk below sends them bottom-up, the code-length code first: The three alphabets, and where the walk below defines and uses each one's code. The alphabets are the same in every dynamic block; the symbols used and their codes are this block's. Level Alphabet Symbols used in this block Their codes in this block Code defined in lines Code used in lines the data literal/length 9: a , b , r , c , d , ␣, 256, 258, 270 2, 3 or 4 bits 24–39 43–55 the data distance 2: d5, d6 1 bit each 40–42 51, 54 the lengths code-length (CL) 7: 1, 2, 3, 4 (the lengths above), 0, 17, 18 (for zeros) 2 to 5 bits 6–23 24–42 1 BFINAL = 1: last block 01 BTYPE: 10 = 2, dynamic 01110 HLIT: 01110 = 14, + 257 = 271 01100 HDIST: 00110 = 6, + 1 = 7 0111 HCLEN: 1110 = 14, + 4 = 18 000 CL symbol 16: 000 = 0, unused 110 CL symbol 17: 011 = 3 bits 010 CL symbol 18: 010 = 2 bits 101 CL symbol 0: 101 = 5 bits 000 CL symbol 8: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 7: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 9: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 6: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 10: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 5: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 11: 000 = 0, unused 010 CL symbol 4: 010 = 2 bits 000 CL symbol 12: 000 = 0, unused 010 CL symbol 3: 010 = 2 bits 000 CL symbol 13: 000 = 0, unused 101 CL symbol 2: 101 = 5 bits 000 CL symbol 14: 000 = 0, unused 001 CL symbol 1: 100 = 4 bits 10 +1010100 18: 32 zeros, 0–31 01 4: ␣ (32) gets 4 bits 10 +1010110 18: 64 zeros, 33–96 11111 2: a (97) gets 2 bits 01 4: b (98) gets 4 bits 01 4: c (99) gets 4 bits 01 4: d (100) gets 4 bits 10 +0100000 18: 13 zeros, 101–113 00 3: r (114) gets 3 bits 10 +1111111 18: 138 zeros, 115–252 110 +000 17: 3 zeros, 253–255 00 3: 256 (end) gets 3 bits 11110 0: 257 unused 00 3: 258 gets 3 bits 10 +0000000 18: 11 zeros, 259–269 00 3: 270 gets 3 bits 110 +010 17: 5 zeros, d0–d4 1110 1: d5 gets 1 bit 1110 1: d6 gets 1 bit 00 literal a (97) 1101 literal b (98) 010 literal r (114) 00 literal a (97) 1110 literal c (99) 00 literal a (97) 1111 literal d (100) 100 length symbol 258 → 4 0 +0 distance d5 → 7 1100 literal ␣ (32) 101 +00 length symbol 270 → 23 1 +11 distance d6 → 12 011 end of block (256) 00000 padding (not read) The block header. BFINAL is 1. BTYPE arrives as 0 , then 1 : binary 10, a dynamic block. Every code from here on is defined by the block itself. How many lengths follow. The code lengths come next as a bare list in symbol order: the first is symbol 0's, the second symbol 1's, with no symbol numbers and nothing to mark where the list ends and the data begins. So the block says first how long each list is. HLIT : 271 literal/length lengths, for symbols 0–270. 270 is the highest symbol this block uses, so the encoder stops there and leaves out 271–285. HDIST : 7 distance length"},{"id":"decoding","heading":"Decoding with only the lengths","text":"The round trip said what happens to one symbol: its slot, its code, its byte. This section is how a decoder does the middle step quickly, and nothing after it depends on it; if you are happy to trust that it can, skip to choosing a block type . The one thing to take from it: the decoder turns the lengths into a lookup table, then decodes each symbol with a single lookup. The middle step, from bits to a code, is where the work is. The codes are 2, 3 and 4 bits long, and nothing in the data marks where one ends, so the decoder can't simply cut the data into codes. It could read one bit at a time and check, after each, whether the bits so far make a code: up to 4 checks per symbol here, and up to 15 in a block with 15-bit codes. Instead it builds a table that answers both questions at once: given the next few bits, which symbol do they start with, and how many bits is its code? Here it is for the example, from start to finish: from the lengths the block sent, to its codes, to its table, and then the table at work on the example's own data. Notice that only the lengths on the left come from the block; the decoder makes everything else from them. A short code fills several table entries and a long one fills one, and the window always peeks at 4 bits but moves only as far as the entry says. The data bits are the example block's real ones, as the decoder reads them. Start from what the block sent: one length per symbol, in symbol order, 271 of them. The decoder knows each one's symbol by counting: the 98th is symbol 97, a , and it is 2. 262 of the 271 are 0, unused; they are folded into ranges here. Only 9 symbols have a code. Turn the lengths into codes, by the canonical rule : sort the 9 used symbols by length, then by symbol number, and count up. a , the only length 2, gets 00 ; the four 3s get 010 to 101 ; the four 4s get 1100 to 1111 . The encoder did the same, so these are its codes. Fill a 's entries. The longest code is 4 bits, so the table has one entry for every 4-bit pattern: 2 4 = 16. a 's code, 00 , is only 2 bits. Any 4 bits that start 00 begin with a , whatever the other two are, so a fills all four entries from 0000 to 0011 . Each one says: a , 2 bits. The longer codes fill fewer entries. Each 3-bit code fills two: r 0100 and 0101 , end 0110 and 0111 , 258 1000 and 1001 , 270 1010 and 1011 . Each 4-bit code fills one, ␣ to d , 1100 to 1111 . That makes 4 + 4 × 2 + 4 × 1 = 16: every entry is filled exactly once, so any 4 bits the decoder meets begin with exactly one code. Decode: peek at 4 bits. The data starts 0011 . Entry 0011 says a , 2 bits. The decoder outputs a and consumes only those 2 bits: the other two are the start of the next code. The window moves on by 2. The next 4 bits are 1101 : b , 4 bits. Output b , consume 4. 0100 : r , 3 bits. The decoder always peeks at 4 bits, and the entry says how far to move. It never needs to know in advance where a code ends. Four more lookups: 0011 a , 1110 c , 0011 a , 1111 d . Output so far: abracad . 1000 : 258, 3 bits. The last of those 4 bits isn't 258's: it was peeked at, not consumed. 258 is not a byte but a copy length, 4, so a distance comes next, looked up in the distance table, built the same way from the block's two distance lengths: 0 is d5, and its extra bit, 0 , makes the distance 7. The decoder copies abra from 7 bytes back: abracadabra . Ducky asks It always peeks at 4 bits. What if only 3 are left, at the very end of the stream? Then it looks up the 3 it has, with a 0 in place of the missing bit, and checks the entry. If the code fits in the real bits, as end-of-block's 3-bit 011 does, it decodes; if it doesn't, the stream really is cut short. zlib's decoder makes this check on every lookup: is the entry's length no more than the bits it holds? So a decoder builds its lookup tables itself, at the start of every dynamic block, from the lengths the block sends. It builds one for literals and lengths and one for distances, and, as the bit walk showed, one mor"},{"id":"choosing","heading":"Choosing a block type","text":"An encoder now has a choice for every block, and it matters. The example's dynamic block is twice the size of its fixed one, while the paragraph's dynamic block is a fifth smaller than its fixed one. And for data with no repetition or skew, such as random bytes, both codes make things worse: the fixed code spends 8 or 9 bits on every byte. That is what the third type, the stored block, is for: it copies the bytes as they are. Here are the three side by side. Stored (00) No compression: up to 65,535 raw bytes after a 4-byte length and its complement. Caps the cost of incompressible data at about 5 bytes per 64 KiB: the 3 header bits, padded to a byte, then the 4-byte length No saving at all Fixed Huffman (01) Codes set by the RFC: 8 or 9 bits for a literal, 7 or 8 for a length, 5 for a distance. No table to send Wins on short inputs Ignores this data's skew Dynamic Huffman (10) Codes built for this block's own counts, sent as code lengths at the start of the block. Removes the skew too Usually wins once a block holds more than a few dozen bytes of text Describing the code costs tens of bytes Any input can be written as any of the three, and every one is valid: a decoder accepts whichever it gets. Which one wins is a sum, not a rule. The encoder works out the exact size of each kind for every block (zlib ends a block after about 16,000 symbols) and writes the smallest. Tiny blocks come out fixed because describing a code costs more than it saves: cut the paragraph in the chart down to its first few dozen bytes, and zlib 1.2.12 switches from fixed to dynamic once the text is 74 bytes long. Input Stored Fixed Dynamic zlib -9 abracadabra ×3 (35 B) 114.3 37.1 74.3 42.9 A paragraph (381 B) 101.3 78.2 62.2 61.7 Random bytes (1 KiB) 100.5 105.6 105.2 100.5 Each input has a different winner. The short string can't pay for a table; the paragraph can; random bytes can't be compressed at all, so storing them is cheapest. The first three columns are the same tokens written as each block type; zlib picks per block. Ducky asks zlib -9 is zlib trying its hardest, yet on the example it loses to the fixed block, 42.9% to 37.1%. Is the chart wrong? No. zlib also wrote a fixed block, but from different tokens. It writes a , copies only bra , and later writes ␣ and a before a 22-byte copy: two more literals than this page's tokens, 119 bits instead of 103, so 15 bytes instead of 13. Level 9 searches harder for matches, but how it chooses among them is a rule of thumb, not a proof of the fewest bits."},{"id":"what-it-costs","heading":"What a block's code costs","text":"Back to the paragraph from the start . Its fitted code saves 854 bits on the data, and the description that makes that possible costs 352: three counts and the code-length code's own lengths, then the 78 code-length symbols that spell out the lengths. Sent as a tree, the same code would cost 581 bits; as a plain list of 316 lengths, 1,264, more than the whole saving. Each step on this page is what turned a code that doesn't pay into one that does. The pattern is worth keeping, because it turns up wherever a format has to ship a model along with its data: send the least that determines the model (lengths, not codes), in an order the receiver can predict (position, not names), and compress the description with the same tool, one level down , until it is too small to repay another level. One check before you go, without scrolling up. A code covers four symbols, A, B, C and D in that order, with lengths A 2, B 1, C 3 and D 3. What does a block send to describe it? Which codes does the decoder rebuild, and what do the bits 100111110 decode to? And what are the three layers of a dynamic block's header, each written with what? Answer on paper, then open the answer and compare. Check your answer The block sends the lengths 2, 1, 3, 3, in symbol order, and nothing else: no names and no shape. Sorted by length, then symbol, the order is B, A, C, D. B is first and shortest, so it gets 0 . Add one and append a 0: A gets 10 . Again: C gets 110 , and D 111 . So 100111110 is 10 0 111 110 : A, B, D, C. If you gave A 0 because it comes first, you skipped the sort by length. The layers, top down: the data, written with the literal/length and distance codes; those codes' lengths, written with the code-length code, runs of zeros included; and the code-length code's own lengths, as plain 3-bit numbers in a fixed order. It stops there because the bottom layer is at most 57 bits. DEFLATE's own limit is per block. Every dynamic block sends a complete new code, even when the previous block's would do. For 16 KB of English text, describing the code takes 718 bits, about 1.3% of the block, so this matters little; for small blocks it is most of the block, which is why they stay fixed. Later formats can reuse a table across blocks, as Zstandard does . Next in the series: the DEFLATE format reference , with every range, field and bit-order rule in one place."},{"id":"terms","heading":"Terms used on this page","text":"These are defined here, for this page. DEFLATE's broader terms are in the site glossary : DEFLATE , LZ77 , Huffman code , canonical Huffman code , back-reference , sliding window , end-of-block and extra bits . Alphabet The fixed set of symbols a Huffman code is built over. DEFLATE uses three: literal/length and distance for the data, and the code-length alphabet, which part 2 uses to send a block's own code. How the two ideas join Code length The number of bits in a symbol's Huffman code. 0 means the symbol has no code. A dynamic block sends only the lengths; the codes follow from them by the canonical rule. Send lengths, not trees Code-length code The small Huffman code a dynamic block uses to write down its code lengths. Its alphabet has 19 symbols: 0–15 are lengths, 16 repeats the previous length, and 17 and 18 are runs of zeros. CL is short for code length. Huffman-code the code-length symbols Dynamic Huffman code Codes built for one block from that block's own symbol counts, and sent as code lengths at its start ( BTYPE 10). They don't change while the block is read: dynamic means per block, not adaptive. A dynamic block, bit by bit Fixed Huffman code Codes set once by the RFC, for any block with BTYPE 01, so the block never has to describe them: 8 or 9 bits per literal, 7 or 8 per length symbol, 5 per distance symbol. Why 7–9 bits HCLEN 4 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many code-length-code lengths follow, minus 4. Those lengths come in a fixed order, so the count can cut off the usually-unused ones at the end. Why that order HDIST 5 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many distance code lengths follow, minus 1. In the bit walk HLIT 5 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many literal/length code lengths follow, minus 257. The lengths come as a bare list in symbol order, so the count says where the list ends. In the bit walk Literal/length alphabet The 286 symbols 0–285: 0–255 for literal bytes, 256 for the end of the block, and 257–285 for ranges of lengths. One Huffman code covers them all, so a single code says whether a literal or a copy comes next. How the two ideas join Prefix code A code in which no codeword is the start of another, so codes can be read back to back without separators. Every Huffman code is a prefix code. Huffman Stored block A block of up to 65,535 raw bytes ( BTYPE 00), after a byte-aligned length and its complement. Encoders use it when compressing would make the data bigger. Choosing a block type Symbol One element of an alphabet: what Huffman coding counts and gives a code. DEFLATE maps LZ77's tokens onto symbols: a literal is its own symbol, a back-reference becomes two plus extra bits. How the two ideas join"},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"RFC 1951, DEFLATE : the format itself, short and readable. Section 3.2 has the alphabets and the canonical code. RFC 1952, gzip and RFC 1950, zlib : the two wrappers, a few header bytes and a checksum around the same stream. Huffman (1952), \"A Method for the Construction of Minimum-Redundancy Codes\" : the original paper. Ziv and Lempel (1977), \"A Universal Algorithm for Sequential Data Compression\" : where LZ77 gets its name. zlib's algorithm.txt : how zlib's encoder chooses matches, which this page leaves out."}]},{"href":"docs/how-deflate-works.html","title":"How DEFLATE works","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer · Compression How DEFLATE works The format inside gzip, zlib, PNG and ZIP is two simple ideas stacked on top of each other. First remove the repetition, then remove the skew. This page builds each one up, step by step, from a single 35-byte string. DEFLATE makes two separate savings. LZ77 replaces each repeat with a short (length, distance) pointer back to an earlier copy. Huffman coding then gives common symbols short bit patterns and rare ones long patterns. It matters because nearly everything uses it: gzip and zlib, PNG images, ZIP files, and Content-Encoding: gzip on the web. The common misconception is that the Huffman tree travels with the data. It doesn't. Only the code lengths are sent, and the codes are rebuilt from them by counting."},{"id":"question","heading":"A question to start","text":"How do you make abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra smaller without losing a single letter? Stored as plain bytes it takes 35 bytes, or 280 bits. Two things about it are wasteful. The same 11 letters appear three times, and the letter a makes up almost half of the text while c and d appear once per word. A good compressor should exploit both. Guess before you read on: compressed as a gzip file by zlib at its best setting (level 9), how many bytes do these 35 take? 33 bytes. That looks like almost no saving, but 18 of those bytes are the gzip wrapper: a 10-byte header, then a CRC-32 and the original length (8 bytes) at the end. The DEFLATE stream inside is 15 bytes , 43% of the input. By the end of this page you will have taken it apart bit by bit, and seen an encoder that does it in 13."},{"id":"two-ideas","heading":"Two ideas, stacked","text":"DEFLATE runs two stages, and each removes a different kind of waste. LZ77 turns the bytes into a shorter list of tokens : a literal byte, or an instruction to copy earlier bytes. Huffman coding then writes each token with as few bits as its frequency allows. literal byte back-reference (length, distance) Huffman code output bits Notice that the two savings are independent. The token count falls because of repetition (35 bytes, 10 tokens); the bits per token fall because of skew. These four colours keep the same meaning in every figure below. The two stages are independent, and each is useful alone. A file of random English words has little exact repetition but plenty of skew. A file of one byte repeated is all repetition. DEFLATE needs both because real data has both."},{"id":"lz77","heading":"LZ77: say it once, then point back","text":"Read the input left to right. Keep everything already read in a sliding window (DEFLATE's holds the last 32,768 bytes). At each position, look for the longest run of upcoming bytes that already appears in the window. If it is at least 3 bytes long, emit a back-reference : copy length bytes, starting distance bytes back. Otherwise emit the byte itself as a literal. Watch the second copy : it starts 12 bytes back but is 23 bytes long, so it reads bytes that the copy itself is still writing. Start with nothing. The cursor is at the first byte and the window behind it is empty, so there is nothing to point back to. Seven literals. None of a b r a c a d starts a run of 3 bytes seen before, so each one drops out of the input as a literal token. The window grows behind the cursor. A match. The next four bytes, abra , already appear 7 bytes back. Any run of 3 or more is worth replacing, and this one is 4 long. Emit (4, 7). The four bytes collapse into one token: copy 4 bytes from 7 back . Four letters cost one token. The space is new , so it goes out as a literal. A long match that overlaps. Everything that is left, 23 bytes, repeats what started 12 bytes back. Only 12 bytes lie between the source and the cursor, so the copy runs into its own output. Emit (23, 12). The decoder copies one byte at a time, so by the time it needs byte 13 of the copy, it has already written it. 35 bytes are now 10 tokens. Overlap is not a corner case. It is how LZ77 encodes runs: aaaaaaaa is the literal a followed by (7, 1) , \"copy 7 bytes from 1 back\"."},{"id":"huffman","heading":"Huffman: short codes for common symbols","text":"LZ77 has removed the repetition, but it leaves every literal at a full 8 bits: the common a costs as much as the rare d . Eight bits per letter is fair only if every letter is equally likely. In abracadabra the counts are a×5, b×2, r×2, c×1 and d×1, so a should get the shortest code. Huffman's algorithm finds the best codes for known counts, and it is greedy: repeatedly join the two rarest things. Here the symbols are the letters themselves, to keep the tree small. DEFLATE runs the same algorithm on the output of LZ77 instead, as the join below shows: on counts of literals, lengths and distances, not of raw bytes. Notice that a sits one step from the root and gets a 1-bit code, while the four rare letters share the bottom row with 3-bit codes. No code is the start of another, because every letter is a leaf. Count each letter. At 8 bits a letter, the 11 letters of abracadabra cost 88 bits. Line them up, rarest first. Each letter is now a leaf, weighted by its count. Join the two rarest: c and d, 1 each, become a node of weight 2. Again. Three things now weigh 2: b, r and the new node. Join any two. Here b and r make 4. Again: 2 and 4 make 6. Only a (5) and this node are left. The last join makes the root, 11, which is every letter. The common a joined last, so it ends up nearest the root. Read the codes. Label every left branch 0 and every right branch 1. A letter's code is the path from the root to it: a is 0 , c 100 , d 101 , b 110 , r 111 . Count the bits. A code's length is the letter's depth in the tree: a has 1 bit, the others 3. Weighted by the counts, the total falls from 88 bits to 23. As a table. The tree has done its job. What remains is each letter with its code and its length, back in the table it started from. At the second join, three nodes tied at weight 2. Join a different pair and you get a different tree. Does the total change? No: it is still 23 bits. Join c+d with b instead, and the code lengths become a 1, r 2, b 3, c 4 and d 4. The tree is different, but 5×1 + 2×2 + 2×3 + 1×4 + 1×4 = 23 as well. Huffman's algorithm gives an optimal code, not the optimal code. That ambiguity is what DEFLATE removes when a block sends its own code, further on ."},{"id":"joining","heading":"How the two ideas join","text":"The two tools don't fit together yet. Huffman coding works on a known alphabet of symbols, like the five letters of abracadabra , and counts how often each one appears. LZ77's output is not like that: it mixes literal bytes with back-references, and a back-reference is a pair of numbers, a length up to 258 and a distance up to 32,768. Before Huffman coding can start, every token has to become symbols from a fixed alphabet. That translation is the join. The two stages meet once per block, always in this order: LZ77 turns the block's bytes into tokens: literals and (length, distance) copies. Map every token to symbols from two alphabets, described below. This is the join. Count the symbols and build two Huffman codes from the counts: one for literals and lengths, one for distances. Write the block: its header (for a dynamic block, including the two codes), then each token's symbols as Huffman codes, each followed by its extra bits, then the end-of-block code. So Huffman coding never sees the raw bytes, or the tokens themselves. It counts and codes the symbols LZ77's output maps to, while the extra bits travel alongside, uncoded. The join is therefore a choice of alphabet for everything LZ77 can produce. The obvious alphabet would give every possible value its own symbol: b0 – b255 for the bytes, eob , w3 – w258 for the lengths and d1 – d32768 for the distances, 33,281 symbols in one code. DEFLATE does not do that. Its alphabets are drawn below, all 316 symbols. Pick something LZ77 can produce and see which symbol it becomes. LZ77 gives a byte end of block a length a distance Value 23 Not DEFLATE: one symbol per value w23 one of 33,281 symbols DEFLATE 270 + extra bits 0 Symbol 270 of the literal/length alphabet stands for lengths 23&#8288;–&#8288;26. 2 extra bits after it give the offset: 23 + 0 = 23. Literal/length alphabet : 286 symbols, one Huffman code bytes 0–255 256 257 258 259 260 261 262 263 264 265 266 267 268 269 270 271 272 273 274 275 276 277 278 279 280 281 282 283 284 285 Distance alphabet : 30 symbols, its own Huffman code d0 d1 d2 d3 d4 d5 d6 d7 d8 d9 d10 d11 d12 d13 d14 d15 d16 d17 d18 d19 d20 d21 d22 d23 d24 d25 d26 d27 d28 d29 Symbol 270 covers 4 lengths. The extra bits say which one: 23 +0 24 +1 25 +2 26 +3 The two changes, one at a time: A length or distance is a range symbol plus extra bits. Length 23 is not a symbol of its own. It is symbol 270, which means \"a length from 23 to 26\", followed by 2 plain extra bits, 00 , that pick 23. The extra bits are written as they are, not Huffman-coded. Short lengths (3–10) and distances (1–4) still get exact symbols, because they are the most common. Distances are coded separately. The bytes, the end of block and the length ranges share the first alphabet, so one code tells the decoder whether a literal or a copy comes next. A distance always follows a length, so the decoder always knows to read it with the second code."},{"id":"join-example","heading":"The join, token by token","text":"Here is the mapping applied to the ten tokens from the window figure , and then the codes that turn the symbols into bits. Notice that the middle row is all Huffman coding ever sees. A back-reference becomes two symbols from two alphabets (square: literal/length; rounded: distance), each followed by plain extra bits, and the end-of-block symbol costs bits too. Start from LZ77's output: eight literals and two back-references. A literal is its own symbol. The byte a (97) is symbol 97 of the literal/length alphabet , which runs from 0 to 285. All eight literals map this way. A back-reference becomes two symbols. Length 4 is symbol 258 of the same alphabet. Distance 7 is code d5 of a second, distance alphabet ; d5 covers distances 7–8, so 1 extra bit follows to pick 7. (23, 12) becomes 270, covering lengths 23–26, with 2 extra bits, then d6, covering 9–12, with 2 more. The block must end. Symbol 256, end-of-block, comes from the literal/length alphabet, so it sits among the literals and lengths and gets a code like them. Huffman codes turn symbols into bits. Each symbol is replaced by its code from its own alphabet's table. This block uses the fixed tables: 8 bits per literal, 7 for symbols 256–279, 5 per distance code. Extra bits go out as they are, uncoded. With the 3 header bits that is 103 bits, the number in the first figure."},{"id":"two-alphabets","heading":"Every range, and the decoder's loop","text":"The full mapping, for when you need the exact ranges: How LZ77's output maps onto DEFLATE's two alphabets. Where a symbol covers a range, plain extra bits follow it to pick the exact value; they are not Huffman-coded. LZ77 gives Symbol Alphabet Extra bits In the example a literal byte, 0–255 0–255, the byte's own value literal/length 0 a (byte 97) → 97 the end of the block 256 literal/length 0 256 , once at the end a length, 3–10 257–264, one each literal/length 0 length 4 → 258 a length, 11–257 265–284, each covering 2–32 lengths literal/length 1–5 length 23 → 270 ( 23–26 ) + 2 bits a length of 258 285 literal/length 0 a distance, 1–4 d0–d3, one each distance 0 a distance, 5–32,768 d4–d29, each covering 2–8,192 distances distance 1–13 distance 7 → d5 ( 7–8 ) + 1 bit; 12 → d6 ( 9–12 ) + 2 bits The decoder's inner loop runs the join backwards. Its two tables are the two alphabets: while True: sym = read_code(lit_len_table) # literal, length or end if sym < 256: out.append(sym) # a literal byte elif sym == 256: break # end of block else: i = sym - 257 length = LENGTH_BASE[i] + read_bits(LENGTH_EXTRA[i]) d = read_code(distance_table) # always follows a length distance = DIST_BASE[d] + read_bits(DIST_EXTRA[d]) for _ in range(length): # byte by byte: may overlap out.append(out[-distance])"},{"id":"block-types","heading":"Blocks, and the fixed code","text":"The join figure turned symbols into bits with \"the fixed tables\", taking them for granted. But a decoder can read the bits only if it knows which codes the encoder used, and there is more than one sensible choice. So DEFLATE splits a stream into blocks, and each block says at its start how it is coded. A stream is a series of blocks. Each block starts with 3 header bits: BFINAL (1 if this is the last block) and the 2-bit BTYPE , which says where the codes come from. Two bits allow four values, and only three are used: The values of BTYPE . Header fields are read lowest bit first, so the bits arrive in the opposite order to how the value is written. BTYPE Arrives as Block 00 0 , then 0 stored: raw bytes, no compression 01 1 , then 0 fixed Huffman codes, set by the RFC 10 0 , then 1 dynamic Huffman codes, sent at the start of the block 11 1 , then 1 reserved: invalid, and a decoder must reject the stream (zlib reports \"invalid block type\") The rest of this half of the page uses the fixed codes ( BTYPE 01): a table of codes set once by the RFC, so a block sends no table at all. Every literal from 0 to 143, which covers all of ASCII, gets 8 bits, and 144–255 get 9. End-of-block and the length symbols get 7 or 8, and every distance symbol gets 5. The join figure above used these codes, and so did zlib for the opening string; the next section reads its block bit by bit. Dynamic codes, which a block fits to its own data, come after that. Go deeper: why are the fixed codes 7–9 bits long? A prefix code has a budget. A code of L bits uses up 2 − L of all possible bit patterns, since nothing else may start with it, and the shares can't add up to more than 1. The fixed code must cover 288 symbols without knowing which are common, and spread evenly that is about log 2 288 ≈ 8.2 bits each. The RFC spends the budget exactly: Symbols Count Bits Share of the budget bytes 0–143 (all of ASCII) 144 8 144 / 256 = 0.5625 bytes 144–255 112 9 112 / 512 = 0.21875 256–279: end of block and short lengths 24 7 24 / 128 = 0.1875 280–287: long lengths (286 and 287 unused) 8 8 8 / 256 = 0.03125 total 288 1 The 7-bit codes for end-of-block and common lengths are paid for by making the upper half of the byte values, rare in text, cost 9 bits. Only a code fitted to the data can make a common symbol short, by making rare ones longer: that is what a dynamic block's code does."},{"id":"one-block","heading":"One real block, bit by bit","text":"These are the bits after Huffman coding: each literal, length and distance appears as its code from the fixed table, not as a raw byte or number. Here is what the reference zlib (version 1.2.12) actually wrote at level 9 for the opening string: the 15 bytes 4b 4c 2a 4a 4c 4e 4c 49 04 52 0a 89 d8 d9 00 , read the way a decoder reads them. Bits are taken from each byte lowest first. Huffman codes are read one bit at a time, most significant first; header fields and extra bits are plain numbers, lowest bit first. 1 BFINAL = 1: last block 10 BTYPE = 01: fixed codes 10010001 literal 97 'a' (byte 01100001) 10010010 literal 98 'b' 10100010 literal 114 'r' 10010001 literal 97 'a' 10010011 literal 99 'c' 10010001 literal 97 'a' 10010100 literal 100 'd' 10010001 literal 97 'a' 0000001 length symbol 257: length 3 00101 distance code 5: distances 7–8 0 + 1 extra bit = 0: distance 7 01010000 literal 32 ' ' 10010001 literal 97 'a' 0001101 length symbol 269: lengths 19–22 11 + 2 extra bits = 3: length 22 00110 distance code 6: distances 9–12 11 + 2 extra bits = 3: distance 12 0000000 end of block (symbol 256) The block header. BFINAL is 1, so this is the last block. The next two bits arrive as 1 0 , lowest first, so BTYPE is binary 01: the fixed Huffman codes (see the BTYPE values ). For 35 bytes, a dynamic table would cost more than it saves. Eight literals, 8 bits each. In the fixed code, bytes 0–143 are 00110000 plus the byte, so a (97) is 10010001 . That is no shorter than the raw byte, 01100001 : the fixed code isn't fitted to this text, so it gives every byte from 0 to 143 the same 8 bits ( why ). This block's whole saving comes from LZ77's two copies. Notice also that zlib wrote the second a of abra as a literal. A back-reference, (3, 7). Length symbol 257 means 3, with no extra bits. Distance code 5 covers 7–8, and its one extra bit, 0, picks 7. zlib copied bra where the window figure copied abra : 13 bits for 3 bytes. Two more literals: the space (32) and another a . The long, overlapping copy, (22, 12). Symbol 269 covers lengths 19–22, and the extra bits 11 add 3: length 22. Distance code 6 covers 9–12, plus 3: distance 12. 16 bits stand for 22 bytes. Only 12 bytes lie between the source and the destination, but the copy is 22 long. Watch the output : the decoder copies one byte at a time, so when it reaches byte 25 it reads byte 13, which this same copy wrote 12 steps earlier. Every byte it reads already exists by the time it needs it. End of block. Symbol 256 is seven zeros in the fixed code. That makes 119 bits; one more bit of padding fills the 15th byte. Replay the copy The decoder's output for zlib's stream. A copy always reads to the left of where it writes. Striped bytes are read by the copy that wrote them: the copy starts 12 bytes back but runs for 22, so from byte 25 on it reads its own output. Why is zlib's version bigger than the figure's? zlib wrote 119 bits; the parse in the window figure needs 103. DEFLATE fixes how to decode , not how to encode: any token stream that rebuilds the input is valid. zlib's hash chains use position 0 as their \"no match\" marker, so zlib never copies from the very first byte of a stream. Both of its copies here would have started there, so each starts one byte later, after an extra literal a : 16 bits lost. On real files, zlib's smarter match choices win by far more. Other builds choose differently again: Chromium's fork of zlib, in Chrome and in Node's official binaries, writes 17 bytes for this string."},{"id":"fixed-limits","heading":"Where the fixed code falls short","text":"The fixed code is fair to every byte, and that is its weakness: it can't know which bytes are common. For the 35-byte opening string that didn't matter, and zlib was right to use it. But take a longer text, a 381-byte paragraph of English about DEFLATE (one of the playground's examples, further down ). zlib compresses it with codes fitted to its own counts, built with the same algorithm as the abracadabra tree : Some literals in zlib's block for the paragraph: 233 literals, using 38 different bytes. Byte Times Fixed code Fitted code space 32 8 bits 3 bits e 20 8 bits 4 bits i 16 8 bits 4 bits s , t 15 each 8 bits 4 bits o 14 8 bits 4 bits k , u , v , x 1 each 8 bits 8 bits The common bytes drop to 3 or 4 bits, and the rare ones lose nothing. Across all 233 literals the fixed code spends 1,864 bits and the fitted code 1,108, about 4.8 bits a literal instead of 8. With the lengths and distances counted too, the data takes 2,375 bits with the fixed code and 1,521 with the fitted one. The fitted code isn't free: the decoder has to be told what it is, and for this block that costs 352 bits. Even so, the block comes to 1,876 bits instead of 2,378, about a fifth smaller. That is the case for dynamic codes. To see one built from start to finish, the next section fits a code to the example; the sections after it work out how a block sends its code cheaply."},{"id":"example-code","heading":"The example's own code","text":"Fitting a code pays for the paragraph. To follow one all the way through, here is the code fitted to the 35-byte example. Huffman coding never sees the raw letters: it counts the symbols that LZ77's output maps to ( the join ). After LZ77 the example is 10 tokens plus the end-of-block symbol: 11 symbols, 9 of them different. a appears 3 times, as a literal; every other symbol appears once. Notice that this is not the letters' tree from earlier. LZ77 has already replaced most of the a s with copies, so a is 3 of 11 symbols here, not 5 of 11, and gets 2 bits instead of 1. Count the symbols. The 8 literals, the two length symbols 258 and 270, and end-of-block. With the fixed code these 11 symbols cost 85 bits. Join the rarest, in pairs. Eight symbols tie at 1. Joining two at a time, lowest symbol number first, makes four nodes of 2: ␣ with b , c with d , r with end, 258 with 270. Again. The four nodes of 2 pair up into two nodes of 4. Now a is one of the two rarest. With 3 it is lighter than either 4, so it joins one of them to make 7. The last join makes the root, 11: every symbol. Read the codes. Label each left branch 0 and each right branch 1. A symbol's code is the path from the root to it: a is 10 , r is 000 , ␣ is 1100 . No code is the start of another, because every symbol is a leaf. Count the bits. A code's length is the symbol's depth in the tree: a has 2 bits; r , end, 258 and 270 have 3; ␣, b , c and d have 4. The 11 symbols now cost 34 bits instead of 85. As a table. The tree has done its job. What remains is each symbol with its code and its length, in the order of the tree's leaves. The next section starts from exactly this table. The distance code is simpler: d5 and d6 are used once each, so each gets 1 bit. This code takes the example's symbols from 85 bits to 34. But the decoder has never seen the example's counts, so it can't build the tree itself: the block has to tell it the code, and every bit spent on that comes out of the 51 bits saved."},{"id":"canonical","heading":"Send lengths, not trees","text":"How should a block tell the decoder its code? The most literal way is a table. For each used symbol, write its number in 9 bits, enough for all 286 literal/length symbols, then its code's length in 4 bits so the decoder knows where the code ends, then the code itself. For the example that is 9 × (9 + 4) bits plus the 30 bits of the codes, 147 bits, and d5 and d6 add 2 × (5 + 4 + 1) = 20: 167 bits, plus something to say where the table ends. That is four times the 41 bits of data it describes, and more than three times the 51 bits the code saves. Sending the tree is cheaper, because the codes are implied by its shape and need not be written out. Walk it from the root, left branch before right. At a branch write 0; at a leaf write 1, then the leaf's symbol number. The decoder rebuilds the same tree from those bits: a 0 means two subtrees follow, a 1 means a symbol follows. Against the example's tree , the walk starts 0 0 0 1 r : the root, the node of 4 on its left, the node of 2 on that one's left, then the leaf r , followed by its sibling, 1 end. Notice how little of the tree is tree. The 0s and 1s that give its shape are the thin slivers; the symbol names are most of the bar. A name is the symbol's number, 0–285, in 9 bits: a is symbol 97, 001100001 . It is neither a 's code nor the byte it decodes to. Each block is drawn as wide as the bits it costs. That is 111 bits for the example. Look at where they go: 20 describe the shape , and 91 name the symbols . Neither is what the decoder actually needs. The names. The tree needs them because its leaves come in tree order, so each leaf must say which symbol it is. A list in symbol order needs none, because the decoder counts as it reads: the first length is symbol 0's, the second symbol 1's, so the 98th is symbol 97's, which is a 's. Nothing in the stream says 97; the position does. Below is the whole trip, from that slot to the byte a . The shape. As the tie-break question showed, many trees are equally good. Swap the two halves under the node of 7: a becomes 11 , and ␣, b , c and d become 1000 to 1011 . Every one of those codes changes, but no length does, so the data still costs 34 bits. The tree spends bits picking one code out of many that are all as good. So DEFLATE drops both. It fixes one rule for turning code lengths into codes, called the canonical Huffman code , so no shape is sent. And it sends the lengths as a list in symbol order, so no symbol is named. Notice that the codes come from the order alone. Once the symbols are sorted by length and then by symbol number, each code is the previous one plus one, and gains a 0 on the right for each bit it grows longer. Start from the tree's codes, in the order of its leaves: r 000 , end 001 , 258 010 , 270 011 , a 10 , then ␣, b , c and d 1100 to 1111 . Throw the codes away. Keep only each symbol's length. This is all a DEFLATE block sends. Sort by length, and by symbol number within a length: a (97); then r (114), end (256), 258 and 270; then ␣ (32), b , c and d . The first code is all zeros, as long as the shortest length: a gets 00 . Count up, then shift. Add one to the last code ( 01 ), then append a 0 for the extra bit of length: r gets 010 . Within a length, count up: end 011 , 258 100 , 270 101 . Again for 4 bits. 101 + 1 is 110 , and one more bit makes ␣ 1100 ; then b 1101 , c 1110 , d 1111 . The decoder runs the same rules on the same lengths, so it gets the same codes without ever seeing a tree. These are not the tree's codes: a was 10 and is now 00 , and r was 000 and is now 010 . But every symbol keeps its length, so the 11 symbols still cost 34 bits. Counting up keeps the code prefix-free: only a 's code starts 00 , and only the 4-bit codes start 11 . The distance code works the same way: d5 and d6, 1 bit each, get 0 and 1 . These are the codes the example's dynamic block uses, in the bit walk further on."},{"id":"decoding","heading":"Decoding with only the lengths","text":"So the decoder gets from a bare list of lengths to the right bytes without any symbol ever being named. For a : Its slot. The decoder counts the lengths as it reads them. Slot 97, the 98th length, is a 's because a is symbol 97, and it holds 2. Its code. Once it has every length, the decoder runs the canonical rule above and builds a lookup table from code to symbol. a is the only symbol of length 2, so it gets 00 , as it did in the encoder. Its byte. In the data, the decoder reads 00 , looks it up and gets symbol 97. A symbol below 256 is a literal, so it outputs byte 97, 01100001 : a . Step 2 is where the work is. The codes are 2, 3 and 4 bits long, and nothing in the data marks where one ends, so the decoder can't simply cut the data into codes. It could read one bit at a time and check, after each, whether the bits so far make a code: up to 4 checks per symbol here, and up to 15 in a block with 15-bit codes. Instead it builds a table that answers both questions at once: given the next few bits, which symbol do they start with, and how many bits is its code? Here it is for the example, from start to finish: from the lengths the block sent, to its codes, to its table, and then the table at work on the example's own data. Notice that only the lengths on the left come from the block; the decoder makes everything else from them. A short code fills several table entries and a long one fills one, and that the window always peeks at 4 bits but moves only as far as the entry says. The data bits are the example block's real ones, as the decoder reads them. Start from what the block sent: one length per symbol, in symbol order, 271 of them. The decoder knows each one's symbol by counting: the 98th is symbol 97, a , and it is 2. 262 of the 271 are 0, unused; they are folded into ranges here. Only 9 symbols have a code. Turn the lengths into codes, by the canonical rule : sort the 9 used symbols by length, then by symbol number, and count up. a , the only length 2, gets 00 ; the four 3s get 010 to 101 ; the four 4s get 1100 to 1111 . The encoder did the same, so these are its codes. Fill a 's entries. The longest code is 4 bits, so the table has one entry for every 4-bit pattern: 2 4 = 16. a 's code, 00 , is only 2 bits. Any 4 bits that start 00 begin with a , whatever the other two are, so a fills all four entries from 0000 to 0011 . Each one says: a , 2 bits. The longer codes fill fewer entries. Each 3-bit code fills two: r 0100 and 0101 , end 0110 and 0111 , 258 1000 and 1001 , 270 1010 and 1011 . Each 4-bit code fills one, ␣ to d , 1100 to 1111 . That makes 4 + 4 × 2 + 4 × 1 = 16: every entry is filled exactly once, so any 4 bits the decoder meets begin with exactly one code. Decode: peek at 4 bits. The data starts 0011 . Entry 0011 says a , 2 bits. The decoder outputs a and consumes only those 2 bits: the other two are the start of the next code. The window moves on by 2. The next 4 bits are 1101 : b , 4 bits. Output b , consume 4. 0100 : r , 3 bits. The decoder always peeks at 4 bits, and the entry says how far to move. It never needs to know in advance where a code ends. Four more lookups: 0011 a , 1110 c , 0011 a , 1111 d . Output so far: abracad . 1000 : 258, 3 bits. The last of those 4 bits isn't 258's: it was peeked at, not consumed. 258 is not a byte but a copy length, 4, so a distance comes next, looked up in the distance table, built the same way from the block's two distance lengths: 0 is d5, and its extra bit, 0 , makes the distance 7. The decoder copies abra from 7 bytes back: abracadabra . So a decoder builds its lookup tables itself, at the start of every dynamic block, from the lengths the block sends. It builds three, in order: first the code-length code's table, from its 3-bit lengths, so it can read the other lengths; then the literal/length and distance tables, from those. Only then can it read the data. A new dynamic block brings new lengths, so the tables are built again. A fixed block's lengths never change, "},{"id":"dynamic-header","heading":"Sending a dynamic code","text":"A dynamic block must tell the decoder its codes before its data. Thanks to canonical codes , it only needs to send each symbol's code length: 286 literal/length symbols and 30 distance symbols, 316 lengths in all, each from 0 to 15. The question is how to send them cheaply, and it matters: every bit spent on the code comes out of the saving. For the paragraph, the fitted code saved 854 bits on the data, and sending it cost 352 of them. Here are four tries, each fixing the biggest waste in the one before, measured on two blocks: the worked example written as a dynamic block (the one walked through below ), and zlib's real block for the 381-byte paragraph. Notice what the four steps do to the same list: the long bar is almost all zeros, cutting its end barely shortens it, and only collapsing the zeros into run symbols shrinks it. The bar is the cost to scale, against a mark at the example's 41 bits of data: even at its best, the code costs more than the data it describes. The strip under it shows what each step's cost is made of, as shares of that step, with the arithmetic under each part. Each chip is one code-length symbol; under it is what it stands for (×32 means a run of 32 zeros), its code once it has one, a run's extra bits as they arrive, and its cost in bits. Step 1: every slot, 4 bits. All 316 of the example's code lengths, in symbol order. Only the 11 marked slots are not zero. 1,264 bits. Step 2: say how many. HLIT and HDIST cut each list after its last used symbol, 270 and d6, so 38 slots go. 1,122 bits: the zeros in the middle are still there. Step 3: runs. Each stretch of zeros collapses into one run symbol: 18 for 11–138 zeros, 17 for 3–10. Each used length becomes one symbol too. The fans show which stretch of the old list each chip replaced. Read each column down. Symbol sent is what goes in the stream; stands for is what it means (×32 is a run of 32 zeros, a is a 's length); extra bits follow a run symbol and give its count as a plain number, least significant bit first, counted from the shortest run the symbol allows. Under the first 18, 1010100 read backwards is 0010101 , 21, and 18's runs start at 11, so 11 + 21 = 32. bits is what it costs: 5 for the symbol, plus its extra bits, so an 18 costs 5 + 7. 19 symbols come to 146 bits. Step 4: code the run symbols. 18, 3 and 4 get 2-bit codes, 17 gets 3 bits, 1 gets 4, and 0 and 2 get 5. With the 54 bits that describe this code, that makes 159 bits: for this short list, 13 more than step 3."},{"id":"step-naive","heading":"1. Every length, 4 bits each","text":"Write all 316 lengths as plain 4-bit numbers, in symbol order. That is 1,264 bits, 158 bytes, in every dynamic block whatever it holds. The example's data is only 41 bits, so its code table would cost 30 times as much as the data it describes. Even the paragraph's data, 1,521 bits, would nearly double. It is also more than ten times the tree's 111 bits for the example: the tree pays about 11 bits for each symbol a block uses, but the list pays 4 bits for every symbol it could use. Steps 2 to 4 cut what the unused ones cost."},{"id":"step-counts","heading":"2. Say how many","text":"Any one block uses few of the symbols. So send two counts first, HLIT and HDIST (plain 5-bit numbers), and stop each list after its highest used symbol; the rest count as 0. It barely helps: 1,122 bits for the example and 1,158 for the paragraph. The unused symbols are mostly in the middle of the alphabet, the byte values a text never contains, not at the end. The counts stay in the final scheme anyway: with no separators, they are how the decoder knows where each list ends."},{"id":"dynamic-runs","heading":"Runs, and a code for the runs","text":""},{"id":"step-runs","heading":"3. Replace runs with one symbol","text":"Write the lengths with a small alphabet of 19 symbols instead: 0–15 for a length, 16 to repeat the previous length, and 17 and 18 for runs of zeros, each followed by plain extra bits that give the count. For now, write each of these symbols as a plain 5-bit number. A whole stretch of unused byte values becomes one symbol: the example's 262 unused literal/length symbols take just 7. The header drops to 146 bits for the example ( step 3 in the figure ) and 443 for the paragraph. That is the big win, but the paragraph's header is still 443 bits. The code-length alphabet. Fixed by the format: the same in every dynamic block. Each range follows from its extra bits: 2 bits give 4 counts, 3 give 8, 7 give 128. Symbol Means Extra bits Count 0–15 a code length of 0 to 15 0 16 repeat the previous length 2 3–6 times 17 a run of zeros 3 3–10 18 a run of zeros 7 11–138 Runs start at 3 because a run of 1 or 2 is cheaper written out. Zeros get two run symbols, a short one and a long one, because they are by far the most common length."},{"id":"step-coded","heading":"4. Huffman-code the run symbols","text":"Step 3 still spends 5 bits on every run symbol, yet they are far from equally common. Of the paragraph's 78, the lengths 8 and 5 appear 14 times each and 0 appears 12 times, while 2 appears once and 16 not at all. That is the same skew Huffman coding removes from the data, one level down, so DEFLATE gives these symbols a Huffman code too: the code-length code , a Huffman code whose job is to send the other two codes. Its own 19 lengths go first, as plain 3-bit numbers. A dynamic block is therefore a stack of three layers, each written with the one below it: The three layers of a dynamic block, from the top. A decoder reads them from the bottom up. Layer Written with Size the data the literal/length and distance codes the whole block those two codes' lengths the code-length code up to 316 lengths the code-length code's lengths plain 3-bit numbers at most 19 × 3 = 57 bits It stops at three layers because the bottom one is so small: 57 bits at most, too little for yet another code to repay its own description. This is DEFLATE's actual scheme, and it costs 159 bits for the example ( step 4 in the figure ) and 352 for the paragraph. For the paragraph, coding the run symbols saved another 91 bits. For the tiny example it cost 13 more: describing the small code took more than it saved on just 19 run symbols. DEFLATE has no way to stop at step 3, which is one more reason tiny blocks are written with the fixed codes. And against the tree? For the example, the tree still wins, 111 bits to 159: a block with only 11 different symbols has too few names to be worth dropping. For the paragraph, which uses 46 literal/length symbols and 11 distances, the tree would cost 581 bits, 469 of them names, against 352 for the lengths. Real blocks run to thousands of symbols and use far more of the alphabet, so the gap only grows. Step The example The paragraph The tree, to beat 111 581 1. 4 bits each 1264 1264 2. Say how many 1122 1158 3. Runs 146 443 4. Code the runs 159 352 Runs make the big difference. Only after them does the list beat the tree, and only for the paragraph: the 35-byte example uses too few symbols, and its header ends up bigger than its data either way. What changes from block to block is only which of the 19 symbols a block uses, and the code each one gets: in the worked example below, symbol 18 is 10 and 16 isn't used at all, but 18 still means 11–138 zeros with 7 extra bits. For the 381-byte paragraph, zlib's whole header ( HLIT = 270, HDIST = 17, HCLEN = 16, then the lengths) is 352 bits, about 44 bytes. That is the price of the table, and the paragraph is long enough to repay it. The code-length code's 19 lengths are sent in a fixed order, 16, 17, 18, 0, 8, 7, 9, 6, 10, 5, 11, 4, 12, 3, 13, 2, 14, 1, 15, and HCLEN (4 bits) says how many are sent; any not sent count as 0. Go deeper: why that order? The order is a bet on which code lengths a block will use, most likely first, so that HCLEN can cut off the unlikely ones. The RFC lists it without a reason, but it reads in four parts: 16, 17, 18 : the repeat and zero-run symbols, which nearly every dynamic block uses to skip the byte values it never sees. 0 : every block has unused symbols. 8, 7, 9, 6, 10, 5, 11, 4, 12, 3, 13, 2, 14, 1 : outward from 8, because with 286 symbols most codes come out around 7–9 bits, and very short or very long codes are rare. 15 last, the rarest of all. For the paragraph, zlib's codes use lengths 2 to 8, so HCLEN is 16 and the last three, 14, 1 and 15, are never sent."},{"id":"dynamic-block","heading":"The same tokens as a dynamic block","text":"zlib wrote a fixed block for the opening string. To see every part of a dynamic Huffman block, here are the same tokens written as one by this page's encoder: it is the \"dynamic\" choice under Write it as . Dynamic means the codes are built for this one block, from its own counts, and sent at its start. They don't change while the block is read. The 26 bytes are 75 c6 31 15 00 00 08 42 c1 2a 56 fb 6a 02 fa 0f 3c 02 30 dd b1 e2 f8 30 e5 06 . This encoder's parse uses the copies from the window figure , (4, 7) and (23, 12), rather than zlib's (3, 7) and (22, 12). This is step 4 in full. It has the codes for the data, and the code-length code that sends their lengths. Keep the levels apart, because the walk below sends them bottom-up, the code-length code first: The three alphabets, and where the walk below defines and uses each one's code. The alphabets are the same in every dynamic block; the symbols used and their codes are this block's. Level Alphabet Symbols used in this block Their codes in this block Code defined in lines Code used in lines the data literal/length 9: a , b , r , c , d , ␣, 256, 258, 270 2, 3 or 4 bits 24–39 43–55 the data distance 2: d5, d6 1 bit each 40–42 51, 54 the lengths code-length (CL) 7: 1, 2, 3, 4 (the lengths above), 0, 17, 18 (for zeros) 2 to 5 bits 6–23 24–42 1 BFINAL = 1: last block 01 BTYPE: 10 = 2, dynamic 01110 HLIT: 01110 = 14, + 257 = 271 01100 HDIST: 00110 = 6, + 1 = 7 0111 HCLEN: 1110 = 14, + 4 = 18 000 CL symbol 16: 000 = 0, unused 110 CL symbol 17: 011 = 3 bits 010 CL symbol 18: 010 = 2 bits 101 CL symbol 0: 101 = 5 bits 000 CL symbol 8: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 7: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 9: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 6: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 10: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 5: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 11: 000 = 0, unused 010 CL symbol 4: 010 = 2 bits 000 CL symbol 12: 000 = 0, unused 010 CL symbol 3: 010 = 2 bits 000 CL symbol 13: 000 = 0, unused 101 CL symbol 2: 101 = 5 bits 000 CL symbol 14: 000 = 0, unused 001 CL symbol 1: 100 = 4 bits 10 +1010100 18: 32 zeros, 0–31 01 4: ␣ (32) gets 4 bits 10 +1010110 18: 64 zeros, 33–96 11111 2: a (97) gets 2 bits 01 4: b (98) gets 4 bits 01 4: c (99) gets 4 bits 01 4: d (100) gets 4 bits 10 +0100000 18: 13 zeros, 101–113 00 3: r (114) gets 3 bits 10 +1111111 18: 138 zeros, 115–252 110 +000 17: 3 zeros, 253–255 00 3: 256 (end) gets 3 bits 11110 0: 257 unused 00 3: 258 gets 3 bits 10 +0000000 18: 11 zeros, 259–269 00 3: 270 gets 3 bits 110 +010 17: 5 zeros, d0–d4 1110 1: d5 gets 1 bit 1110 1: d6 gets 1 bit 00 literal a (97) 1101 literal b (98) 010 literal r (114) 00 literal a (97) 1110 literal c (99) 00 literal a (97) 1111 literal d (100) 100 length symbol 258 → 4 0 +0 distance d5 → 7 1100 literal ␣ (32) 101 +00 length symbol 270 → 23 1 +11 distance d6 → 12 011 end of block (256) 00000 padding (not read) The block header. BFINAL is 1. BTYPE arrives as 0 , then 1 : binary 10, a dynamic block. Every code from here on is defined by the block itself. How many lengths follow. The code lengths come next as a bare list in symbol order: the first is symbol 0's, the second symbol 1's, with no symbol numbers and nothing to mark where the list ends and the data begins. So the block says first how long each list is. HLIT : 271 literal/length lengths, for symbols 0–270. 270 is the highest symbol this block uses, so the encoder stops there and leaves out 271–285. HDIST : 7 distance lengths, d0–d6. HCLEN : 18 of the 19 code-length-code lengths. The names are the RFC's: they count the lit eral/length, dist ance and c ode- len gth codes. The RFC never says what the H stands for, though it is usually read as Huffman. All three are plain numbers, which arrive lowest bit first, so each line shows the bits the usual way round as well: 0111 arrives, read backwards that is 1110 = 14, and HCLEN stores the count minus 4. The code-length code. CL is short for code length : the code-length alphabet has 19 symbols, 0–18, used to write out the code"},{"id":"choosing","heading":"Choosing a block type","text":"An encoder now has a choice for every block, and it matters. The example's dynamic block is twice the size of its fixed one, while the paragraph's dynamic block is a fifth smaller than its fixed one. And for data with no repetition or skew, such as random bytes, both codes make things worse: the fixed code spends 8 or 9 bits on every byte. That is what the third type, the stored block, is for: it copies the bytes as they are. Here are the three side by side. Stored (00) No compression: up to 65,535 raw bytes after a 4-byte length and its complement. Caps the cost of incompressible data at 5 bytes per 64 KiB No saving at all Fixed Huffman (01) Codes set by the RFC: 8 or 9 bits for a literal, 7 or 8 for a length, 5 for a distance. No table to send Wins on short inputs Ignores this data's skew Dynamic Huffman (10) Codes built for this block's own counts, sent as code lengths at the start of the block. Removes the skew too Usually wins once a block holds more than a few dozen bytes of text The table costs tens of bytes Any input can be written as any of the three, and every one is valid: a decoder accepts whichever it gets. Which one wins is a sum, not a rule. The encoder works out the exact size of each kind for every block (zlib ends a block after about 16,000 symbols) and writes the smallest. Tiny blocks come out fixed because a code table costs more than it saves: for the paragraph in the chart, zlib 1.2.12 switches from fixed to dynamic once the text is 74 bytes long. Input Stored Fixed Dynamic zlib -9 abracadabra ×3 (35 B) 114.3 37.1 74.3 42.9 A paragraph (381 B) 101.3 78.2 62.2 61.7 Random bytes (1 KiB) 100.5 105.6 105.2 100.5 Each input has a different winner. The short string can't pay for a table; the paragraph can; random bytes can't be compressed at all, so storing them is cheapest. The first three columns are the same tokens written as each block type; zlib picks per block."},{"id":"not-symbols","heading":"Everything in the stream that isn't a symbol","text":"Every part of a stream has now appeared, but scattered across the sections above, each one introduced where it was needed. To read a stream yourself, or to write a decoder, you need them in one place, with one rule for how each is written. A DEFLATE stream is mostly Huffman codes, but not entirely. Bits are written in two ways, and a decoder must know which kind comes next: A Huffman code is read one bit at a time, most significant bit first, because the decoder walks down the code tree as each bit arrives. Everything else is a plain number of a fixed, known width, read lowest bit first. Each byte of the stream fills from its lowest bit up, so a plain number's bits arrive in the order they are packed. In other words, DEFLATE is little-endian throughout, at the level of bits and of bytes (a stored block's 16-bit LEN comes low byte first), except that Huffman codes are stored bit-reversed. Write a byte the usual way, most significant bit on the left, and its plain fields read as ordinary binary, stacked from the right. Here is the first byte of the dynamic block above: 0 1 1 1 0 | 1 0 | 1 HLIT | BTYPE | BFINAL = 14 | = 2 | = 1 A Huffman code reads correctly in arrival order instead, so in this byte view it would look reversed. Decoders such as zlib reverse each code once, when they build their lookup tables, so that one little-endian bit reader can handle everything. Every plain field in a DEFLATE stream. None of these is Huffman-coded: the decoder knows each one's width from what came before it. Field Bits Where Holds BFINAL 1 start of every block 1 if this is the last block BTYPE 2 after BFINAL the block type ( values ) length extra bits 0–5 after a length symbol which length within the symbol's range: 270 means lengths 23–26, and its 2 extra bits, 0–3, add the offset ( try one ) distance extra bits 0–13 after a distance symbol which distance within the symbol's range: d6 means distances 9–12, and its 2 extra bits add the offset, so 11 = 3 gives 12 HLIT 5 dynamic block, after BTYPE the number of literal/length code lengths, minus 257: 257–288 is 32 values HDIST 5 after HLIT the number of distance code lengths, minus 1: 1–32 is 32 values HCLEN 4 after HDIST the number of code-length-code lengths, minus 4: 4–19 is 16 values code-length-code lengths 3 each after HCLEN , 4–19 of them the lengths of the code that compresses the code lengths, in the fixed order; that code's lengths are at most 7 run extra bits 2, 3 or 7 after code-length symbol 16, 17 or 18 the run's count, minus 3, 3 or 11 alignment 0–7 stored block, after BTYPE nothing: skipped to reach the next byte boundary LEN , NLEN 16 each stored block, after the alignment the number of bytes, then its ones' complement as a check stored bytes 8 each stored block, after NLEN the data itself, byte for byte final padding 0–7 after the last block nothing: fills the last byte Everything else in the stream is a Huffman code: the literal/length and distance symbols, and, inside a dynamic block's header, the code-length symbols. The worked example's 119 bits show both kinds side by side in the bit walk . Nothing in the stream says how long a field is. The decoder always knows what comes next and how big it is, in one of three ways: A width fixed by the RFC , chosen to fit the field's range: BFINAL , BTYPE , HLIT , HDIST , HCLEN , the 3-bit code-length-code lengths, and a stored block's LEN and NLEN . After BTYPE = 2, the next 5 bits are HLIT because the format says so. A width set by the symbol just decoded : the extra bits. Symbol 270 is always followed by 2 extra bits, d6 by 2, run symbol 18 by 7. No width at all : Huffman codes end themselves. The decoder reads one bit at a time until the bits so far match a code, and since no code is the start of another, it can never stop in the wrong place. That is why the stream needs no separators or length prefixes, and also why it must be read strictly in order: where HDIST starts depends on having read HLIT , and one corrupted bit misalign"},{"id":"try-it","heading":"Try it","text":"You have seen both savings on one 35-byte string. Here they are on any text you like. Type anything, or pick an example. The tokens are what LZ77 found; the numbers show each saving on its own and both together. Every count is the size of a real DEFLATE stream that this page's encoder writes, not an estimate. Text to compress 35 bytes abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra ×3 A paragraph One letter ×300 No repeats 280 bits as plain bytes 223 bits with Huffman alone 103 bits with LZ77 alone (fixed codes) 203 bits with both, incl. a 159 -bit table 13 bytes out, as a fixed block Write it as the smallest stored fixed dynamic 13 bytes ( 103 bits) as a fixed block: 4b 4c 2a 4a 4c 4e 4c 01 51 0a 38 d8 00 LZ77 replaced 27 of 35 bytes with 2 back-references, leaving 8 literals. Codes built for this text would need a 159-bit table, more than they save, so the fixed codes win. For comparison, your browser's own DEFLATE ( CompressionStream ) wrote … bytes. literal byte back-reference (length, distance) Click a back-reference to see the bytes it copies. a b r a c a d (4,7) ␣ (23,12) The token stream as a table # Token Stands for At byte 1 a a 0 2 b b 1 3 r r 2 4 a a 3 5 c c 4 6 a a 5 7 d d 6 8 (4,7) abra 7 9 ␣ ␣ 11 10 (23,12) abracadabra abracadabra 12 Click any back-reference to see which earlier bytes it copies, and use Write it as to see the same tokens as each kind of block: the smallest is what an encoder would send, but every one decodes. The paragraph shows both savings clearly: LZ77 finds the repeated words, and the skew of English letters pays for a dynamic table. One letter ×300 is almost all repetition. \"No repeats\" has nothing for LZ77 and too little text to pay for a table, so it comes out slightly larger than it went in."},{"id":"serial","heading":"Why decoding is serial","text":"Everything so far has been about size. The price of that density is speed. The output has no separators and no index. A code's length is known only once it has been decoded, so the decoder cannot find where symbol n + 1 starts without decoding symbol n . Back-references add a second chain: a copy reads bytes that earlier tokens wrote. That is why a gzip file is decompressed by one core, front to back, however many cores the machine has. Getting around it, for example to decode on a GPU, means finding where blocks start without decoding what comes before, and resolving copies whose sources aren't decoded yet. A later series here takes that on; nothing on this page depends on it."},{"id":"edge-cases","heading":"Edge cases","text":"Overlapping copies Allowed and common: distance 1 with length 258 repeats the previous byte 258 times. A decoder must copy byte by byte, not with a block copy, or it reads bytes that haven't been written yet. The longest match 258 bytes, with its own symbol (285) and no extra bits, so with the fixed codes a long run costs 13 bits per 258 bytes. 258 = 255 + 3: a length fits in a byte once the minimum of 3 is subtracted. End of block Symbol 256 ends every Huffman block, even the last. It needs a code, so it counts as a symbol when the tree is built. Empty input zlib writes 2 bytes, 03 00 : a final fixed block holding only the end-of-block code. Incompressible input Stored blocks cap the overhead at 5 bytes per 65,535. A large file of random bytes grows by under 0.01%; the 1 KiB in the chart grows by 0.5%."},{"id":"terms","heading":"Terms used on this page","text":"These are defined here, for this page. DEFLATE's broader terms are in the site glossary : DEFLATE , LZ77 , Huffman code , canonical Huffman code , back-reference , sliding window , end-of-block and extra bits . Alphabet The fixed set of symbols a Huffman code is built over. DEFLATE uses three: literal/length, distance and code-length. How the two ideas join BFINAL The first bit of every block: 1 if it is the last block of the stream. Blocks, and the fixed code BTYPE The 2 bits after BFINAL that give the block type: 00 stored, 01 fixed Huffman codes, 10 dynamic Huffman codes. 11 is invalid. Like every plain number, they arrive lowest bit first. The values Code length The number of bits in a symbol's Huffman code. 0 means the symbol has no code. A dynamic block sends only the lengths; the codes follow from them by the canonical rule. Send lengths, not trees Code-length code The small Huffman code a dynamic block uses to write down its code lengths. Its alphabet has 19 symbols: 0–15 are lengths, 16 repeats the previous length, and 17 and 18 are runs of zeros. CL is short for code length. Huffman-code the run symbols Distance alphabet The 30 symbols, d0–d29, that stand for back-reference distances from 1 to 32,768. Most stand for a range, picked out by extra bits. It has a Huffman code of its own. How the two ideas join Dynamic Huffman code Codes built for one block from that block's own symbol counts, and sent as code lengths at its start ( BTYPE 10). They don't change while the block is read: dynamic means per block, not adaptive. A dynamic block, bit by bit Fixed Huffman code Codes set once by the RFC, for any block with BTYPE 01, so no table is sent: 8 or 9 bits per literal, 7 or 8 per length symbol, 5 per distance symbol. Why 7–9 bits HCLEN 4 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many code-length-code lengths follow, minus 4. Those lengths come in a fixed order, so the count can cut off the usually-unused ones at the end. Why that order HDIST 5 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many distance code lengths follow, minus 1. In the bit walk HLIT 5 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many literal/length code lengths follow, minus 257. The lengths come as a bare list in symbol order, so the count says where the list ends. In the bit walk Literal A byte that LZ77 sends as itself, because no match of 3 or more starts there. It becomes the literal/length symbol with the same value, 0–255. LZ77 Literal/length alphabet The 286 symbols 0–285: 0–255 for literal bytes, 256 for the end of the block, and 257–285 for ranges of lengths. One Huffman code covers them all, so a single code says whether a literal or a copy comes next. How the two ideas join Overlapping copy A back-reference whose length is greater than its distance, so it reads bytes it is itself writing. It works because the decoder copies one byte at a time; distance 1 simply repeats a byte. The bit walk shows one Plain number A field of fixed, known width that is not Huffman-coded, read lowest bit first: the block header, the extra bits, a dynamic block's counts and code-length-code lengths, and a stored block's LEN and NLEN . Every plain field Prefix code A code in which no codeword is the start of another, so codes can be read back to back without separators. Every Huffman code is a prefix code. Huffman Stored block A block of up to 65,535 raw bytes ( BTYPE 00), after a byte-aligned length and its complement. Encoders use it when compressing would make the data bigger. Choosing a block type Symbol One element of an alphabet: what Huffman coding counts and gives a code. DEFLATE maps LZ77's tokens onto symbols, so Huffman coding never sees the raw bytes. How the two ideas join Token One item of LZ77's output: a literal, or a back-reference (length, distance). Each becomes one or two symbols. LZ77"},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"RFC 1951, DEFLATE : the format itself, short and readable. Section 3.2 has the alphabets and the canonical code. RFC 1952, gzip and RFC 1950, zlib : the two wrappers, a few header bytes and a checksum around the same stream. Huffman (1952), \"A Method for the Construction of Minimum-Redundancy Codes\" : the original paper. Ziv and Lempel (1977), \"A Universal Algorithm for Sequential Data Compression\" : where LZ77 gets its name. zlib's algorithm.txt : how zlib's encoder chooses matches, which this page leaves out."}]},{"href":"docs/how-succinctly-reads-json.html","title":"How succinctly reads JSON without building a tree","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer · succinctly 0.8 How succinctly reads JSON without building a tree jq turns the whole document into a tree of values before your filter runs. succinctly jq turns it into two strings of bits instead, and answers the filter by counting bits in them. This page builds those bits for a 23-byte document, walks .a[1] through them by hand, and then says what they cost and what they don't check. succinctly reads a JSON document once into a semi-index : one bit per byte that marks where each value starts, and two bits per value that record how the values nest. It finds any value by counting bits in those two strings, and decodes only the values a filter reads. On a 1 GB file, that is the difference between holding about 1.24 times the file in memory and jq 1.7.1's 8.6 to 9.8 times, on each of the three chips the release was measured on. The common mistake is to think a reader that skips the tree checks nothing. It checks every value it reads, with jq's own error message; what it skips is the values it never reads, unless you ask for --validate . This page assumes you use jq and know what a bit and a byte are; it assumes nothing about succinct data structures. It is one of three pages behind the succinctly 0.8.0 announcement . If you already know rank, select and balanced parentheses, skip to what the index costs ."},{"id":"need","heading":"What jq does before your filter runs","text":"Ask jq for one field of a big file and it still reads the whole file into a tree first: every object, array, string and number becomes a value in memory before the filter looks at any of them. That is a fine design for a 10 KB document and an expensive one for a 1 GB document. succinctly's release comparison runs first(.users[])|.id over a 1 GB file of user records, a query that wants one number near the start. jq 1.7.1's peak memory on it is 8.57 times the file's size on an AMD Ryzen 9 7950X and on an AWS Graviton4 (ARM Neoverse-V2), and 8.86 times on an Apple M4 Pro. From the 1 GB tier of the committed reports, docs/compare/jq-*.md , run at commit 955a984 . The file is 1,047.1 MiB. Almost all of that is the tree, and almost all of the tree is values the query never reads. Here is the document this page uses, 23 bytes long, and the query we will answer from it: $ cat doc.json {\"a\": [1, \"]\"], \"b\": 2} $ sjq -c '.a[1]' doc.json \"]\" Ducky asks Why build anything? Search the text for \"a\" , then read what comes after it. Try it. The search finds \"a\" at byte 1, and the [ at byte 6 starts the array. To reach element 1 you must find where element 0 ends, so you scan forward for a comma or the closing bracket. Count brackets from the [ , and the first ] you meet is at byte 11. That one is inside the string \"]\" . A text search can't tell, so it decides the array ended after one element, and answers wrong. A search for \"a\" has the same problem with a string value that happens to be \"a\" , or a key a one level down. And each new query would scan the text again. So the text alone is not enough, and a tree is too much. What we need is a record, made once, of where every value starts and which values are inside which , that keeps none of the values themselves. That record is called a semi-index: an index of the document's structure, with the values left in the text until someone asks for one. The term and the design come from Ottaviano and Grossi's 2011 paper, linked under further reading . succinctly's JSON cursor is modelled on hw-json, a Haskell library."},{"id":"index","heading":"Two strings of bits","text":"succinctly scans the document once, byte by byte, and writes two strings of bits. The first says where values start; the second says how they nest. The interest bits have one bit for every byte of the text. A byte gets a 1 when a value starts there: a { or [ , the opening quote of a string, or the first character of a number, true , false or null . Every other byte gets a 0: closing brackets, commas, colons, spaces, and everything inside a string. To know that a byte is inside a string, the scanner carries a state from byte to byte: between values, in a string, just after a backslash in a string, or in a number or word. That state is how the ] at byte 11 gets a 0 where the text search went wrong. The balanced parentheses have two bits for every value. Each 1 in the interest bits writes an open parenthesis, a 1 bit. A string or a number closes straight away, with a 0 bit, because nothing can be inside it; an array or object closes at its own closing bracket. Matching parentheses therefore enclose a value and everything inside it, and our document's seven values become (()(()())()()) , the 14 bits 11011010010100 . Step through the figure. The first four steps build the index; the last five answer .a[1] from it. Watch the lines between the two strings: they turn a position in one into a position in the other. The ] inside the string at byte 11 has no interest bit and no parenthesis, and each open parenthesis joins the interest bit with the same count: the fourth open, the fourth 1. Commas, colons and spaces leave no trace in either string. The bits are the ones succinctly 0.8.0 builds for this document. The text, 23 bytes. succinctly keeps it as it is. Nothing below copies a value out of it; a value is decoded from these bytes only when the query reaches it. Interest bits, one per byte. A 1 marks each byte where a value starts: { at 0, \"a\" at 1, [ at 6, 1 at 7, \"]\" at 10, \"b\" at 16 and 2 at 21. The ] at byte 11 gets a 0, because the scanner is in its in-a-string state there. Balanced parentheses, two bits per value. Each value opens with a 1 . The key \"a\" , the numbers and the strings close at once with a 0 ; the array closes at index 8, where its ] is, and the object at index 13. The two strings are in step. The k-th open parenthesis belongs to the k-th 1 in the interest bits, because the scanner wrote both at the same byte. Neither string stores the lines; they follow from the order, and counting recovers them, as step 9 shows. .a starts at the root, index 0. It is the first open, so its byte is the first 1: byte 0, { , an object. Bit 1 is another open, so index 1 is the object's first child: the second open, the second 1, byte 1, the key \"a\" . It matches. The key's value is its next sibling. The key opens at 1 and closes at 2, so the next value opens at 3. No text was read to find it. Every key is checked. jq keeps the last of two equal keys, so the lookup can't stop at the first match. The open at 3 is matched by the close at 8, which jumps over the whole array without reading a byte of it, and index 9 is the next key, \"b\" at byte 16. It doesn't match, so \"a\" 's value at index 3 stands. [1] . Index 3 is the third open, so byte 6, [ : an array. Its first element opens at 4 and closes at 5, so element 1 opens at 6. Which byte is index 6? Count the opens before it: 4, at 0, 1, 3 and 4. So it is the fifth value, and its byte is the fifth 1 in the interest bits: byte 10. Counting the 1s before a position is called rank , and finding the fifth 1 is called select . succinctly decodes the string that starts at byte 10 and prints \"]\" . The figure's bits are not worked out by hand: a small program built against the succinctly 0.8.0 library printed them three ways, from the SIMD scanner the index uses on an ARM machine, the table-driven scanner and the plain byte-at-a-time state machine, and all three agree. It also ran the walk above through the library's cursor and got the same indexes and byte 10."},{"id":"rank-select","heading":"Counting is the navigation","text":"Every move in the walk was one of three operations on the parentheses, and the one jump from the parentheses back to the text was rank followed by select. This is how succinctly's cursor turns a position in the parentheses into a byte of the text. A cursor is nothing but that position: pub struct JsonCursor<'a, W> { text: &'a [u8], index: &'a JsonIndex<W>, bp_pos: usize, // a position in the parentheses } pub fn text_position(&self) -> Option<usize> { let rank = self.index.bp().rank1(self.bp_pos); // opens before this one self.index.ib_select1_sequential(rank) // the byte of that interest bit } The moves through the tree are just as small. A value's first child is the next bit, if that bit is an open; its next sibling is one past its matching close, if that is an open. Step 6 was one of each. pub fn first_child(&self, p: usize) -> Option<usize> { if self.is_open(p) && self.is_open(p + 1) { Some(p + 1) } else { None } } pub fn next_sibling(&self, p: usize) -> Option<usize> { let close = self.find_close(p)?; if self.is_open(close + 1) { Some(close + 1) } else { None } } Ducky asks Haven't we just moved the problem? Counting the opens before position 6 is easy. Counting the opens before position 60 million is a scan of 60 million bits. It would be, with nothing else stored. So succinctly stores a little more: small directories of counts, built in the same pass, that turn each count into a lookup. Rank keeps a running total of 1s at the start of every 64-bit word. The 1s before position p are the total stored for p 's word, plus a popcount (one CPU instruction) of that word's bits below p . For the interest bits the totals are 32-bit numbers, one per 64 bits; the parentheses use a two-level version of the same table. Select looks for the word whose running total passes k , by binary search over the same totals, then finds the bit inside that one word. A walk in document order, like printing a whole document, starts each search where the last one landed, so it takes a few probes, not a full binary search. Matching a parenthesis is quick for a string or number, which is most of a document's values: its close is the very next bit. For an array or object, the parentheses keep the lowest nesting depth reached in each word, each 32 words and each 1,024 words, so the search can skip any block that cannot hold the match. That is how step 7 jumped the array. Everything from here down changes how fast the index is built and walked, not what it holds. The real scanner doesn't loop over bytes one at a time: it uses lookup tables, and SIMD on x86-64 and ARM, and a differential test runs every scanner a target compiles over every short combination of the bytes that matter, over random bytes and over mangled documents, and requires the same bits as the byte-at-a-time rules. The rules are state_machine in src/json/standard.rs ; the test is tests/json_builder_differential_properties.rs . The cursor operations shown here are the library's public API, and the jq evaluator adds many shortcuts on top of them that this page doesn't cover."},{"id":"cost","heading":"What the index costs","text":"The index is far smaller than a tree, but it is not free, and its size depends on how densely the document packs its values. succinctly's users test file has a value every 7.8 bytes on average. Estimate the index's size as a share of the file: about 5%, about 25%, or about 100%? Commit to one before you open this. About 25%: it measures 23.8%. Add up the parts you have already met. The interest bits are one bit per byte, 1/8 of the file: 12.5%. Their running totals are 32 bits per 64, half as much again: 6.25%. The parentheses are 2 bits per value, and a value every 7.8 bytes makes that 2 ÷ (8 × 7.8) = 3.2%. Their directories add 1.9%, and 12.5 + 6.25 + 3.2 + 1.9 is about 23.8%. If you said 5%, you counted the parentheses and their directories and forgot that the interest bits cost a bit for every byte, value or not. If you said 100%, you were thinking of a copy of the document, which the index never makes; a tree costs several times more than that. The interest bits and their totals cost the same 18.75% on every document. The parentheses add more where the values are packed tighter. Here is the same sum for four of succinctly's generated test files, measured by building the index with the 0.8.0 library and adding up its bit strings and directories: Index size as a share of the input, by file. Each file is one of succinctly's generated patterns, 6 to 10 MB. File Bytes per value Interest bits and totals Parentheses and directories Index strings , long string values 123 18.8% 0.3% 19.1% users , an array of user records 7.8 18.8% 5.1% 23.8% comprehensive , every JSON feature 6.0 18.8% 6.5% 25.3% arrays , arrays of small numbers 2.0 18.8% 19.7% 38.5% You can check the arithmetic against a real run. On the 1 GB file of user records, succinctly's peak memory is 1.24 times the file on the 7950X and on Graviton4, and 1.25 times on the M4 Pro: the file's own bytes, plus an index of about a quarter of them. jq 1.7.1 on the same file holds 9.48 times the file to print it with . on the 7950X and Graviton4, and 9.77 times on the M4 Pro. These are peak resident memory from the 1 GB tier of the reports, of the comparison harness's own build of succinctly (features cli,simd,regex,serde plus bench-runner ); the release archives are built with cli alone. Ducky asks A quarter of the file is a lot for an index. Why spend a bit on every byte? Why not keep a list of the bytes where values start? Because the list costs more on this file. Each entry would be a 4-byte offset, 32 bits per value. The interest bits and their totals cost 1.5 bits per byte, which at a value every 7.8 bytes is 11.7 bits per value. The list would only be smaller when values are more than about 21 bytes apart, as in the strings file. The other cost is time up front. The index covers the whole document and is built before the filter runs, so first(.users[])|.id holds the same 1.24 times the file as . does. The saving is in what happens next: no value is built until the filter reads it."},{"id":"validation","heading":"What it does not check","text":"jq refuses a malformed document before the filter runs. succinctly checks each value as the filter reads it, so a fault in a part the filter never reads goes unnoticed. Do {\"z\":1 \"y\":2} , which is missing its comma, and {\"z\":1,\"y\":2} get the same index, or different ones? Decide before you open this. The same. Both get the interest bits 1100010100010 and the parentheses 1101010100 . A comma or a colon writes nothing to either string, so the index can't hold its absence. In the same way tru indexes exactly as 1 does: both are a byte where a value starts. If you said different, you were picturing an index that records the commas. That would cost a bit for each one and would still not be a validator: it could not tell tru from true . So a fault in the index's blind spots is found when the faulty value is read, and not before. Break element 0 of our document and ask for element 1: $ cat broken.json {\"a\": [tru, \"]\"], \"b\": 2} $ sjq -c '.a[1]' broken.json \"]\" $ sjq -c '.a[0]' broken.json jq: parse error: Invalid literal at line 1, column 11 $ jq -c '.a[1]' broken.json jq: parse error: Invalid literal at line 1, column 11 $ sjq --validate -c '.a[1]' broken.json jq: validation error: invalid keyword 'tru' (expected null, true, or false) --> broken.json:1:8 | 1 | {\"a\": [tru, \"]\"], \"b\": 2} | ^ The read of element 0 fails with jq's own message and exit status 5. The read of element 1 never touches tru , and succeeds where jq 1.7.1 refuses the file. --validate runs a strict RFC 8259 check of the whole document before the filter, and exits with status 3. Why not always validate? Because a full check reads every byte, which is the work the index exists to skip. One indicative measurement in succinctly's architecture notes put strict validation of an 8.4 MB file at 8.6 ms: two thirds as much again as a query like .users[0].name , and under 5% of printing the whole file with . . On an Apple M5 Max, minimum of 7 runs with process start-up subtracted, not interleaved and not on an idle machine; the notes ask for a re-measure before relying on the exact figures. Invalid UTF-8 is the exception: checking it is cheap, so it is done for every document as it is read. This is one of succinctly's recorded differences from jq, the kind it permits only where matching jq would cost what the design is for. The rule, and the record of every difference, are on the compatibility page ."},{"id":"rebuild","heading":"Rebuild it yourself","text":"Without scrolling back up, write the interest bits and the parentheses for [1,[2]] , then work out which byte element 1 starts at. Show the answer Interest bits 1101100 , parentheses 11011000 . The values start at bytes 0 ( [ ), 1 ( 1 ), 3 ( [ ) and 4 ( 2 ); the comma and the two closing brackets get 0s. The parentheses are open for the outer array, 10 for the 1 , open for the inner array, 10 for the 2 , then a close for each array. Element 1 starts at byte 3. Element 0 is index 1, the first child of the root. It opens at 1 and closes at 2, so element 1 opens at index 3. Two opens come before index 3, so its byte is the third 1 in the interest bits: byte 3, the inner [ . succinctly agrees: $ sjq -c '.[1]' small.json [2] Reach for this kind of index when the document is large and a query reads a small part of it, or when the memory a tree would take is the problem. A tree still wins when the document is tiny and the run is mostly process start-up: on an Apple M4 Pro jq 1.7.1 starts faster, as the announcement's Where jq still wins section shows."},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"Announcing succinctly 0.8.0 : what the release is, how fast it is on which chips, and how to install it. Benchmarking against jq : how the speed and memory figures on this page were measured. Bug-for-bug jq compatibility : the rule that makes succinctly jq answer as jq 1.7.1 does, and the recorded differences such as the one above. Ottaviano and Grossi, Semi-Indexing Semi-Structured Data in Tiny Space (2011): the paper that named semi-indexing and pairs it with balanced parentheses. succinctly's semi-indexing notes , and its JSON parsing notes for the state machine and the SIMD scanners. The code: the scanner's rules in src/json/standard.rs , the cursor in src/json/light.rs , and the parentheses in src/trees/bp.rs . hw-json : the Haskell library succinctly's JSON cursor is modelled on."}]},{"href":"docs/pedagogical-writing.html","title":"Pedagogical writing: so the reader could have invented it","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer Pedagogical writing: so the reader could have invented it Pedagogical writing is writing whose job is to teach: the reader should leave able to use an idea, not just able to say they have read it. Clarity is not enough for that. Here is what else it takes, built up from one clear paragraph that fails. Order an idea so the reader could have invented it: make them need it, try the obvious answer, show exactly where that answer breaks, then give the fix, and only then its name. Then ask a question that someone still holding the wrong answer would get wrong. It matters because clarity is not learning. In one physics study, a clear, correct video taught half as much as a version that voiced the students' wrong ideas and took them apart. The common mistake is writing in the order you now understand the idea, answer first and reason after. That hides the question the answer was for, and leaves the reader's wrong model untouched. Already know the loop? The method in five lines is in the last section , and a routine for your next draft is in Your turn ."},{"id":"clear-is-not-enough","heading":"Clear, correct, and nothing learned","text":"A first-year student answers a physics question, watches a seven-minute video that explains the answer, and answers it wrong again, more confident than before. The question is about a book sliding down a rough ramp at constant speed: is the force pulling it down the ramp greater than the friction holding it back, less, or equal? She picks \"greater\", reasoning that something has to win for the book to keep moving. The right answer is \"equal\": at constant speed the forces balance. Then she watches a video that explains Newton's first and second laws with animations and live demonstrations, including a book pushed across a table at constant speed. Asked again, she answers \"greater\" again, and raises her confidence from 5 to 6 out of 7 \"because she believes she better understands the concepts after watching the video\". Notice that her picture has a winner, and a winner is what makes a book speed up. Sliding at constant speed needs no winner: the two arrows are the same length. Her model, that something has to win for the book to keep moving, is exactly what the video never touches. She is the opening example of a paper by Derek Muller and Manjula Sharma at the University of Sydney, drawn from their studies of more than a thousand physics students over three years. They introduce her as \"Imagine a first year student\", so she is a typical case from their data, not one named person. Muller later started the Veritasium channel. Nothing was wrong with the video. In their words, \"the sentences used to describe the phenomena are not long or convoluted, nor does the presenter seem to struggle with making the concepts plain.\" They compared that clear lecture with other versions of the same lesson. One, the Dialogue, was a scripted conversation in which a student says the common wrong ideas out loud and a tutor takes them apart. Another, the Refutation, had a single narrator voice and correct the same wrong ideas. Guess before you open this: which raised the students' confidence more, the clear lecture or the Dialogue that voiced their wrong ideas? The lecture, the Dialogue, or about the same? About the same. In the 2006 study every version raised students' confidence by 0.6 to 0.7 points on average, on the seven-point scale, with no significant difference between them. Learning was another matter: students who watched the Dialogue or the Refutation gained on average twice as much as those who watched the clear lecture. Notice that the confidence column is flat , so a reader's confidence can't tell you which version taught, while the learning column separates the two that put the wrong ideas on the table. The bars show the headline results, not raw scores: confidence at the middle of the reported range, learning as multiples of the lecture's gain. If you picked the lecture, you had a reason: it is the more polished explanation, and polish feels like teaching. If you picked the Dialogue, you expected confidence to follow learning. It doesn't. Confidence tracks how smoothly the material went down, and a clear video goes down smoothly whether or not it changes anything. A later experiment found the same from the other side: viewers of a clear video that taught misconceptions felt just as sure as viewers of a correct one, and came away with \"more misconceptions and fewer scientifically correct conceptions\" (Kulgemeyer and Wittwer, 2022). That gap between feeling taught and being taught is what this page is about. Clarity is necessary, but the ramp shows it is not sufficient: the reader also has to want the idea, meet their own wrong answer, and see where it breaks. So the question for this page is concrete. Take a clear, correct paragraph that a newcomer reads, feels good about, and cannot use. What do you change, and how do you find out whether it worked? Here is the route. The next two sections take one such paragraph, from Wikipedia, and find exactly where it fails a reader. Then we rebuild it in four steps, which I'll call beats, and switch"},{"id":"first-try","heading":"A first try: just say it clearly","text":"The obvious way to explain something is to say it clearly: state the rule, give the reason, name it, and get every detail right. This is how most of us write, and it rests on a model of reading worth stating in full. The reader takes in each sentence. If every sentence is true and clear and follows from the one before, the reader ends up holding what the writer holds. On this model, every failure to teach is a failure of clarity: a long sentence, an undefined term, a skipped step. So the cure is always more editing for clarity. Here is a paragraph that passes that test. It is from Wikipedia's article on the dynamic array, the growable array behind Rust's Vec , Java's ArrayList and Python's list . I chose it because it is good: short, true, and in a sensible order. To avoid incurring the cost of resizing many times, dynamic arrays resize by a large amount, such as doubling in size, and use the reserved space for future expansion. […] As n elements are inserted, the capacities form a geometric progression. Expanding the array by any constant proportion a ensures that inserting n elements takes O ( n ) time overall, meaning that each insertion takes amortized constant time (so long as any given allocation takes O (1) time). From Wikipedia, “Dynamic array”, section “Geometric expansion and amortized cost”, revision of 21 September 2026 , CC BY-SA 4.0 . […] marks an omitted code sample that doubles the capacity and copies. Read it once as a newcomer would. If you already know why arrays double, this reads well, and that turns out to be part of the problem. One fact the paragraph takes for granted: a resize allocates a new buffer and copies every item across. With that, here is a question the paragraph should let a newcomer answer. Commit to a guess before you open it; a guess is enough. A colleague suggests growing by a fixed 1,000 slots instead of doubling: less wasted space, and still \"a large amount\". Starting empty, a million appends copy roughly how many items in total? About 1 million, about 500 million, or about 500 billion? About 500 million , nearly 500 times what doubling copies. A copy happens every 1,000 appends and moves everything so far: 1,000 + 2,000 + … + 999,000, which is 1,000 × (1 + 2 + … + 999) = 499,500,000. Doubling copies only at 1, 2, 4, … items, up to 524,288 (2 19 ), 1,048,575 in all: about one copy per item. Appends Fixed step of 1,000 Doubling 100k 4.95 0.13 200k 19.9 0.26 300k 44.85 0.52 400k 79.8 0.52 500k 124.75 0.52 600k 179.7 1.05 700k 244.65 1.05 800k 319.6 1.05 900k 404.55 1.05 1,000k 499.5 1.05 Notice that the fixed step curves upward because every resize copies everything so far, while doubling stays on the floor and only steps up when the array fills at 2 k items. At a million appends the gap is 499.5 million against 1.05 million. \"About 1 million\" is the answer the paragraph's first sentence teaches: \"resize by a large amount\", and 1,000 is large. \"About 500 billion\" is the count for growing by one slot at a time, 1 + 2 + … + 999,999 items. That sum is about 999,999² ÷ 2, so it grows with the square of the number of appends. A step of 1,000 divides it by 1,000, and that is all it does: the total still grows with the square. Ten million appends would cost 100 times as much, not 10 times. Doubling is cheap because its step grows with the array."},{"id":"crack","heading":"Where clear writing cracks","text":"If you answered \"about 1 million\", the paragraph taught you exactly what it says first. The idea that rules out 1,000 is in it, but in the one place a reader is least likely to take it from. Let's look at where each part sits, and at what a reader takes from it. The answer comes before the question. The first sentence gives the rule, \"resize by a large amount, such as doubling\", for a problem stated only in the abstract: \"the cost of resizing many times\". The reader never sees that cost, so they have nothing to measure \"large\" against. The wrong answer is never tried. The natural first attempt, growing by a fixed step, never appears, so it is never broken. Worse, the first sentence half-endorses it, since 1,000 slots is \"a large amount\". The reader's own model leaves the paragraph exactly as it came in, as the ramp student's did. The idea is buried. What makes doubling work, \"any constant proportion\", sits inside the subject of the last sentence, among symbols, while the end of the sentence, where readers put their weight (the section on sentences says why), goes to a caveat in brackets. It is the one phrase that separates the right model from the wrong one. Remember the word proportion : we will come back to where it should have gone. None of these is a failure of clarity: each sentence is clear. The failure is the order. The paragraph runs in the order its author holds the idea, rule first and reason after, which is the reverse of the order a newcomer could build it in. Grant Sanderson, who makes the 3Blue1Brown maths videos on YouTube and has thought harder than most about why explanations work, gives the same diagnosis as advice: \"try very hard to structure your explanation to go from the concrete to the abstract. I think almost always when you understand something the natural inclination is to go the other way around.\" That inclination has a name, the curse of knowledge : once you know something, it is hard to imagine not knowing it. Steven Pinker calls it \"the single best explanation I know of why good people write bad prose.\" It is also why the paragraph may have read well to you. If you already hold the model, every gap fills itself in, so the gaps are invisible to the one person who could close them. So the crack points at what the fix must do, and better sentences are not it. The reader has to hold the question before the answer, and their own wrong answer before the right one, so that by the time the rule arrives they can nearly guess it."},{"id":"core-loop","heading":"The fix: the question before the answer","text":"This is the key step of the page. We rebuild the paragraph so that each part answers a question the part before it raised, and the rule arrives when you can nearly guess it. Below is the same content in both orders, Wikipedia's on top and my rewrite underneath. Step through it and watch what moves and what we have to add. Notice how little of the content changes. The rule becomes the fix , fourth instead of first; the buried reason becomes the crack's conclusion ; the name stays last . What the rewrite adds, the dashed boxes, is a question, a wrong answer and a check . Arrows mean \"becomes\". Both orders at once. Wikipedia's paragraph is on top, the rewrite underneath, and each arrow shows where a part of the original ended up. The steps take the rewrite one beat at a time. Wikipedia's order: rule, reason, name. Every sentence is true, and each follows from the last. Nothing in it is a question, and nothing in it is wrong. Two beats with no counterpart. A question with a stake (a million appends: how much copying?) and the answer a newcomer is most likely to give: grow by a fixed 1,000 slots. It is labelled as a first try, so a skimmer doesn't keep it. The crack counts what the attempt costs , 500 million copies, and finds the waste: every resize copies the early items again. So the room added has to grow with the array. That is Wikipedia's \"any constant proportion\", promoted from a clause to the conclusion of an argument. Only now the rule: double it. It moves from first to fourth, where the reader can nearly guess it, because the crack has said what it must do. The name is last in both. Wikipedia gets this right. In the rewrite the name labels counts the reader has just seen: a million appends, about a million copies. A check the reader can fail. Growing by 1.5× is a near-twin of the 1,000-slot question: same idea, different surface. A reader who took away \"double\" rather than \"in proportion\" calls it quadratic, and finds out they were wrong. The figure has six boxes, but only four beats. The name belongs to the fix, which ends on it, and the check comes after the explanation rather than inside it; it gets a section of its own . I'll call the four beats together the core loop , because on a teaching page every idea goes round it once: Need. Make the reader want the idea, with a puzzle, a goal or a failure, and put numbers in it: a million appends, how much copying? Naive attempt. Try the approach your reader would try, concretely, and label it as a first try where it appears, so a skimmer doesn't leave with it: a fixed step of 1,000 slots. Crack. Show where it breaks, and exactly where: which case, which term. Phrase the break as the property any fix must have: every resize copies the early items again, so the room added must grow with the array. Fix, then name. Give the fix in the words of that property, then its technical name with its plain meaning: double it; that is amortised O(1). Each beat does a different job. The need is what keeps a reader reading; Sanderson argues that education \"is not rate limited by the clarity of explanation, it's rate limited by motivation.\" The naive attempt and the crack do what Muller's Dialogue added to the clear lecture: the reader's wrong model is said out loud, as a mechanism, then broken on a case. On the ramp, that addition doubled the learning gains. And the name comes last because, as Sanderson says of notation in a good explanation, \"it arrives only once it's articulating something that already exists at least loosely in the viewer's mind.\" A caution about the word \"invented\". People left to rediscover an idea on their own learn less than people who are told it: a meta-analysis of 580 comparisons found unassisted discovery behind explicit instruction (d = −0.38), while discovery with scaffolding, worked examples and feedback came out ahead (d = +0.30). d is an effect size: the gap between two groups' average scores, in standard deviations. 0.3 is a modest but real difference. Alfie"},{"id":"beats","heading":"What each beat does","text":"\"Each beat matters\" is easy to nod along to. Let's test it instead: switch a beat off and read what is left. Below is the rewrite in full, each paragraph tagged with its beat. Read it once as it stands. Then switch off the Crack and read the fix again; the note under the text says what a newcomer would now be missing. The other switches work the same way, with two twists: Name last off moves the name to the top, and with the naive attempt off the crack goes too, since there is nothing left to break. Rule and name only strips the text back to Wikipedia's shape. Switches Need Naive attempt Crack Name last Check All beats Rule and name only Name, moved first A dynamic array gets amortised O(1) appends by growing in proportion. Need A dynamic array, like Rust's Vec or Python's list , never asks how long it will get. Its items sit in one contiguous buffer, and a buffer can't grow in place: when it is full, the array must allocate a bigger one and copy every item across. Append a million log lines to one, and how much copying is that? Naive attempt A first try: grow by a fixed step, say 1,000 slots at a time. Then only one append in 1,000 has to copy anything. Crack Count the copies. Each resize moves everything so far: 1,000 items, then 2,000, then 3,000. A million appends copy 1,000 + 2,000 + … + 999,000 items: about 500 million. A bigger step only divides that total, because the early items are still copied again at every resize. So whatever we change must make resizes rarer as the array grows: the room we add has to grow with the array. Fix So grow in proportion: when the buffer is full, double it. Copies now happen only at 1, 2, 4, 8, … items, and each one buys as many free appends as it moved. A million appends copy 1 + 2 + 4 + … + 524,288 items: about a million, roughly one copy per item, and nearly 500 times fewer than the fixed step. Name That is what amortised O(1) means: n appends cost work proportional to n , so the average append costs a constant amount, even though one unlucky append still copies half a million items. Check Java's ArrayList grows by half its length each time, not by double. Is that still a constant number of copies per item, or does the total grow with the square, like the fixed step's? Still a constant per item: the room it adds, half the array, grows with the array. Counting from ArrayList 's default 10 slots, a million appends copy about 2.4 million items. That is more than doubling's million, and nowhere near the fixed step's 500 million. Doubling was never the point; proportion is. Every beat on. Each paragraph answers the question the one before it raised, and the check tests the one idea a reader is most likely to get wrong. Switch a beat off to see what a newcomer loses. Need off. The text now opens on an answer to a question nobody asked: why would anyone grow anything by 1,000 slots? \"Each resize moves everything so far\" refers to copying the reader was never told about, and 500 million has nothing to be large next to. Naive attempt off, and the crack with it: with no wrong answer on the table, there is nothing to break. \"Double it\" arrives as a rule handed down, which is the shape of the Wikipedia paragraph. Crack off. The fixed step is proposed and never shown to fail, and \"So grow in proportion\" now follows from nothing. Why double, and not keep the 1,000-slot step just offered? The property that rules it out, room that grows with the array, was in the crack. A reader who guessed \"about 1 million\" earlier would still think they were right. Name first. The reader meets \"amortised O(1)\" before any count it could summarise, so it is a label with nothing to stick to. It also gives away the answer before the need has asked the question. Check off. Nothing tells a reader who took away \"double\" rather than \"in proportion\" that they did; they would call Java's 1.5× quadratic. They finish as confident as the student on the ramp. Rule and name only: the Wikipedia paragraph's shape. Every sentence le"},{"id":"ladder","heading":"But a page holds many ideas: a ladder","text":"So far, one idea and one loop. A real page has several, and how we join their loops decides whether the page reads as a lesson or as a list. The rewrite already shows the join. Doubling has a flaw of its own: right after a resize, up to half the new buffer is empty. That flaw is the next loop's need. Its fix is a smaller factor, such as Java's 1.5 or CPython's roughly 1.125 (Wikipedia's article has a table of them), which trades more copies for less empty space while keeping the room growing with the array. And that fix has its own flaw: every resize still copies the whole array in one go, a pause that a program with a latency budget notices, and the need for a further loop. Each fix leaves a problem, and each problem starts the next loop, so the joins between loops are always \"but\" or \"therefore\", never \"and then\". Nicky Case borrowed this rule for interactive explanations from Matt Stone and Trey Parker, who write stories not as \"this happens, and then that happens\", but as \"this happens, THEREFORE that happens, BUT that happens, THEREFORE this happens\". A page in this blog's series on DEFLATE, the compression inside gzip and PNG, shows a whole ladder. You don't need to know DEFLATE to follow its shape. A compressor that has invented its own short codes for a stretch of data must first tell the decoder what they are. It does that by sending a list of 316 small numbers, one for every symbol the format allows, and most of them are 0 because most symbols never occur. The question is how to send that list cheaply. How a DEFLATE block sends its own code takes four tries, each measured in bits on the same 381-byte paragraph of English, and each fixing the biggest waste the one before it left: Notice that each crack, in italics, is what the next try fixes, and that one try barely helps: cutting the tail saves 106 bits , because the zeros are in the middle of the list. The page keeps that try anyway, since the counts it adds tell the decoder how many numbers to read. Bars are to scale; the numbers are the page's own measurements. Three habits keep your ladder climbable. One new idea per rung : a section that needs a second mechanism is two sections. Measure every rung on one benchmark , as the bits above are, so the gap that remains is visibly the next need. And raise the next problem before the reader does . Just before the ladder above, that page swaps a description naming only the 11 symbols its worked example used for a list with an entry for all 316. It objects to its own move straight away: \"You might object that we have swapped 11 symbol names for something worse: a length for every symbol a block could use, 316 of them, used or not. You would be right, and squeezing that list is the rest of this page.\" A reader who was about to object learns that the writer saw it too, and why to keep reading. Not every attempt deserves a loop of its own. A dead end that teaches nothing gets one sentence; show in full only the attempt whose crack points somewhere."},{"id":"promises","heading":"When a step can't be guessed: promises","text":"But not every rung can be made guessable yet. You may need a simplification for now, a \"why\" that only makes sense later, or a detail that would derail the story. The loop doesn't cover these steps. A promise does. A promise is any place where you ask the reader to accept something on credit. Promises are fine. Unflagged or unpaid ones lose readers, because the reader can't tell a temporary simplification from the truth, and a skimmer keeps the simplification. Three moves handle them: Flag it where you make it, with its size. Sanderson, simplifying a quantum algorithm: \"You should know there is that added bit of complexity that I'm postponing, but this is 90% of the right idea.\" The reader now knows what kind of claim they are holding. Pay it off by name. Come back to the claim, quote it, correct it, and say what the simple version would have got wrong. At its third try, the DEFLATE page writes each run symbol (a short code standing for a run of zeros) \"for now … as a plain 5-bit number\", and the next section opens on exactly that cost: \"Step 3 still spends 5 bits on every run symbol, yet they are far from equally common.\" Defer out loud. Name the question the reader is about to ask, say where it is answered (or that it isn't), and give them the one fact they need to carry on. Claims need the same honesty. For each statement the page doesn't show, say where it stands: shown later, cited, measured, or only asserted. And treat every \"obviously\" as a claim with no status: either show it, or say where it stands. I have made promises on this page too. \"For now, treat a page as a single loop\" was paid off in the last section. The word proportion is still owed; it is paid in the section on sentences ."},{"id":"checks","heading":"Smooth reading isn't learning: make the reader commit","text":"Even a perfect loop can be read without being learned, which is exactly what happened on the ramp. The only way you or the reader can find out is a question they can get wrong. You have answered two on this page, and that was deliberate. A meta-analysis of questions asked before a lesson, with its methods registered before the analysis, found that they improved learning of the material they asked about (g = 0.54) but barely touched the rest (g = 0.04). They helped far more when people guessed an answer (g = 0.65) than when they only read the question (g = 0.22). g is the same kind of effect size as d . St. Hilaire, Chan and Ahn, 2024. So ask for a commitment, an option, a number or a shape, and say that a guess is enough. Aim it at the key step, because the benefit stays where the question points. The harder part is choosing which question to ask. A question teaches only if the reader's likely wrong model gives a different answer from the right one. Here are two questions we could have asked about the Wikipedia paragraph: Is doubling better than adding one slot at a time? Both models answer yes. A reader who thinks \"grow by a large amount\" gets it right Says nothing about proportion Grow by 1,000 slots: about how many copies? The models disagree. \"A large amount\" predicts about 1 million; \"proportion\" predicts about 500 million A wrong guess tells the reader which model they hold Build each wrong option from one misconception, and in the reveal say why each was a reasonable guess before correcting it, as the 1,000-slot reveal did. Then state the insight, not only the number, so a reader who was right for the wrong reason finds out. When a reader meets a mechanism more than once, fade the support: work the first case fully, leave the last step of the second as a prediction, and give the third to the reader. This page does that with the loop itself. Wikipedia's paragraph was worked through for you, the DEFLATE ladder came with its cracks labelled, and the exercise is yours."},{"id":"sentences","heading":"The same order inside a sentence","text":"The loop puts what the reader already holds before what is new. A good sentence does the same, on a smaller scale. George Gopen and Judith Swan found that readers expect a sentence to open with old information that links back, and to end on the new information it exists to deliver. They call the end of a sentence its stress position , because readers give the most weight to whatever comes just before the full stop. Their rule: \"Put in the topic position the old information that links backward; put in the stress position the new information you want the reader to emphasize.\" Now we can pay off proportion . Wikipedia's last sentence puts that word near the start, inside the subject, and gives its stress position to a caveat in brackets. In both versions below, the load-bearing word and the end of each sentence are marked: As written “Expanding the array by any constant proportion a ensures that inserting n elements takes O ( n ) time overall, meaning that each insertion takes amortized constant time (so long as any given allocation takes O (1) time) .” Stress where it belongs “Inserting n elements then takes O ( n ) time overall, amortised constant time per insertion . What makes that true is that the array grows by a constant proportion, not by a constant amount .” The second version adds no facts. It reorders them so that each sentence ends on its point, and the last one ends on the contrast a newcomer is most likely to miss: proportion, not amount. That is the 1,000-slot question, answered in the stress position. (The caveat about allocation is true but secondary; it can go in its own sentence or a footnote.) Two more habits come from the same idea: Keep one story per paragraph. List the first few words of each sentence. They should name the same character, or follow a chain in which each sentence starts where the last one ended. If they read \"The array… Copies… A factor… Memory…\", the paragraph has no subject, and the reader has to build the links alone. One name per thing. A new word reads as a new thing. If \"buffer\", \"allocation\" and \"storage\" all mean the same memory, the reader counts three things. Joseph Williams, in his textbook Style , warns against this \"elegant variation\". It is why this page calls the beats need, naive attempt, crack and fix every time."},{"id":"figures","heading":"Figures and interactives earn their place","text":"Order fixes most of what goes wrong in prose. But some things no ordering of sentences can fix: a reader who has to hold too much in their head, or see something words can't show. That is where a figure earns its place. So write the prose first, find where a reader would have to juggle too much, and add a figure only there. This page's two main figures each answer one such failure. The two orders are drawn one above the other, because comparing a paragraph with its rewrite from memory is a memory test. Amit Patel, who makes the interactive explanations at Red Blob Games, puts it as a rule: \"It's often better to show all the variants than to ask the reader to interact with the diagram to see the variants.\" The beat switches are interactive for the opposite reason: there are more variants (one per switch, and their combinations) than a page can show, and the claim that each beat matters is only convincing once you have removed one yourself. Three rules make an interactive teach rather than decorate: The default state already teaches. Patel: \"when feasible, start a diagram in a 'useful' state that doesn't require interaction to learn from it.\" The beat switches start with every beat on, so a reader who never touches them, or has no JavaScript, still reads the whole rewrite. Say what to do and what to look for in the sentence before it. \"Switch off the crack and read the fix again\" is an instruction; \"explore the demo\" is not. The caption says what to notice , not what the picture is, and what not to read into it. The beat switches' caption says their notes are a reading, not a measurement."},{"id":"practice","heading":"Your turn: a drafting routine","text":"Here is the whole method as a routine for your next explanation: a blog post, a design doc, an onboarding page. Name the reader and the one question the page answers, with a number in it if you can. Leaving out fundamentals that reader already has is correct, not careless. Read two or three existing explanations and mark where you stalled. Whichever step stalled you gets a section of its own in your page. Note the wrong model your reader most likely brings. Pick one running example that keeps the hard part hard, from real data or real code where you can. Lay out the ladder: for each idea a need, a naive attempt, a crack and a fix, joined by \"but\" and \"therefore\". List your promises: every simplification and deferral, with where it is paid off. Place one or two predictions at the key step, built so the nearest wrong model gives a different answer. Check that nothing above it, the TL;DR included, has already given the answer away. Draft the prose , add each figure against a named failure of the prose, and write the opening and the summary last. Get a cold read. Ask someone who doesn't know the topic where they first stalled, not what they thought of it. The last step is the one the curse of knowledge makes necessary, because you can't read your own page as a newcomer. Ask the reader to read silently and stop at the first sentence where they lost the thread. When no person is free, this blog uses a fresh AI agent as a stand-in: given only the page and the reader's background, with no conversation history, it reports its first stall, every term it couldn't place, and every number whose origin it couldn't trace. It has read far more than any newcomer, so treat what it finds as a minimum, not the whole list. Optional exercise: rewrite a clear paragraph about B-trees Here is an explanation in the same textbook shape as Wikipedia's. I wrote it for this exercise, but you will find versions of it in many books: “A B-tree is a balanced search tree in which each node holds many keys and has many children. Its large fanout keeps the tree shallow, which minimises the number of disk reads per lookup.” It is clear and correct. Rewrite it with a need, a naive attempt, a crack and a fix, and keep the word \"fanout\" until the end. Open the hints one at a time, only when you are stuck. Hint 1: what would a reader try first? The need: look a key up among a billion stored on disk, where each read from disk costs far more than any comparison. What search structure would a programmer reach for first? Hint 2: count what that attempt costs A balanced binary search tree over a billion keys (about 2 30 ) is about 30 levels deep, and each level is a separate read from disk. Each read also fetches a whole page, typically 4 to 16 KB, only to use one key from it. Where is the waste, and what property must the fix have? Hint 3: one worked answer Need: a lookup among a billion keys on disk, where each read costs far more than any comparison. Naive attempt: a balanced binary search tree. Crack: about 30 reads per lookup, and each read brings in a whole page to use one key, so the fix must use the rest of the page. Fix: fill each node with as many keys as a page holds, a few hundred, so one read narrows the search a few-hundred-fold, and a billion keys need about 4 levels. Name: the number of children per node is the fanout, and a tree built this way is a B-tree."},{"id":"limits","heading":"When the loop is the wrong tool","text":"Everything so far assumes a reader who has to learn the idea. But the loop spends words to buy understanding, and not every reader wants to buy it. Reference material. A reader looking up a field width wants the table, not a story. Put specifications, full tables and edge cases after the narrative or on a page of their own, as this blog does with the DEFLATE format reference . Readers who need the decision, not the idea. Someone approving a design wants the proposal first. This blog's design-doc template puts the proposed design before the alternatives for that reason. Even then, each section should open by saying where it is going. Readers who already know. Help that a newcomer needs can stop helping an expert, and in several studies it lowered their scores; the research calls it the expertise reversal effect (Kalyuga and colleagues, 2003). Experts also find redundant help hard to skip, so fold it away rather than weaving it in, and point them past it, as the line under this page's summary does. Wrong models your reader doesn't hold. Refuting a misconception works when the reader holds it. Refuting one they don't is a detour. Bartosz Ciechanowski's Airfoil builds the right model of lift and leaves the popular wrong explanations to its further reading. Interesting extras. Anecdotes and history added as colour compete with the idea for the reader's attention. Muller and Sharma tested this too: an \"Extended Exposition\" with \"additional interesting information\" did no better than the plain lecture. Put extras in a sidenote, or after the payoff. Your own aha. The analogy that finally made an idea click for you is the residue of hours spent with its details. Handed over without the details, it explains nothing. Brent Yorgey named this the monad tutorial fallacy: \"failing to recognize the critical role that struggling through fundamental details plays in the building of intuition.\" Give the details and examples, and the analogy at the end, as a summary. A toy that teaches the wrong thing. Sanderson traced much of the confusion over his video on Grover's algorithm to an example. \"This is almost certainly exacerbated by me briefly flashing an example function that just checks if the input is 12\", which made the function look as if it already knew the answer. Ask what a literal reader would infer from your example."},{"id":"answer","heading":"Back to the book on the ramp","text":"The question was what to change in a clear, correct explanation that a reader feels good about and cannot use. The student on the ramp had a model before the video started: something has to win for the book to keep moving. The clear video never mentioned that model, so nothing in it collided with it. She heard true sentences, fitted them around what she already believed, and felt she understood better. The Dialogue said her model out loud and broke it on a case. That is a naive attempt and a crack, and it doubled what students learned. So what we change is not the clarity. We change the order, and we ask for commitment. Give the reader the question before the answer and their own wrong answer before the right one; break the wrong answer where the break points at the fix; name the fix last; then ask a question their old model gets wrong. The Wikipedia paragraph needed nothing new for any of this except a count, the reader's own wrong answer, a check and a new order. Here is the whole method in five lines, the four beats and the check: Need: a question, with numbers, that the reader wants answered. Naive attempt: the obvious answer, tried concretely and labelled as a first try. Crack: where it breaks, phrased as the property any fix must have. Fix, then name: the fix in the words of that property, then its technical name. Check: a question the reader's old model answers differently. Nothing here is specific to prose. This blog's template for decision records runs the loop by construction: the context is the need, each option considered is an attempt with its drawbacks as its crack, and the decision is the fix. So does a review comment that shows the failing input before the suggested change, or a commit message that says what broke before what changed. Reach for the loop when you catch yourself writing the answer first: a definition, a rule, a \"simply\". Reach for a check whenever a reader tells you an explanation was clear. As the ramp showed, that is the one thing a reader can say whether or not they learned. Two cautions on how far to trust all this. The evidence comes mostly from short lessons with novices, physics students in the clearest case, tested soon afterwards; the effects are moderate, not miracles. And the core of the method comes from one explainer's habits, Grant Sanderson's, found by counting simple features across 118 of his scripts (sentence length, how often he says \"you\", how often he asks the viewer to predict) and cross-checked against other writers and the research. Treat it as a strong default, and check it against your own readers."},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"The pedagogical-writing skill , in this blog's repository: the full set of moves this page condenses, distilled from 245 3Blue1Brown videos and from other explainers, writing guides and learning research. Its sources file grades each source by the strength of its evidence. Derek Muller and Manjula Sharma, “Tackling misconceptions in introductory physics using multimedia presentations” (UniServe Science proceedings, 2007): the ramp student, the confidence data and the Dialogue, in six readable pages. Christoph Kulgemeyer and Jörg Wittwer, “Misconceptions in Physics Explainer Videos and the Illusion of Understanding” (International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education, 2022): a clear but wrong video felt as convincing as a correct one. Kyle St. Hilaire, Jason Chan and Dahwi Ahn, “Guessing as a learning intervention: a meta-analytic review of the prequestion effect” (Psychonomic Bulletin & Review, 2024): why the predictions on this page ask you to commit. Louis Alfieri and colleagues, “Does discovery-based instruction enhance learning?” (Journal of Educational Psychology, 2011): unassisted discovery against guided discovery. Grant Sanderson (3Blue1Brown), “What makes a great math explanation?” and “Where my explanation of Grover's algorithm failed” : an explainer on his own method, and an honest postmortem of a toy that taught the wrong thing. The other quotes are from “Make math videos!” , “There's never been a better time to go into math” and “But what is quantum computing?” . George Gopen and Judith Swan, “The Science of Scientific Writing” (American Scientist, 1990): topic and stress positions, with worked rewrites. Steven Pinker, The Sense of Style (2014), chapter 3: the curse of knowledge, and why rereading your own prose can't find it. Nicky Case, “How I make an explorable explanation” : BUT and THEREFORE, and a playground built one piece at a time. Amit Patel, “Little design elements” : dozens of small choices that make interactive diagrams teach, including the two quoted here. Brent Yorgey, “Abstraction, intuition, and the ‘monad tutorial fallacy’” : why your analogy is not your reader's explanation. Wikipedia, “Dynamic array” : the paragraph examined here, and a table of the growth factors real languages use."}]},{"href":"docs/token-bucket-rate-limiting.html","title":"Token bucket rate limiting","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer · Rate limiting Token bucket rate limiting How one small data structure lets clients burst when they've been quiet, while still holding them to an average rate — and the production details that the textbook version leaves out. A token bucket lets a client that has been quiet send a burst, and still holds it to an average rate. It needs two numbers per client and no timer. Its two knobs mean something you can choose from your capacity: one sets the largest burst, the other the sustained rate. The common mistake is counting requests in fixed windows. It lets a burst past the limit at the seam between two windows, and no setting fixes that."},{"id":"the-need","heading":"What a rate limiter has to allow","text":"Suppose 200 clients share a backend that serves 2,000 requests per second. Split evenly, each client gets 10 per second . Two of them misbehave in different ways. The dashboard Loading a page fires 25 calls in parallel, then nothing for 30 seconds. That averages under 1 request per second, far below its share of 10. The retry loop A script stuck in a loop sends 500 per second for as long as it runs. That is 50 times its share. A limiter has to admit the dashboard's 25 calls and refuse almost all of the script's 500. That is two demands at once: cap the average at 10 per second, and don't punish a short burst from a client that has been quiet. Let's see how far the obvious rules get."},{"id":"fixed-window","heading":"A first try: count requests per second","text":"The obvious rule is \"at most 10 requests per second\". Keep a counter per client, reset it to zero every second, and refuse a request when the counter is already 10. The state is tiny: a count and the time the window started. Limit: 10 per second, counted in fixed one-second windows. What is the most requests one client can get admitted within a single second? Decide before you open this. 20. The limit only holds inside each window, so a client can spend all 10 at the end of one window and 10 more at the start of the next. Anyone who answered 10 assumed the limit holds for any second you pick. It holds only for the seconds the counter happens to start and stop on. 0.95 s 10 requests arrive The counter for the window 0 to 1 s reaches 10. All are admitted. 1.00 s The counter resets A new window begins at 0. 1.05 s 10 more arrive All are admitted. That is 20 requests in 0.1 seconds, against a limit of 10 per second. That is the first crack, and it comes from the reset. The counter forgets a burst at an arbitrary instant, so a burst that straddles the reset is invisible. The second crack is the dashboard. Its 25 calls arrive in one window, so 15 are refused, even though the client has used nothing in the last 30 seconds. Quiet earns nothing. Both come from judging a request by which clock second it landed in . A better rule would judge it by how much the client has used lately ."},{"id":"sliding-log","heading":"A second try: remember every request","text":"Then do that. Keep the timestamp of every admitted request and admit a new one only if fewer than 10 fall in the last second. There is no seam to hide a burst in: any one-second span holds at most 10. It costs memory. Ten timestamps of 8 bytes is 80 bytes per client, which is 80 MB for a million clients. The cost grows with the limit: at 1,000 per second it is 8 KB per client, or 8 GB. And the dashboard still loses 15 of its 25 calls, because \"10 in the last second\" is as flat as \"10 per window\". Quiet still earns nothing. So the rule we want has two properties the first two tries lack. Unused allowance should build up , up to a cap. And its state should not grow with the limit."},{"id":"mental-model","heading":"The fix: save up what you don't use","text":"Both tries ask how much a client has used . Ask the opposite: how much does it have left to spend ? Picture a bucket under a dripping tap. Each drip is permission for one request. A request may pass only if it can take a drip out. If the bucket is full, the extra drips spill away and are lost. That spill is the whole design. Saving is capped, so a client that has been idle for an hour can't then fire an hour's worth of requests at once. The token bucket is equivalent to GCRA (the generic cell rate algorithm) from ATM networking. GCRA stores one timestamp instead of a token count and a timestamp. Two numbers describe a bucket. Its capacity b is how many tokens it can hold, which is the largest burst. The tap's rate r is how many tokens it adds per second, which is the sustained rate. A client that has been idle for b/r seconds, the time to fill from empty, starts with a full bucket. Here is the picture as a machine you can work. Each coin is a token. The magazine is the bucket, the dial on top is the tap, and the button sends a request. A coin that reaches the service bin was admitted. A coin dropped onto a full magazine spills into the lost bin, and when the magazine is empty the 429 flag goes up. Fig 1 Token bucket Press the button or Space 6 of 6 tokens · 0 admitted · 0 rejected A coin on the move: dropped by the timer, sent on to the service, or lost The six coins the magazine holds A toy bucket that holds six tokens (b = 6), refilled by one every 1.25 s (r = 0.8 per second). Each press takes the bottom coin; with none left the pusher finds nothing and the 429 flag goes up. A coin dropped onto a full magazine is lost. The machine counts whole coins and its drip clock keeps running when the magazine is full, so after a take from a full magazine the next coin can land sooner than the refill rule below allows. This is a token bucket , and the numbers it needs are the ones the story asked for. Give each client b = 25 and r = 10 . The dashboard finds a full bucket, so all 25 calls are admitted and the bucket empties. The retry loop gets its first 25, then one request per tap drip: 10 a second, not 500. In its first second, 35 of its 500 get through: the 25 in the full bucket plus the 10 that dripped in. And there is no seam to hide in, because nothing resets. The state is two numbers per client: the tokens left, and the time of the last visit. Two 8-byte numbers make 16 bytes, which is 16 MB for a million clients, whatever the limit is."},{"id":"no-timer","heading":"But who turns the tap?","text":"A real limiter can't run this machine as it stands. With a million clients it would need a million taps, each dripping 10 times a second: 10 million timer events every second, nearly all for clients that are idle. Nobody looks at a bucket between requests, so nothing needs to drip then. When a request arrives, work out what the tap would have delivered since the last visit, add it, and cap the total at b . The machine above is the picture. In code there is no clock: the drips are worked out from the time that has passed whenever a request arrives."},{"id":"how-it-works","heading":"How a request is decided","text":"Everything now happens at the moment a request arrives. The diagram shows the pieces, and the steps beneath it walk through one request. The limiter is the only part that acts. In code, refill isn't a background process. It is calculated when a request arrives. A request arrives at the limiter with a timestamp now . Refill lazily. Add (now − last) × r tokens and cap the total at b . Anything over the cap spills. Store last = now . Try to take a token. If tokens ≥ 1 , subtract one. A request can cost more than one token. Weight expensive endpoints that way. Admitted. Forward the request. The state for this client is just two numbers: tokens and last . Rejected. Return 429 with Retry-After = (1 − tokens) / r , the seconds until one whole token exists. The client knows exactly how long to wait, so it doesn't need to guess with a backoff."},{"id":"simulation","heading":"Watch it run","text":"Two claims to check: b sets the largest burst, and r sets the sustained rate. The slots show tokens in the bucket, and the partly filled slot is the next token arriving. Beside them, on the right, are the last ten seconds of requests. Set the background traffic, then press Burst ×10 and watch the bucket drain. Then lower r and press it again. Capacity b Refill rate r (tokens/s) Background traffic (req/s) Send 1 Burst ×10 Reset Tokens 0 · admitted 0 · rejected 0 · refill from empty takes s b = 10, r = 1/s, bucket full. A client sends 15 requests at once, then 1 per second. What happens? The first 10 are admitted and the next 5 are rejected. After that, every request at 1 per second is admitted. Each one spends the single token that arrived in the second before it, so the bucket stays almost empty. The client can't burst again until it slows below r and lets tokens build up."},{"id":"choosing-parameters","heading":"Choosing b and r","text":"The simulation shows what the knobs do. The next question is what to set them to. Start from what the downstream system can take, not from what clients would like to send. r The sustained rate you can serve for every client at once, with headroom. The 200 clients sharing a backend of 2,000 req/s each get r = 10, the same 10 per second the first try tried to enforce. b The largest legitimate burst, such as the dashboard's 25 parallel calls. Too small and real users hit 429s. Too large and one client can saturate a replica briefly. A bucket that has just emptied takes b/r = 2.5 s to fill again. cost Requests don't all weigh the same. Charge a report export 20 tokens and a health check 0."},{"id":"implementation","heading":"Implementation","text":"Now the code for the refill-then-take step. The core is about ten lines. The distributed version moves the same arithmetic into Redis, so the read-modify-write is atomic. use std::time::{Duration, Instant}; pub struct TokenBucket { capacity: f64, // b — largest burst rate: f64, // r — tokens per second tokens: f64, last: Instant, // monotonic clock: immune to NTP jumps } impl TokenBucket { pub fn new(capacity: f64, rate: f64) -> Self { Self { capacity, rate, tokens: capacity, last: Instant::now() } } /// Ok(()) if admitted, otherwise Err(how long until `cost` tokens exist). pub fn try_acquire(&mut self, cost: f64) -> Result<(), Duration> { let now = Instant::now(); let elapsed = now.duration_since(self.last).as_secs_f64(); self.tokens = (self.tokens + elapsed * self.rate).min(self.capacity); self.last = now; if self.tokens >= cost { self.tokens -= cost; Ok(()) } else { Err(Duration::from_secs_f64((cost - self.tokens) / self.rate)) } } } type Bucket struct { mu sync.Mutex capacity float64 // b rate float64 // r, tokens per second tokens float64 last time.Time } func (b *Bucket) TryAcquire(cost float64) (ok bool, retryAfter time.Duration) { b.mu.Lock() defer b.mu.Unlock() now := time.Now() // carries a monotonic reading; Sub() uses it b.tokens = math.Min(b.capacity, b.tokens+now.Sub(b.last).Seconds()*b.rate) b.last = now if b.tokens >= cost { b.tokens -= cost return true, 0 } return false, time.Duration((cost - b.tokens) / b.rate * float64(time.Second)) } -- KEYS[1] = bucket key -- ARGV = capacity, rate (tokens/s), now (ms, from the Redis TIME command), cost local b, r, now, cost = tonumber(ARGV[1]), tonumber(ARGV[2]), tonumber(ARGV[3]), tonumber(ARGV[4]) local s = redis.call('HMGET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', 'ts') local tokens = tonumber(s[1]) or b local ts = tonumber(s[2]) or now tokens = math.min(b, tokens + math.max(0, now - ts) / 1000 * r) local ok = tokens >= cost if ok then tokens = tokens - cost end redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', tokens, 'ts', now) redis.call('PEXPIRE', KEYS[1], math.ceil(b / r * 1000) + 1000) -- idle keys vanish when full return { ok and 1 or 0, tostring(tokens) }"},{"id":"alternatives","heading":"How it compares","text":"The two failed tries above are in this table as fixed window and sliding log. The rest are the usual alternatives. Algorithm Bursts State per key Boundary spikes Best for Token bucket Up to b 2 numbers None APIs: tolerate bursts, enforce an average Leaky bucket (queue) Smoothed queue None Shaping output to a fixed rate Fixed window Up to 2× at edges 1 counter Yes Coarse quotas (per day) Sliding log Exact N timestamps None Low volume, exact limits Sliding window counter Approximate 2 counters Small A cheap approximation of the log"},{"id":"gotchas","heading":"Gotchas in production","text":"The arithmetic is the easy part. In production the same ten lines go wrong in three ways. Wall-clock time Measure elapsed time with a monotonic clock. If NTP steps the wall clock backwards, the elapsed time comes out negative. Without a clamp, that can drain the bucket, or in some code mint a huge pile of tokens. Per-node limits multiply Ten gateway replicas, each with its own bucket at r = 100, give a client an effective limit of 1,000. Either share state (the Redis script above) or divide r by the replica count and accept some unfairness. Read-modify-write races A distributed limiter that does GET and then SET as two round trips lets concurrent requests spend the same token twice. Run the update atomically, with a Lua script or a database transaction. Go deeper: why return Retry-After? Clients that get a bare 429 back off blindly, usually with exponential backoff, and often all at the same moment. You already know when the next token will exist, so tell them. That turns a stampede of retries into an orderly queue."},{"id":"back-to-the-clients","heading":"Back to the two clients","text":"The question was how to admit the dashboard's 25 calls and refuse the retry loop's 500 a second. A bucket with b = 25 and r = 10 admits all 25 of the dashboard's, and holds the loop to 10 a second after its first 25. Next time you need a limit, ask two things. Should a quiet client be allowed to burst? And can you afford state that grows with the limit? If the first is yes and the second no, you want a token bucket."},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"Token bucket (Wikipedia) : the formal definition and its relation to the leaky bucket. Generic cell rate algorithm : the same idea, storing a single timestamp. RFC 9110 §10.2.3, Retry-After : the header semantics."}]},{"href":"docs/visibility-threat-model.html","title":"What each visibility level hides, and from whom","kind":"design","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Design document What each visibility level hides, and from whom The site's four visibility levels and its private-corpus guard, set against four adversaries: what each hides, what leaks by design, and the check behind every guarantee. Answer: each level hides a page from some people, never from everyone. Unlisted only keeps the site from pointing a visitor or a crawler at a page, so anyone who looks can still list it; sealing stops everyone without the key; nothing stops someone with the repo from learning that a page exists, how big it is and when it changed. Why it matters: a new feature is safe when it hands none of the four adversaries (visitor, crawler, repo reader, key holder) anything their level was meant to withhold. Every guarantee below names the check or test that holds it, and the ones nothing holds are listed. Common mistake: treating \"unlisted\" as private, or a new key as revoking a leaked one. The old key still opens every version committed under it."},{"id":"hidden-from-whom","heading":"Hidden from whom?","text":"Suppose someone adds a \"Related posts\" box to the foot of every post: the three pages that share the most tags with it. The obvious rule is to build it from every page in docs/ and leave out the ones marked hidden. That rule has no answer yet, because \"hidden\" means four different things here. Built at index time, the box would write an unlisted page's title into the HTML of a listed one, which every visitor loads. It would link a post to an example page, which returns 404 on the published site. On the author's machine it could do worse: a private page's plaintext sits in docs/ beside the public pages, so the box could copy a private title into a public one. In CI, where only the sealed copy exists, the same code would find no tags for that page at all. Each of those is a different failure, against a different person. So the question a feature has to answer is not \"is this page hidden?\" but \"hidden from whom, and does this feature hand that person something new?\" That needs a fixed list of who is looking. The rest of this page is that list, what each level withholds from each of them, and the code that keeps it so."},{"id":"adversaries","heading":"Four people who might look","text":"Each has everything the one before has, and more: the last is a repo reader who also holds a key. Casual visitor Uses the published site in a browser: follows links, uses search, edits the address, reads the page source and the files the page loads. Does not dig through the repository. Crawler Fetches addresses it finds in links and in sitemap.xml , and honours robots.txt and the robots meta tag. Chat apps' link-preview bots count here: they read a page's head to draw a card. Repo reader Clones the public repository with its whole history: every source page, every generated file, every commit. The strongest adversary who holds no key. Key holder Has everything a repo reader has, plus one #k= link to a sealed page, whether it was shared with them on purpose or leaked. The author's own machine, the 1Password account and anyone who can push to main are trusted, not adversaries. Out of scope says what that excludes."},{"id":"levels","heading":"What each level hides from whom","text":"Here are the four levels against the four people. Each cell says, in words, what that person gets. Read across a row: no level hides a page from everyone. Read down the repo column: only sealing withholds content from someone with the repo, and even then not the slug, the size or when it changed. Two cells carry most of the misunderstandings. An unlisted page is readable by anyone with its address ; the level only keeps the site from pointing at it. And a key holder reads more than the current page , for a reason What a key opens explains."},{"id":"normal-and-example","heading":"Normal and example pages","text":"A normal page hides nothing from anyone: it is in the catalogue, search, the feed and the sitemap, and it has a social card. It is here as the baseline the other rows are measured against. An example page only demonstrates the theme, so it is hidden from readers of the published site , and not from the repo : its source and its entries in the committed site data are public. Guarantee Mechanism Enforced by Hidden by default in the browser on every host, local ones included; only ?examples shows them. resolve in theme/examples.js sets <html data-examples> before first paint. cmdCheck ( scripts/bh.mts ) requires the script in every head, after the marker. Tests in tests/examples.test.mts : \"examples are hidden by default on every host, local ones included\", \"check rejects the `examples` tag, a bad marker value and a missing examples.js\", \"check requires the marker to come before examples.js\". Absent from the published site: page and card deleted, links to it reduced to their text, and the index, search, backlinks and reviews rebuilt without it. preparePublishedSite in scripts/publish.mts , run by the deploy job in .github/workflows/ci.yml . tests/publish.test.mts : \"published artifact omits examples, keeps posts and local source, and serves 404\", \"publication fails loudly for no active markers, mismatched pages and missing cards\". tests/hidden-pages.test.mts checks that publish, check and the feed agree about which links to hide. Its slug is in no published list of the social cards: social-cards.txt , which records every card's key by slug, examples included, is not part of the site. CARD_RECORDS_FILE in scripts/card-records.mts puts the file at the root of the repository; the deploy job copies only index.html , docs/ and theme/ into the site. \"records: the committed file is in canonical form, outside the published directories\" ( tests/card-records.test.mts ) fails when the file moves out of the root, or a copy into _site in .github/workflows/ci.yml could take it along. Never in the feed or the sitemap, even in the repo. feedPosts and sitemapPages in scripts/bh.mts drop it. check fails on an ineligible entry ( feedTextProblems , rootFileProblems ). Tests: \"index keeps examples, unlisted, reference and sealed pages out of the feed, and escapes text\" ( feed.test.mts ), \"index keeps example and unlisted pages out of the sitemap and robots.txt, and dates the entries\" ( sitemap.test.mts ). No record of its earlier versions is published: the diff data behind the Review panel's \"changes since\" is written only for pages the site carries. writeReviewDiffs in scripts/page-history.mts reads the staged pages, after preparePublishedSite has removed the examples. \"the published diff data: one file per reviewed page and commit, none for a sealed page, an unknown commit or a page the site does not carry\" ( tests/page-diff.test.mts ). An element marked data-example inside a page is drawn only while example pages are shown, and the same holds in a page diff's copy of an earlier version of it. This decides what is drawn, not what is sent: the element's text is in the page's HTML and in the diff data either way. The theme's rule on <html data-examples> ( theme/components.css ) reaches a copy in the page. A copy drawn in a shadow tree is out of its reach, so theme/modules/diff.js repeats the rule there ( HIDDEN_CSS ) and writes the switch onto the tree's content ( mirrorSwitches ). The copy hides the element unless that says on. \"a copy in a shadow tree hides an unlisted or example element whenever the page hides it\" ( tests/page-diff-browser.test.mts , in Chrome: skipped where there is none). It checks each switch on and off, a tree that was never told, and a rule of the page's own that would show the element."},{"id":"unlisted","heading":"Unlisted pages: discoverability, not access","text":"An unlisted page is published at its normal address and is fully readable there. What the level withholds is the site's help in finding it: no card, no search result, no backlink, no live link from a listed page, and a polite request to crawlers. A casual visitor who never opens the repo can still list the title of every unlisted page, with one request. Fetching theme/unlisted-pages.js gives the whole list. So does opening any page with #unlisted=true : that turns the flag on, and the home page then shows a card for every unlisted page. The flag is not a secret: it is written in this page and in the theme. The default page load really does carry no unlisted title, but that default protects against stumbling on a page, not against looking for one. Use a sealed page when the content must stay secret. Guarantee Mechanism Enforced by Off the home page, header search, backlinks and review summaries by default. Among the site's data files, its title and search text are only in theme/unlisted-pages.js , and its review threads only in theme/unlisted-reviews.js . (The page itself and its social card carry the title too.) buildSiteData , cmdIndex and compileReviews in scripts/bh.mts . check fails while any generated file differs from a fresh build. tests/unlisted.test.mts : \"new --unlisted writes the marker; index keeps the page out of every default file and is idempotent\", \"backlinks and review summaries that touch an unlisted page stay out of site.js\". With the flag off, the browser never requests theme/unlisted-pages.js . loadUnlisted in theme/bh.js . Nothing. See unenforced guarantees . The flag is off by default everywhere, local hosts included, and is decided before first paint. resolve and parseFragment in theme/unlisted.js . check requires the script after examples.js and after the marker. Tests: \"the flag is off by default everywhere, local hosts included\", \"check accepts a well-formed unlisted page, and fails or warns on each fault\". A link to it from a listed page is inert: no href , not focusable. hideUnlistedLinks in theme/bh.js , which reads paths only from theme/unlisted-slugs.js . check warns on such a link at the source (same test as above). The browser behaviour: nothing . Crawlers are asked to skip it, and nothing lists it for them. noindex, nofollow in its generated social block ( expectedSocial ); out of sitemap.xml ; robots.txt names no page ( buildRobots ). check fails on a stale social block or sitemap. Tests: the new --unlisted test above asserts the noindex ; \"index keeps example and unlisted pages out of the sitemap and robots.txt, and dates the entries\". Out of the feed and bundle --all . listedDocFiles feeds both. The feed test above; \"bundle --all leaves out an unlisted page, and naming it bundles it\". A decision chain is all listed or all unlisted, so a listed page's \"superseded\" banner never shows an unlisted title. The hero's list and banner, which npm run index writes into the page, name the other page of a chain by its title only when it is listed (an example only on another example), and by its path otherwise; bh.js puts the title in only from the data the reader has. The decision-link block of cmdCheck ; lifecycleLink in scripts/bh.mts . \"check requires the home page's list to load the unlisted cards, and keeps decision chains whole\"; \"a decision link names a page that every reader of this one may see; an unlisted or example page is named by its path\" ( tests/hero-meta.test.mts ). The published site keeps the page and its card, and the same exclusions. preparePublishedSite reruns index on the staged copy. \"published artifact keeps unlisted pages and their cards, and leaves them out of the rebuilt index, search and backlinks\" ( publish.test.mts ). What the page said at the commits its review threads name is published as diff data, theme/diffs/<slug>.<commit>.js : named by the page's own slug, requested only by that page, and listed nowhere. writeReviewDiffs ( scripts/page-history.mts ); load in theme/"},{"id":"sealed","heading":"Sealed pages: content, not existence","text":"A private page is written as a git-ignored docs/<slug>.private.html . npm run seal gzips it and encrypts it with AES-256-GCM under a random 256-bit key, and commits only the result, docs/<slug>.html : a fixed shell around the ciphertext. The key travels in the link's fragment, which browsers never send to a server. So the shell withholds the page's content and its card from everyone without the key, and it cannot withhold the file itself. Guarantee Mechanism Enforced by The plaintext is never committed. docs/*.private.html in .gitignore . A git add -f goes past that, so trackedPrivatePlaintext and commitLeaks ( scripts/private-leaks.mts ) look for the name in the index and in the paths a pushed commit adds. \"git ignores docs/<slug>.private.html, and a public page beside it is not ignored\" ( tests/private-leaks.test.mts ) pins the ignore rule. check fails on any tracked *.private.html (\"private page rule: a force-added docs/*.private.html fails check\", tests/checker-rules.test.mts ). The pre-push hook ( leak-check --pushed ) and the Leak check workflow ( --hashed ) refuse a commit that adds one, with or without the private corpus or the CI secret (\"pre-push, with no private data: a force-added docs/*.private.html, and a #k= key in a file or a commit message, are refused without printing the key\", tests/githooks.test.mts ). The hook holds only where npm run hooks was run; CI holds after the push, so a branch that reaches GitHub is refused but already public. At deploy, the deploy job refuses a *.private.html in the site, and addGoatcounter ( scripts/publish.mts ) refuses one when counting is on. No #k= key is in a committed file or a commit message. KEY_PATTERN in scripts/private-leaks.mts : #k= or &k= and the 43 base64url characters of a 32-byte key (a longer run holds one too). It reads every blob and every commit message, and any annotated tag's message, that a push or change adds, and prints the place, never the key. The pre-push hook and the Leak check workflow, as above, with no corpus and no secret needed; the tests in tests/private-leaks.test.mts and tests/githooks.test.mts , and \"the workflow's step refuses a #k= key in a commit message of the range on every event, with or without the secret\" ( tests/leak-hashes.test.mts ). Nothing sees a pull request's title or body, an issue or a comment. The committed copy is the current plaintext, encrypted. cmdSeal ; the shell carries sealDigest , an HMAC of the plaintext under the key. check recomputes the digest where the key is at hand and fails on a stale or never-sealed page. Tests: \"grouped pages share one key, and sealing is idempotent\" ( private-cards.test.mts ), \"a stale seal and an unreadable key are still reported with the lookups ahead of time\" ( check-keys.test.mts ). Outside the payload and digest, every sealed page is byte-identical, so no title, description, tag or group name can show; a link preview reads \"Private page\". sealedShell in scripts/bh.mts . cmdCheck compares each sealed file with the generic shell, and searches it for the local plaintext's title, description and group name. The group-name case is tested (\"check accepts a clean site and fails each injected fault\"); the shell comparison and the title and description searches have no test of their own. Crawlers are told to skip it; it is never in the feed or sitemap. noindex, nofollow in the shell; docFiles leaves sealed shells out of every generator. check : \"sealed page must be noindex\" (no test of its own). The feed test above, and \"the committed sitemap lists the home page and every surfaced page, and check accepts all three files\". Out of the catalogue, search, backlinks, social cards and bundle --all . docFiles and listedDocFiles ; orphanCards finds a card with no public page, and cardRecordProblems a record in social-cards.txt with none. check compares the generated files, and fails on an orphan card or record. The orphan record has a test, \"card record rule: a record of no public page\" ( tests/chec"},{"id":"key-reach","heading":"What a key opens","text":"A key's reach is wider than one page in two directions. Across pages: several private pages can share a group key, and anyone with it opens every page in the group and sees every card in it on the home page. Back in time: the key stays the same across re-seals, so that old links keep working, which means it also decrypts every version of the page that was ever committed under it. So a leaked key cannot be taken back. Re-sealing under a new key protects only the commits after it, and deleting the page leaves every earlier commit in place. Rewriting the repo's history removes those commits from GitHub, but not from a clone or fork made before the rewrite, and the repo is public, so there is no knowing who has one. Treat a leaked key as a permanent disclosure of everything it ever encrypted. The key holder never gets other groups' pages or cards: each page's payload is encrypted under its own key, or its group's, and a card blob opens only under that key and that slug."},{"id":"corpus","heading":"The private corpus","text":"One more thing must never become public, and it is not a page: the writers' posts and everything derived from them, kept under the git-ignored corpus/private . The adversary is the repo reader, and the mechanism is that nothing derived from that text is committed. Guarantee Mechanism Enforced by Git tracks nothing under corpus/ but the README, each sources.yaml and the numbers-only model files. .gitignore covers corpus/private and corpus/*/posts/ . Any other new file under corpus/ would be staged: only the allow-list ALLOWED_CORPUS_FILE in the test catches it. tests/advisor-guards.test.mts : \"git tracks nothing under corpus/ except the README, sources.yaml and the two model files\", \"git ignores corpus/private as a symlink and as a directory, and everything in a data repo stays out\". The published site holds no corpus path and no private text. The deploy job copies only index.html , docs , theme and the review schema. \"the published site holds no corpus path and nothing from the private data\", which reads the copy list from the workflow itself. A push that adds a run of 8 or more words from the private text is blocked, and the report never prints the words. .githooks/pre-push runs leakCheckPushed in scripts/advisor/guards.mts ( LEAK_RUN_WORDS ) on the blobs the push adds. tests/githooks.test.mts : \"pre-push: a push with an injected run is rejected without printing its words, and passes once it is removed\", and the tests after it. Only where the hook runs: see below. The last row holds only on a clone that ran npm run hooks and has the private data linked. It fails open: without the hook setting nothing runs at all, and with the hook but no linked data it prints that no corpus text was checked and lets that text through (the private-page key and plaintext checks still run), so an unlinked checkout skips the check on every push without failing. A checkout with no node_modules (a new git worktree has none) is the one case where the hook cannot run the check and blocks instead: it prints a line saying to run npm ci there, because a skipped check would look like a passed one. git push --no-verify skips it too, and so does a merge made on GitHub. The Leak check workflow ( .github/workflows/leak-check.yml ) covers those in CI. It compares every pushed branch and pull request with corpus/leak-hashes.txt , keyed hashes of the 8-word runs whose key is a GitHub secret, so the list confirms no guess. Until the owner sets that key, it passes with a notice that nothing was compared."},{"id":"by-design","heading":"What leaks by design","text":"These are known and accepted. A feature that makes one of them worse needs a reason; one that adds a new kind needs a line here. Every slug. Sealed pages' filenames, and their slugs again in theme/private-cards.js . Unlisted pages' paths in theme/unlisted-slugs.js , which any listed page may load, and in the GoatCounter dashboard, which counts unlisted pages like any other. To a repo reader, every public page's slug, example and unlisted ones included, in social-cards.txt , as in the file names in docs/assets/og/ ; the rest of each line is hashes. Sizes. A sealed payload is the page after gzip, plus 28 bytes of IV and tag, written in base64 (four thirds of that); a card blob is the same for its card. Edit times and count. How many pages are sealed, and from the history, when each was re-sealed: the ciphertext and digest change only when the plaintext or the key does. All of an unlisted page , to anyone who opens a page with #unlisted=true or fetches theme/unlisted-pages.js . Its title is also drawn on its social card, docs/assets/og/<slug>.png , which is published like any other. All of an example page , to a repo reader. The earlier text of a reviewed page. For each commit that a merged review thread names, the published site carries what the page said then, text deleted since included ( theme/diffs/ ). The public history holds it either way; this puts it one request from the page. Links in those copies are plain text, so a link to a removed example page does not come back. An element that the page draws only with the unlisted or the example switch on is in that text like any other: the switch decides what a copy draws, not what the file holds. A group key's reach : every page and card in the group, and a key's reach into history : every version it encrypted. What GoatCounter receives for each public page view: the path, the title, the referrer, the screen size and the user agent. Which pages share a group is not written in clear anywhere, but pages re-sealed in the same commit hint at it."},{"id":"out-of-scope","heading":"Out of scope","text":"Defended The four adversaries above, using the published site and the public repo. Mistakes by the author that a check can see before a commit or deploy. Not defended A compromised browser or device, the author's or a reader's: the tab keeps keys in sessionStorage , and browser history may keep a full #k= address. The 1Password account and its desktop integration. Anyone who can push to main or change the workflows: they can change any mechanism. GitHub and GitHub Pages as hosts, and GoatCounter's own script, which is not pinned and which this site trusts to drop the fragment. Where a #k= link goes once shared: chat logs, mail archives, preview services."},{"id":"unenforced","heading":"Guarantees no check enforces","text":"Each of these is a promise the code keeps today but nothing would catch it breaking. They are candidates for a check or a test, not known bugs. No corpus text is pushed. Until the CI leak check has its key, the pre-push hook is the only check, and it passes open: a clone without core.hooksPath set pushes unchecked and says nothing, and one without the private data linked pushes corpus text unchecked after a single line saying so. Nothing fails when the check did not run, except in a checkout with no node_modules , where the hook blocks and says to run npm ci . With the flag off, no unlisted title reaches the browser. The generated files are tested; that bh.js never requests theme/unlisted-pages.js is not. Links to an unlisted page are inert in the browser. check warns at the source; the rewriting in hideUnlistedLinks is untested. The sealed shell takes the key out of the address bar. The browser test opens a sealed page but never looks at the address bar afterwards. The exact shell comparison does not help: remove the history.replaceState call and every shell merely reads as outdated, which npm run seal -- --reshell clears. Several rules that check does enforce have no test that fails when the rule is removed: the generic-shell comparison and the title and description searches, \"sealed page must be noindex\", a public page linking to a private one, a review of a private page, an orphan card, a public page sharing a slug with a private source, seal refusing to overwrite a public page, and the absence of private pages from theme/site.js and theme/search-index.js ."},{"id":"judging","heading":"Judging a new feature","text":"Back to the \"Related posts\" box. Run it through the four people: List what it adds , file by file: new markup in a page, a new field in a generated file, a new request, a new outside service. For each addition, ask each adversary what it now gives them that their level was meant to withhold. The box written into every post at index time gives a casual visitor unlisted titles; built in the browser from theme/site.js , it gives them nothing new, because that file has none. Check the levels it reads from. It must take its pages from the same lists as the catalogue ( listedDocFiles , without examples) and never from a private source. If it links to pages, ask which are hidden through hiddenHrefs in scripts/hidden-pages.mts , not a copy of the rule: its HIDDEN_RULE table says what the feed, check and publish.mts each do with an example, unlisted, sealed or private page, and tests/hidden-pages.test.mts runs all three against it. Name the check that will hold it , add a row to the right table above, and if nothing holds it, add it to the list above . The trigger for reaching for this page is any change that writes a page's title, path or text somewhere new: a generated file, a page's head, a request, a third party. That is where every leak listed here started."}]},{"href":"docs/voice-notes-on-trial.html","title":"Voice notes on trial: a verdict can't settle a clash of norms","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer Voice notes on trial: a verdict can't settle a clash of norms A Guardian column asks readers whether a partner's long voice notes are guilty. With Duncan Sabien's ideas, a better question appears: what does each message mean to each person? When two people disagree about how to talk to each other, asking which of them is guilty means picking one person's habits as the rule. Ask instead what each message means to each of them, and what arrangement would cost both of them least. A verdict comes with advice but not with reasons both people can check. Saying \"this is how it works for me\", and agreeing when to use which channel, gives each of them something to do tomorrow. The common mistake: assuming a message has one true meaning that a fair jury could find. A heart emoji, a curt text or an eight-minute voice note can mean one thing to the sender and another to the reader. What you're reading This page critiques a newspaper column. It summarises the column in its own words and quotes only short phrases where the exact wording matters. Read the original, with its illustration and the full reader verdicts, at The Guardian . The critique applies Duncan Sabien's published essays, with his permission. Two kinds of text appear, set apart on purpose. Quotations from his essays have a solid bar and a link to the essay. Passages in a dashed box are simulated commentary from a \"shoulder advisor\": a language model that drafts in Duncan's voice, working only from his published writing. They are not his words, and he has not written about this column. Every quotation, and every reference to a particular passage, has a dotted underline. Hover over it, or tap it, to see it in context: the essay's surrounding passage, or, for the column, a paraphrase of what surrounds it."},{"id":"the-case","heading":"The case: ten minutes of voice note","text":"Every week, the Guardian's You be the judge column puts a couple's disagreement on trial. One partner prosecutes, the other defends, five readers give verdicts, and everyone else votes in a poll. On 29 October 2026 the charge was voice notes. The interviews are by Georgina Lawton. The column changes the couple's names. Ayo and Elena have been together a year. They live apart and he travels for work. Both say they get on well in person; the trouble is the phone. Ayo's case. He calls himself \"a simple man\" and would rather talk on a call, where he can take things in as they come. Elena sends a lot of voice notes, some for questions a text would answer. By the end of a long one he has forgotten the start. On work trips he sometimes forgets to reply at all. He thinks she reads too much into texts, and calls the habit unhealthy. When he's blunt she assumes he's angry; he says he's just \"a bit less smiley on text\" . Her longest note ran twelve minutes. Once he answered a note with a heart, because he was on the move, and asked for a call two days later. She was annoyed. Elena's case. She sometimes thinks \"simple man\" is code for a bad communicator. Because they live apart, a voice note sent while she walks or cooks gives him \"a window into the life you're leading\" . A call needs both of them free at once; a note doesn't. She denies analysing his full stops. He is blunt over text, she says, and then surprised when she wonders whether he's annoyed. Her request is small: don't answer eight minutes with one line, and don't reply to the first thing she said while missing something important later on. She adds that he sometimes calls when she's busy and then complains that she doesn't pick up. The twelve-minute note was never repeated, \"because he ignored it\" . Predict: of the five reader-jurors, how many side mainly with Ayo? None or one, two or three, or four or five? Pick one before you open this. Three. Nicola, Kitty and Margie find mainly against Elena. Daniel finds for her. Bryony splits it: Ayo could show more interest, and Elena could send fewer long notes. If you said four or five, you probably think a twelve-minute note is plainly too long, and Kitty agrees with you. If you said none or one, you probably think a partner sharing her day deserves a hearing whatever the medium, which is Daniel's reason. Neither guess is unreasonable, and that is what the rest of this page is about. \"Who is guilty?\" has an answer only once you've decided what normal looks like, and the jurors decided differently."},{"id":"a-first-try","heading":"A first try: put it to a jury","text":"The column's method is the obvious one, so let's take it seriously before we criticise it. When two people disagree about what's reasonable, ask some outsiders. If most of them think twelve-minute voice notes are too much, maybe they are. The five verdicts, each reduced to its reason. Read the column for them in full. Juror Leans Reason, in brief Nicola Ayo Notes are fine for friends; Ayo doesn't like them, so save them for friends. Kitty Ayo Twelve minutes is \"an act of emotional terrorism\" , though two days without a reply is poor form too. Bryony Both Calls are hard to arrange; each could give a little. Daniel Elena She's sharing her life with care; the medium doesn't matter. Margie Ayo She finds listening to voice notes a chore herself. Now look at the reasons rather than the verdicts. Margie's reason is her own taste. Nicola's is Ayo's taste, treated as the rule. Kitty's starts from a point where twelve minutes is plainly outrageous; Daniel's from a point where any sharing is welcome. Four of the five measure the couple against an idea of normal that they don't argue for, and the four ideas differ. Bryony is the exception: she proposes a trade, which is where this page ends up too. That's the crack in the jury method. Each verdict comes with advice, but the advice rests on the juror's own idea of normal, so Ayo and Elena can't check it against anything they share. Whatever replaces the jury has to start from what each message means to these two people ."},{"id":"no-single-meaning","heading":"The crack, named: a message has no single meaning","text":"Duncan Sabien has an essay on exactly this failure, and its example is a text message. \"There is not, in fact, a single clear and obvious and unambiguous meaning to a text reply that is just the letter 'K.'\" Duncan Sabien, Single Answers Make You Stupid You can get partway, the essay says, with clumsy proxies : check with ten people you know, and find that six read the \"K\" the same way. That is a poll. The same essay tells a story about what a poll's answer is worth . The US Air Force measured thousands of pilots to build a cockpit for the average one. When someone checked how many pilots were within 30% of the average on all ten of the most important measurements, the answer was none. The fix was adjustable seats. The story is from the essay; this page doesn't check its history. The essay also grants that when you're forced to pick one option, choosing what suits the median person is sensible. A newspaper poll is forced to return one number; Ayo and Elena aren't. Put those together and we can see what the column built. Its jury is the ten-people proxy. Its verdict is a fixed seat: it decides the shape of the right partner and asks whoever doesn't fit to change shape. The simulated advisor makes the same link. Simulated Duncan · not his words That proxy is the jury . Margie dislikes voice notes herself, so Elena is guilty. Nicola tells her to save the \"mini podcasts\" for friends. That's the cockpit built for the average pilot, and nobody actually sits in it."},{"id":"zero-points","heading":"Everyone stands on a different zero","text":"A second essay names what each juror is doing when they pick a normal. In Setting the Zero Point , Duncan's example is a drop of five points . Five points from 87 to 82 is a dip; from 2 to −3 it's a slide from good to bad. The change is the same, but where you put zero decides the story. Choosing zero is a move in an argument, usually made without saying so. Here is the same move with the couple's messages. Same messages, opposite signs. Measured from each person's normal as they describe it, what sits at zero on one line is a loss on the other, so each can truthfully say the other started it. Positions show direction, not size. Each of them is right about their own line. A long note does cost Ayo more than a call. A one-line answer to eight minutes does tell Elena her news didn't land. Both give good reasons about their own costs. What neither defends is why their line should be the one the other is measured on. Simulated Duncan · not his words Nobody argues for where their zero goes. They just stand on it."},{"id":"mindreading","heading":"Where the meanings go wrong: mindreading in both directions","text":"If a message has no single meaning, each reader supplies one, and that's where the couple's specific complaints come from. Duncan's essay Mindreading Fails Both Ways separates two ways it goes wrong . You can expect to be read : assume the other person will understand what you never said. Or you can believe you can read them , and treat your interpretation as what they meant. \"…they believe they can read my mind, and are interpreting me as sending secret subtle messages that I am not, in fact, sending.\" Duncan Sabien, Mindreading Fails Both Ways Predict: Ayo answered a voice note with a heart and asked for a call two days later. From the column alone, can you tell what he meant the heart to say? Yes or no? Commit before you open this. No. All he tells us is that he was on the move. Whether the heart meant \"got it, I'll call\", \"lovely\" or \"can't talk\", the column doesn't say, and neither, apparently, did he. We also can't tell whether Elena was annoyed by the heart or by the two-day wait; Kitty, for one, faults the wait. If you said yes, notice where the meaning came from: you supplied it, as Elena had to. That is how expecting to be read works. The sender knows the context (busy, on a train, will call later). The reader gets a heart. Each signal: what the sender says, how the other took it, and which failure fits. A dash means the column doesn't say. Signal The sender's account How it was taken Which failure Ayo's heart, then a call two days later He was on the move Elena was annoyed Ayo expected to be read Ayo's blunt texts \"a bit less smiley on text\" , not angry Elena wonders whether he's annoyed Ayo expected his tone to read as neutral. Elena's wondering is a question, not a conclusion Something important, minutes into a note — Ayo answers the first thing and misses it Possibly Elena expected to be read: the column doesn't say whether she flagged it Elena's long voice notes \"a window into the life you're leading\" Ayo: hard to take in, easy to forget None: a real difference in cost Elena's reaction to texts She denies analysing every full stop Ayo calls it unhealthy Ayo believes he can read her Ayo's \"simple man\" He values direct communication Elena sometimes thinks it's code for a bad communicator Elena reads his motives, though she labels it a suspicion No reply to the 12-minute note — Elena: \"he ignored it\" Elena assumes a reason she wasn't given The pattern is evenhanded. Each of them makes at least one claim about what's going on inside the other. \"Unhealthy\" is a diagnosis of Elena's mind; \"code for a bad communicator\" is a guess at Ayo's motives, offered more tentatively. Neither is something the speaker observed. And each may expect a signal of their own to carry more than it does. One row is different, and it matters later. The long voice notes aren't a misreading. They really do cost Ayo more than they cost Elena, and both of them say so. So the dispute has two parts. The mindreading can be fixed by saying more. The cost difference can't; it needs a trade."},{"id":"the-fix","heading":"The fix: say it as your culture, not as the law","text":"So the fix needs two properties. It has to stop treating either person's normal as the standard, and it has to give each of them words for their costs that the other can act on. Duncan's essay In My Culture offers a phrase with both. You preface a norm with \"in my culture\", and so describe how things work for you without legislating for anyone else. Let's call a sentence like that a culture statement . \"…sharing something about yourself is distinct from making a request of others…\" Duncan Sabien, In My Culture Both of them already say true things about themselves. \"I just hate voice notes\" is a fact about Ayo; Elena's wish that he not answer eight minutes with one line is a fair, specific request. The column turns both into charges. As culture statements they become information: \"in my culture, a long note is hard work to take in\", and \"in my culture, a one-line answer to a long message means you didn't listen\". Does that mean nobody can ever be in the wrong? The essay says no. When Alex does something and Bailey gets angry, it says, \"the fix could be on either side\" , and it lists what decides it: what actually happened, how reasonable the reaction was, and whether a boundary was crossed that both had acknowledged, or that had been \"previously-communicated-and-agreed-upon\" . That last test is the telling one here. Ayo and Elena have never agreed a norm for voice notes. There was nothing for either of them to break, which is why a verdict is premature rather than impossible. When two people do negotiate, the essay lists the outcomes: one side's norms, the other's, a compromise, or \"a set of triggers for distinguishing when to do A versus when to do B\" . A trigger , in that sense, is an agreed rule for when to do which: here, when a voice note is the right channel, when a text is, and when a call is. But the essay adds something easy to skip. More often there's no negotiation . Each side now knows how its actions land, and that knowledge doesn't compel anyone to change. That limits Elena's request. Between acquaintances, \"now you know\" would be the end of it. Whether a relationship is a place where it isn't, where each owes the other a negotiation, is the question we come back to at the end ."},{"id":"messages-as-bills","heading":"A message can come with a bill","text":"The cost difference needs one more idea. In Obligated to Respond , Duncan argues that a message can create an obligation to answer, and that the sender should own that obligation rather than create it and then deny it . The essay is about comments posted in public, where a reply that never comes is seen by onlookers, so we're borrowing its idea of cost, not its setting. Even borrowed, it gives Ayo a way to state his complaint without insulting Elena. \"…it's not your questions are bad, it's your questions are costly, and I don't have the spare resources to pay the costs; I'd like to not keep receiving bills and invoices from you…\" Duncan Sabien, Obligated to Respond (the essay's emphasis removed) The essay's practical suggestion is to let the pressure off in the message itself, with an opener like \"please feel free to skip over this if it's too time-consuming\" . A voice note can do the same: \"no reply needed, I'm just on my walk\". The other half is this page's addition: a note that does need an answer can say so first, so Ayo knows which kind he's holding."},{"id":"what-they-could-say","heading":"What each of them could say next","text":"Put the pieces together and you get statements instead of verdicts. The simulated advisor drafted one for each of them to a pattern this page asked for: a culture statement, the speaker's own cost, one retracted guess about the other's mind, one thing the speaker owns, and a proposal with a trigger. The pattern is ours, not the essays'. Simulated Duncan, drafting for Ayo · not Duncan's or Ayo's words \"In my culture, a long voice note is a bill I can't pay on the move. By minute three I've lost minute one. That's my cost, not a flaw in your notes. I said you read texts in a way that isn't healthy. That was a guess about what's going on inside you, and I take it back. And on work trips I forget to reply at all. That's on me. A proposal: from now on, a heart means 'got it, can't engage yet.' And within a day, you get a real reply to whatever you flag.\" Simulated Duncan, drafting for Elena · not Duncan's or Elena's words \"In my culture, voice notes are how I let you into my day. When eight minutes gets a one-line reply, it lands as 'you didn't listen.' That's my cost. I said 'simple man' was code for 'bad communicator.' That was a guess about what's going on inside you, and I take it back. And I send voice notes for quick questions that could be texts. That's on me. A proposal: anything I need answered, I'll open with 'one thing I need from you.' And from now on, quick logistics go by text.\" Let's check the scripts against the table of signals . \"Unhealthy\" and \"code for\" are withdrawn, so the two read-their-mind rows go. The heart gets a meaning both can know, and \"one thing I need from you\" makes sure the important part of a note is flagged, so the two expect-to-be-read rows go too. The row that was never a misreading, the difference in cost, is handled by trade: she moves logistics to text and leads with what she needs; he commits to a reply within a day. One row is left open: Ayo's blunt texts. An earlier draft from the advisor had a trigger for it too: when a text reads blunt, Elena asks, \"Annoyed, or just texting?\" Nothing in any of this needed a verdict."},{"id":"limits","heading":"What this reading can't tell you","text":"We have two edited statements, not two people. The column gives each partner a few hundred words, with names changed. Everything above is about Ayo and Elena as the column presents them, not their actual psychology or relationship. The essays are about other situations. None of them is about voice notes or a couple's dispute like this one. Obligated to Respond is about public comments, and its costs depend on onlookers; a private channel has none (though the column, by publishing who failed to reply to whom, supplies some). And one step is an extrapolation. In My Culture says you can leave any conversation \"unless you've made a specific commitment to the contrary\" , and its example of owing nothing is closure after a break-up . Whether an ongoing relationship is such a commitment, so that partners owe each other more attention, and a negotiation, than strangers do, is the real question under the column. The essay doesn't settle it. The commentary is simulated. The dashed passages come from a language model working from Duncan's published writing. The quotations with solid bars are his, and each links to its source; everything else is this page's reading."},{"id":"your-turn","heading":"Your turn","text":"Here's a check you can try on yourself, aimed at the step this page cares about: turning a verdict into a culture statement and a trigger. Optional exercise: a colleague sends long, detailed chat messages; you'd rather have a five-minute call. You're tempted to say \"Your messages are way too long.\" Rewrite it as a culture statement plus a trigger. Draft one before opening this. One version: \"In my culture, anything longer than a screen is easier for me as a quick call. It's not that your messages are bad, they just cost me more to read than to hear. If it needs a decision, put the question in the first line, and I'll answer that day.\" Check yours for three things. Does it describe you rather than rule on them? Does it name the cost? Does it give a trigger they can follow without guessing? A rewrite that still says \"too long\" is still a verdict. If yours only describes you, it's half done: the colleague learns your preference but not what to do differently."},{"id":"verdict","heading":"So, is Ayo right?","text":"Back to the poll. Ayo is right that long voice notes cost him more than they cost Elena. Elena is right that a one-line answer to eight minutes of her day tells her he didn't listen. Each is wrong where they guess at the other's mind. And the question the column asks, guilty or innocent, comes too early. There's no agreed norm yet for either of them to have broken, so any verdict just picks whose normal counts. The useful questions are what each message means to each of them, and which arrangement costs both of them least. The cue to take away: when you catch yourself asking who is in the wrong about how someone communicates, check whether you're standing on different zeros. If you are, a verdict won't help either of you. Say what your culture is, name what it costs you, and agree on when to do which."},{"id":"sources","heading":"Sources","text":"Georgina Lawton (interviews), \"You be the judge: Should my girlfriend stop sending me 10-minute voice notes?\" , The Guardian , 29 October 2026. The column critiqued here; read it for both statements and all five verdicts in full. Duncan Sabien, Single Answers Make You Stupid : the \"K\" text and the average pilot. Duncan Sabien, Setting the Zero Point : where \"normal\" sits decides what counts as a loss. Duncan Sabien, Mindreading Fails Both Ways : expecting to be read, and believing you can read others. Duncan Sabien, In My Culture : describing a norm without imposing it; how to decide where a fix belongs; triggers. Duncan Sabien, Obligated to Respond : messages that create obligations, and owning them."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#adr","title":"ADR","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"ADRs, architecture decision record, architecture decision records","text":"An architecture decision record: a short, permanent note of one significant decision, covering its context, the options considered and the consequences. ADRs are superseded by new records rather than edited."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#back-reference","title":"Back-reference","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"back-references, backreference, backreferences","text":"In LZ77 compression, an instruction to copy L bytes starting D bytes back in the output already produced, instead of storing them again. DEFLATE allows distances up to 32,768 bytes. Step by step"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#backoff","title":"Backoff","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"exponential backoff","text":"Waiting longer after each failed attempt before retrying, usually doubling the delay each time. Without jitter , clients that failed together retry together."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#barrier","title":"Barrier","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"barriers","text":"A GPU instruction that makes every thread in a threadgroup wait until all of them arrive, and makes their earlier memory writes visible to each other. There is no safe barrier across threadgroups."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#burst","title":"Burst","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"bursts","text":"A short spike of requests above the average rate. In a token bucket, the largest burst allowed is the bucket's capacity b ."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#cache-miss","title":"Cache miss","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"cache misses","text":"A lookup that finds nothing in the cache, so the request falls through to the slower source of truth and usually back-fills the cache."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#canonical-huffman-code","title":"Canonical Huffman code","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"canonical Huffman codes, canonical code, canonical codes","text":"A Huffman code rebuilt from code lengths alone: symbols are sorted by length, then by value, and each gets the previous code plus one, shifted left when the length grows. A DEFLATE block sends only the lengths. Step by step"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#compute-kernel","title":"Compute kernel","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"compute kernels, kernel, kernels, compute shader, compute shaders","text":"A function that runs on the GPU once per thread of a dispatch . Each thread reads its own index to decide which part of the work it does."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#crc-32","title":"CRC-32","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"CRC, CRCs, CRC32","text":"A 32-bit checksum computed by polynomial division over the bits of the data. ZIP and gzip store one per file so a decoder can detect corruption. Checksums of adjacent pieces can be combined, which allows computing it in parallel. Cyclic redundancy check (Wikipedia)"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#deflate","title":"DEFLATE","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"The compression format inside ZIP, gzip and PNG: LZ77 back-references plus Huffman coding , organised in blocks. Its bit stream has no index, so decoding is serial. Step by step · RFC 1951"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#dispatch","title":"Dispatch","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"dispatches","text":"One launch of a compute kernel over a grid of threads, divided into threadgroups . Dispatches in one command buffer run in order, which is the safe way to order work between threadgroups."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#divergence","title":"Divergence","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"divergent, diverge, diverges","text":"When lanes of one SIMD group take different sides of a branch. The hardware runs each side in turn with the other lanes switched off, so the branch costs the sum of both sides."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#end-of-block","title":"End-of-block","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"end of block, end-of-block symbol, end-of-block code","text":"Symbol 256 of DEFLATE 's literal/length alphabet. It closes every Huffman-coded block, so it needs a code of its own even though it appears once. Step by step"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#extra-bits","title":"Extra bits","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"extra bit, length extra bits, distance extra bits","text":"In DEFLATE , the plain bits that follow a length or distance symbol to pick an exact value, because each symbol stands for a range: symbol 270 means a length from 23 to 26, and the 2 extra bits after it say which. They are written as a plain number, not Huffman-coded, and the symbol alone decides how many there are. Step by step"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#gcra","title":"GCRA","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"generic cell rate algorithm","text":"The generic cell rate algorithm. It is equivalent to a token bucket, but stores one timestamp per key: the theoretical arrival time of the next conforming request. Generic cell rate algorithm (Wikipedia)"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#huffman-code","title":"Huffman code","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"Huffman codes, Huffman coding, Huffman table, Huffman tables","text":"A variable-length binary code that gives frequent symbols short bit patterns and rare ones long patterns. No code is a prefix of another, so a decoder can read them back to back without separators. Step by step · Huffman coding (Wikipedia)"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#jitter","title":"Jitter","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Randomness added to retry or polling delays, so many clients that start together don't keep acting in lock-step and overload a recovering service."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#lane","title":"Lane","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"lanes","text":"One thread's slot within a SIMD group . Each lane has its own registers, but all lanes execute the same instruction at the same time."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#lcov","title":"lcov","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"lcov report, lcov reports","text":"A plain-text coverage format that lists, per source file, how many times each line ran. Most coverage tools can write it, which makes it the common currency between them and tools like patchcov."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#leaky-bucket","title":"Leaky bucket","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"leaky buckets","text":"A queue that drains at a fixed rate. It smooths output to a constant pace instead of allowing bursts; compare with a token bucket ."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#lz77","title":"LZ77","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"The compression idea, from Lempel and Ziv in 1977, of replacing repeated data with back-references to an earlier occurrence. Step by step · LZ77 and LZ78 (Wikipedia)"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#marker","title":"Marker","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"markers","text":"In rapidgzip-style decoding, a 16-bit placeholder meaning \"the byte n positions before this chunk started\". It lets a decoder start mid-stream without the history, and is replaced by the real byte once the previous chunk is decoded."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#merge-base","title":"Merge base","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"merge bases, fork point, fork points","text":"The newest commit that a branch and its target (usually main ) share: where the branch forked. Comparing against it, rather than the tip of main , shows only what the branch itself changed."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#monotonic-clock","title":"Monotonic clock","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"monotonic clocks","text":"A clock that only moves forwards. Use it to measure elapsed time, because the wall clock can jump backwards when NTP corrects it."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#occupancy","title":"Occupancy","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"How many SIMD groups a GPU core keeps resident at once. More resident groups give the core more work to switch to while others wait for memory. Registers and threadgroup memory per group limit it."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#p99","title":"p99","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"99th percentile","text":"The 99th-percentile latency: 99% of requests complete at least this fast. Tail latency like this, rather than the average, is what users notice."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#patch-coverage","title":"Patch coverage","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"diff coverage","text":"The share of the executable lines a change added that the tests ran. Unlike the project-wide total, it shows the gaps in new code even when they are too small to move the total. Announcing patchcov lets you try it."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#rate-limiting","title":"Rate limiting","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"rate limit, rate limits, rate limiter, rate limiters","text":"Capping how many requests a client may make in a period of time, to protect a service and share its capacity fairly. Token bucket rate limiting explains the most common algorithm."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#read-replica","title":"Read replica","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"read replicas","text":"A copy of a database that serves reads and follows the primary asynchronously, so it can lag slightly behind."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#retry-after","title":"Retry-After","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"An HTTP response header that tells the client how long to wait before retrying, sent with 429 or 503 . RFC 9110 §10.2.3"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#retry-budget","title":"Retry budget","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"retry budgets","text":"A cap on retries as a fraction of normal traffic (for example 10%). It stops retries from multiplying load during an outage."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#shuffle","title":"Shuffle","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"shuffles, lane shuffle, lane shuffles","text":"A GPU instruction in which every lane reads a register value from another lane of its SIMD group, without going through memory. In Metal it is simd_shuffle ; in HLSL, WaveReadLaneAt ."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#simd-group","title":"SIMD group","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"SIMD groups, simdgroup, simdgroups, warp, warps, subgroup, subgroups, wavefront, wavefronts","text":"The set of threads a GPU executes in lock-step: 32 on Apple GPUs (NVIDIA calls it a warp, Vulkan a subgroup, HLSL a wave). One instruction is fetched once and executed by every lane ."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#sliding-window","title":"Sliding window","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"sliding windows","text":"In LZ77 , the most recent bytes already processed, which back-references may copy from. It moves forward with the input; DEFLATE 's is 32,768 bytes. Step by step"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#slo","title":"SLO","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"SLOs, service level objective, service level objectives","text":"A service level objective: a reliability target, such as 99.9% of requests succeeding over 30 days. It is set below 100% on purpose, leaving an error budget."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#spir-v","title":"SPIR-V","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"The Khronos binary intermediate language for GPU programs. Vulkan drivers consume it directly, and SPIRV-Cross can translate it into Metal Shading Language. SPIR-V registry"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#threadgroup","title":"Threadgroup","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"threadgroups, workgroup, workgroups, thread block, thread blocks","text":"A group of GPU threads that runs on one core and can share threadgroup memory and barriers . Vulkan calls it a workgroup and CUDA a thread block."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#threadgroup-memory","title":"Threadgroup memory","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"groupshared, groupshared memory, shared memory, workgroup memory","text":"Small, fast on-chip memory shared by the threads of one threadgroup : at most 32 KiB per threadgroup on Apple GPUs. HLSL calls it groupshared ."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#tls","title":"TLS","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"transport layer security","text":"Transport Layer Security: the protocol that encrypts and authenticates a connection, as in HTTPS. A gateway that \"terminates TLS\" decrypts traffic there."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#token-bucket","title":"Token bucket","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"token buckets","text":"A rate limiter that refills tokens at rate r up to a capacity b , and admits each request only by spending a token. It allows bursts up to b but enforces r on average. Token bucket rate limiting"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#unified-memory","title":"Unified memory","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"A design, used by Apple Silicon, in which the CPU and GPU share the same physical DRAM. A buffer can be visible to both without being copied, though first GPU use of a buffer still has a cost."}]}];
